const e=[{id:1,icon:"Snowflake",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Winter safety",subtitle:"Protecting yourself from the cold and the dangers of winter",summary:"Winter is a beautiful season, but it also has its dangers: cold, snow, ice, slipping and poisonous gas from heaters. We need to know how to protect ourselves and our families.",blocks:[{heading:"Heating your home safely",body:`Use gas, kerosene and electric heaters correctly and according to the manufacturer's instructions.
• Keep an opening for fresh air in the room, because gas and kerosene heaters produce the poisonous gas carbon monoxide (CO).
• Do not leave a heater switched on while you sleep.`},{heading:"Protection from the cold",body:"Wear warm clothes in layers. Cover your head, hands and feet well. When the weather is very cold, do not stay outside for a long time."},{heading:"Driving safely on snow and ice",body:"Drive slowly on snow and ice. Increase the distance between your car and the car in front. Use winter tires or snow chains."},{heading:"Preventing frozen pipes",body:"Water pipes can freeze and burst in the cold. Wrap and insulate outdoor pipes. On very cold nights, let a little water run slowly from the faucet."}],tips:["Install a CO (carbon monoxide) alarm at home.","Turn off the gas heater before you go to sleep.","Wear clothes in layers: they are better than one thick garment.","Keep a first aid kit and a blanket in the car.","Make sure your phone is fully charged when you go out."],warnings:["Do not place a heater near curtains or clothes.","Keep children away from heaters.","Do not let heavy snow build up on the roof of the house.","Do not overload one electrical outlet with several electric heaters."],activity:{prompt:"You are at home and the gas heater is on. A family member has a headache and feels dizzy. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I get everyone out into fresh air at once (I turn off the heater on the way only if it is quick and safe), call 122 even if they start to feel better, and nobody goes back in until the room is aired and the heater is checked",correct:!0,feedback:"Headache and dizziness near a gas heater can mean CO poisoning, so fresh air and a call to 122 come first."},{text:"I give them a headache tablet and leave the heater on",correct:!1,feedback:"A tablet does not help if the heater is giving off CO; get everyone out into fresh air and call 122."},{text:"I only turn off the heater and we stay in the room",correct:!1,feedback:"Turning off the heater is not enough: the poisonous gas stays in the room, so everyone must get out into fresh air."},{text:"I do nothing and wait",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting is dangerous: the gas keeps building up, so get everyone out into fresh air and call 122."}]},quiz:[{question:"Why should there be an opening for fresh air in the room when a gas heater is on?",options:[{text:"So that the room smells fresh and pleasant",correct:!1},{text:"To stop poisonous carbon monoxide (CO) from building up",correct:!0},{text:"So that the room does not overheat while the heater is on",correct:!1},{text:"So that the heater makes less noise",correct:!1}],explanation:"Gas and kerosene heaters produce carbon monoxide (CO), a poisonous gas. An opening for fresh air keeps it from building up in the room."},{question:"Which clothes are best for protection from the cold?",options:[{text:"One thick garment",correct:!1},{text:"Clothes in layers",correct:!0},{text:"Thin clothes",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"Several layers of warm clothes protect better than one thick garment. Also cover your head, hands and feet well."},{question:"How do we prevent water pipes from freezing?",options:[{text:"We wrap the pipes and let a little water run on very cold nights",correct:!0},{text:"We leave them as they are, because metal pipes do not freeze",correct:!1},{text:"We light a fire under the pipes on very cold nights",correct:!1},{text:"We leave the outside pipes uncovered so the sun can warm them by day",correct:!1}],explanation:"Water pipes can freeze and burst in the cold. Wrap and insulate outdoor pipes, and on very cold nights let a little water run slowly from the faucet."},{question:"You come home and the CO alarm is sounding while the gas heater is on. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Get everyone out into fresh air and call 115 from outside",correct:!0},{text:"Take out the alarm's battery, because it is probably faulty",correct:!1},{text:"Turn the heater off and stay in the room to see if it stops",correct:!1},{text:"Open one window and wait inside until the alarm goes quiet",correct:!1}],explanation:"A sounding CO alarm means the poisonous gas may be building up. Everyone goes out into fresh air and you call from outside; call 122 as well if anyone feels unwell."},{question:"Which of these winter habits is dangerous?",options:[{text:"Wearing several thin layers instead of one thick garment",correct:!1},{text:"Leaving the heater on overnight in a closed bedroom",correct:!0},{text:"Keeping a blanket and a first aid kit in the car",correct:!1},{text:"Wrapping outdoor water pipes before a very cold night",correct:!1}],explanation:"A heater left on while people sleep in a closed room lets poisonous CO build up. The other habits are what the lesson recommends."},{question:"You are driving on an icy road and the car in front is close. What is the safe choice?",options:[{text:"Slow down and increase the distance to the car in front",correct:!0},{text:"Keep your speed and be ready to brake hard if needed",correct:!1},{text:"Overtake it quickly so that the road ahead is clear",correct:!1},{text:"Stay close behind it and drive in its wheel tracks",correct:!1}],explanation:"On snow and ice a car needs more room to stop, so drive slowly and leave more distance to the car in front."}],steps:[{title:"Use the heater safely",detail:"Use gas, kerosene and electric heaters according to the manufacturer's instructions. Keep them away from curtains, clothes and children, and do not overload one outlet with several electric heaters."},{title:"Keep fresh air coming in",detail:"Gas and kerosene heaters produce the poisonous gas carbon monoxide (CO). Keep an opening for fresh air in the room and install a CO alarm at home.",critical:!0},{title:"Turn the heater off before sleeping",detail:"Never leave a heater on while the family sleeps. Use warm clothes in layers and extra blankets instead.",critical:!0},{title:"Headache or dizziness near a heater: get out",detail:"These can be signs of CO poisoning. Take everyone out into fresh air, call 122 even if they feel better, and let no one go back in until the room has been aired and the heater checked. If the CO alarm sounds, everyone goes out and you call 115 from outside.",critical:!0},{title:"Protect yourself from the cold outside and on the road",detail:"Wear clothes in layers and cover your head, hands and feet. On snow and ice, drive slowly and increase the distance to the car in front. Keep a first aid kit and a blanket in the car and a charged phone with you."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"A very cold night. Your family wants to sleep in one room with the kerosene heater on and the window shut to keep the warmth in. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I turn the heater off before we sleep and we use warm layers and extra blankets instead",correct:!0,feedback:"The lesson's rule is never to leave a heater on while sleeping: in a closed room the poisonous CO builds up."},{text:"I leave the heater on low, because a small flame is safe all night",correct:!1,feedback:"A low flame still produces CO and the window is shut; turn the heater off before sleeping."},{text:"I keep the heater on and move it next to the beds so everyone stays warm",correct:!1,feedback:"Near beds and blankets the heater can also start a fire, and it makes CO all night; turn it off before sleeping."},{text:"I open the door a little and leave the heater on, so there is some air",correct:!1,feedback:"Leaving a heater on while sleeping is the danger itself; a gap in the door does not make it safe."}]},{prompt:"In the morning someone lights the heater again with the room still closed. After a while your grandmother has a headache and feels dizzy. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I take everyone out into fresh air, call 122 and let no one back in until the room is aired and the heater checked",correct:!0,feedback:"Headache and dizziness near a heater may be CO poisoning: fresh air and 122 come first, even if she feels better outside."},{text:"I give her a headache tablet and let her rest in the warm room",correct:!1,feedback:"A tablet does not remove CO, and staying in the room keeps her breathing it; take everyone out and call 122."},{text:"I open the window and we all stay inside to keep an eye on her",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening a window is not enough while people stay in the room; everyone goes out into fresh air and you call 122."},{text:"I turn the heater up, because she may be dizzy from the cold",correct:!1,feedback:"More heat in a closed room means more CO; her signs point to the heater, so get everyone out and call 122."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: On a cold winter night a family closed every window and left the gas heater on in the room where the children slept. They had fitted a CO alarm a few weeks earlier. Late at night the alarm sounded. The father did not switch it off or look for a fault: he woke everyone, took them outside into fresh air and called 115 from outside, and 122 because one child had a headache. The team aired the house and the heater was checked before anyone went back in. Since then the family turns the heater off before sleeping and tests the alarm."},{id:2,icon:"Sun",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Summer safety",subtitle:"Protecting yourself from heat and wildfires",summary:"Summer is a hot season with its own dangers, such as heatstroke, crop fires, wildfires, drowning and water shortages. Let us learn how to protect ourselves.",blocks:[{heading:"Protection from heatstroke",body:"Drink water throughout the day, before you feel thirsty. Stay out of the sun during the hottest hours of the day. Wear light, light-colored clothes and a hat."},{heading:"Heat exhaustion or heatstroke?",body:`• Heat exhaustion: heavy sweating, cold, pale and clammy skin, weakness, dizziness, headache, nausea and thirst. Move the person to a cool place, loosen their clothing, cool them with water and give sips of water if they are fully alert. Get medical help if they vomit, if the symptoms get worse or last more than an hour, or if they become confused (that is heatstroke).
• Heatstroke: a very hot body with confusion, strange behavior, slurred speech, a seizure or collapse; the skin may be hot and dry or still sweating. This is an emergency: call 122, move the person to a cool place, remove extra clothing and cool them at once, before they are taken to the hospital. The best way is to put the body up to the neck in cold water for 15 minutes or until they are thinking clearly again, whichever comes first; if that is not possible, use a cold shower, cold wet towels or sheets and ice packs, and fan them. Give nothing to drink to a person who is confused.`},{heading:"Preventing wildfires",body:"Do not light fires in forests or among dry grass. Do not throw away cigarettes. Do not leave glass bottles outside, because they can act like a lens, focus the sun's rays and start a fire."},{heading:"Safety at lakes and rivers",body:"Never swim alone. Swim only in safe places where there is supervision. Wear a life jacket. Never take your eyes off children."},{heading:"Keeping food and water safe",body:"In summer, food spoils quickly. Keep food in the refrigerator. Drink clean water. Wash fruit and vegetables well."}],tips:["Drink water throughout the day: usually 2 liters or more.","Stay out of the sun at midday and in the afternoon, when the heat is at its highest.","Use sunscreen (SPF 30+).","Eat fruit with a high water content (such as watermelon and melon).","Never leave children or animals alone in a hot car."],warnings:["Heatstroke can kill: drink plenty of water and stay in the shade.","Fire spreads quickly in forests.","Drowning: never leave children near water without supervision.","Spoiled food causes food poisoning."],activity:{prompt:"You are outside and feel dizzy and weak. Your body has become very hot. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I move into the shade, drink water, cool my body with cool water and call 122",correct:!0,feedback:"Dizziness, weakness and a very hot body are warning signs of heat danger: get into the shade, cool down and call 122."},{text:"I carry on with my work",correct:!1,feedback:"Carrying on with work keeps heating your body; stop, move to a cool place, cool down and call 122."},{text:"I stay in the sun and only drink iced water",correct:!1,feedback:"Staying in the sun keeps heating your body; move into the shade and cool your body with water."},{text:"I lie down in the sun and go to sleep",correct:!1,feedback:"Lying in the sun makes your body even hotter; move to a cool place, cool down and call 122."}]},quiz:[{question:"Why should we not leave glass water bottles outside in the sun?",options:[{text:"Because the water gets hot and is no longer good to drink",correct:!1},{text:"Because they can act like a lens and start a fire",correct:!0},{text:"Because the glass breaks easily in strong heat",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference, as long as the lid is closed",correct:!1}],explanation:"A glass bottle can act like a lens: it focuses the sun's rays and can start a fire. Do not leave glass bottles outside."},{question:"In summer, how much water should we drink?",options:[{text:"Half a liter, all at once in the morning",correct:!1},{text:"1 liter is enough",correct:!1},{text:"2 liters or more, throughout the day",correct:!0},{text:"Only when we feel thirsty, even on hot days",correct:!1}],explanation:"Drink water throughout the day, usually 2 liters or more, and before you feel thirsty. This helps protect you from heatstroke."},{question:"In summer, at what time of day are the heat and the danger greatest?",options:[{text:"Early morning and sunrise",correct:!1},{text:"Midday and afternoon",correct:!0},{text:"Evening and sunset",correct:!1},{text:"Late at night",correct:!1}],explanation:"The heat is at its highest at midday and in the afternoon, so stay out of the sun at those hours."},{question:"A person in the heat is sweating and feels weak. Which new sign means it has become heatstroke?",options:[{text:"They become confused and behave strangely",correct:!0},{text:"They feel thirsty and ask for some water",correct:!1},{text:"Their face and clothes are wet with sweat",correct:!1},{text:"They say their legs feel tired and heavy",correct:!1}],explanation:"Confusion, strange behavior, slurred speech, a seizure or collapse in the heat mean heatstroke: call 122 and cool the person at once."},{question:"A confused person who collapsed in the heat asks for a drink. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Give nothing to drink, cool them at once and call 122",correct:!0},{text:"Give them as much cold water as they can drink quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Give them a little salt water to replace their sweat",correct:!1},{text:"Give them hot tea so that their body sweats more",correct:!1}],explanation:"A confused person is given nothing to drink. Call 122 and cool them at once, best in cold water up to the neck, otherwise with a cold shower, wet towels, ice packs and fanning."},{question:"On a hot day your child falls asleep in the car while you stop at a shop for a few minutes. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Take the child with you into the shop",correct:!0},{text:"Leave the child asleep with the windows slightly open",correct:!1},{text:"Leave the engine running so the air conditioning stays on",correct:!1},{text:"Leave the child, because a few minutes cannot do any harm",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never leave children or animals alone in a hot car, not even for a short time: take the child with you."}],steps:[{title:"Drink water and keep out of the midday sun",detail:"Drink water throughout the day, before you feel thirsty. Stay out of the sun at midday and in the afternoon, and wear light, light-colored clothes and a hat."},{title:"Heat exhaustion: cool the person down",detail:"Heavy sweating, pale cool skin, weakness, dizziness, headache or nausea: move the person to a cool place, loosen their clothing, cool them with water and give sips of water if they are fully alert. Get medical help if they vomit, the signs get worse or last more than an hour, or they become confused.",critical:!0},{title:"Heatstroke: call 122 and cool at once",detail:"Confusion, strange behavior, slurred speech, a seizure or collapse in the heat is heatstroke, even if the skin is still sweating. Call 122, move the person to a cool place and cool them at once: the body up to the neck in cold water for 15 minutes or until they think clearly, whichever comes first; otherwise a cold shower, cold wet towels, ice packs and fanning. Give nothing to drink to a confused person.",critical:!0},{title:"Never leave anyone alone in a hot car",detail:"Never leave children or animals alone in a parked car in the heat, not even for a short time.",critical:!0},{title:"Prevent wildfires and keep food safe",detail:"Do not light fires among dry grass, do not throw away cigarettes and do not leave glass bottles outside. Keep food in the refrigerator and drink clean water."},{title:"Near water, never take your eyes off children",detail:"Swim only in safe, supervised places, never alone, and wear a life jacket at lakes and rivers.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"A family picnic on a hot afternoon. Your uncle has been grilling in the sun for a long time. He is sweating heavily, pale and dizzy, and has a headache, but he answers you clearly. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I move him into the shade, loosen his clothes, cool him with water and give him sips of water while I watch him",correct:!0,feedback:"These are signs of heat exhaustion and he is fully alert: cooling and sips of water are right, and you keep watching for confusion."},{text:"I tell him to finish grilling and drink something when the food is ready",correct:!1,feedback:"Staying in the sun makes heat exhaustion worse and it can turn into heatstroke; move him to a cool place now."},{text:"I let him lie down in the sun on a blanket until the dizziness passes",correct:!1,feedback:"Resting in the sun keeps heating his body; move him into the shade and cool him with water."},{text:"I tell him it is normal on a hot day and leave him on his own",correct:!1,feedback:"Heat exhaustion needs action and someone watching, because it can become heatstroke; cool him and stay with him."}]},{prompt:"You have been cooling him in the shade. Now he is confused, says strange things and cannot stand up. His skin is still wet with sweat. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I call 122, cool him at once with cold water, wet towels, ice packs and fanning, and give him nothing to drink",correct:!0,feedback:"Confusion in the heat means heatstroke, even with sweating skin: call 122 and cool him at once; a confused person is given nothing to drink."},{text:"I keep giving him water to drink, because he needs fluids",correct:!1,feedback:"The lesson's rule: give nothing to drink to a confused person; call 122 and cool him at once."},{text:"I wait, because sweating skin shows it cannot be heatstroke",correct:!1,feedback:"In heatstroke the skin may still sweat; confusion is the warning sign, so call 122 and cool him now."},{text:"I put him in the car and drive to the hospital without cooling him first",correct:!1,feedback:"Cooling must start at once, before he is taken to the hospital; call 122 and cool him while you wait."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: On a very hot day a young man was helping his neighbors move furniture in the afternoon sun. He felt dizzy and had stopped drinking, but he kept working. Suddenly he began to talk strangely and sat down, confused. A neighbor recognized heatstroke. She called 122, moved him into the shade with the help of others, removed his extra clothing, poured cold water over him and fanned him, and gave him nothing to drink. By the time the ambulance arrived, he was beginning to think more clearly."},{id:3,icon:"Home",accent:"#059669",title:"Home safety",subtitle:"Protecting the family from dangers inside the home",summary:"The home should be a safe place for the family, but there are many dangers in the home, such as fire, electricity, gas, falls and poisoning. Let us learn how to make a home safe.",blocks:[{heading:"Fire safety and fire extinguishers",body:`Install a smoke alarm and test it every month. Keep a fire extinguisher near the kitchen. Types of fire extinguisher (NFPA classes):
• Water: for wood, paper and fabric (Class A).
• CO₂: for electrical equipment (Class C).
• ABC powder: for Classes A, B and C, but not for cooking oil and not for metals.
• Foam: for flammable liquids (Class B).
• Never pour water on burning oil!

Use an extinguisher only if all of this is true: the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher is the right type, your exit is behind you, the alarm has been raised and 115 called, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out. If the fire does not go out at once, get out and close the door. The PASS method: Pull the safety pin, Aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, Squeeze the handle firmly and Sweep the nozzle from side to side. Plan an emergency escape route from your home.`},{heading:"Electrical safety and generators",body:`Keep outlets and electrical cables in good condition. Repair or replace cut or damaged cables. Do not touch electrical appliances with wet hands. Do not connect many appliances to one power strip.

Generators: run a generator only outdoors, at least 6 meters (20 feet) from windows, doors and vents — never inside a house, garage, basement, shop or storeroom, even with the doors and windows open. Fit a battery-powered CO alarm in the home. Never plug a generator into a wall outlet or connect it to the house wiring: it can electrocute neighbors and electricity workers. Only a qualified electrician may connect it, through a transfer switch.`},{heading:"Gas safety",body:"Check gas pipes and cylinders regularly. If you smell gas: do not switch any electrical switch on or off, do not light any flame, do not use a phone inside and do not start a generator or a car nearby. Open the windows and doors on your way out if you can. Close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it safely, without going into the area where the gas has collected; if not, do not go near it. Leave the house, warn others and call 115 from outside."},{heading:"Preventing falls",body:"Fit handrails on stairs. Do not leave things on the floor or on the stairs. Make sure stairs are well lit. Clean up spilled water from the floor immediately."}],tips:["Test the smoke alarm every month.","Do not connect many appliances to power strips.","Keep medicines and chemicals in a locked place, away from children.","Always keep the emergency exit route clear.","Write the emergency numbers (115, 122, 104) in a visible place."],warnings:["Do not leave children alone in the kitchen.","Keep glass and sharp objects in a high place, away from children.","Make windows and balconies safe for children.","Playing with fire or electricity can kill."],activity:{prompt:"There is a smell of gas in the kitchen. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I open the windows on my way out, close the gas valve only if I can reach it safely, leave the house and call 115 from outside",correct:!0,feedback:"No switch or flame, windows opened on the way out, the valve closed only if safe, and 115 called from outside."},{text:"I switch on the light to find where the gas is leaking",correct:!1,feedback:"Switching on a light can ignite the gas; touch no switch and get out of the house."},{text:"I switch on my phone's flashlight inside the house",correct:!1,feedback:"Using a phone inside the house can ignite the gas; get out first and call 115 from outside."},{text:"I wait until the smell of gas goes away",correct:!1,feedback:"The gas keeps building up while you wait; get out, warn others and call 115 from outside."}]},quiz:[{question:"How often should we test the smoke alarm?",options:[{text:"Once every 5 years",correct:!1},{text:"Every month",correct:!0},{text:"Never",correct:!1},{text:"Only when we buy it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Test the smoke alarm every month, so you can be sure it will warn you if a fire starts."},{question:"When there is a smell of gas in the house, what do we do?",options:[{text:"We switch on the lights and the kitchen fan so that we can quickly find where the gas is leaking",correct:!1},{text:"We open windows on the way out, shut the gas valve only if safe to reach, and leave",correct:!0},{text:"We light a match to find where the gas is coming from",correct:!1},{text:"We close the windows and wait inside the house until the smell of gas goes away",correct:!1}],explanation:"Any switch or flame can ignite the gas. Open windows on your way out, close the valve only if it is close and safe to reach, and call 115 from outside."},{question:"Why should we not touch electricity with wet hands?",options:[{text:"Because wet hands leave dirt and water marks on the switches",correct:!1},{text:"Because water conducts electricity and causes an electric shock",correct:!0},{text:"Because the water makes the electricity in the whole house cut off",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference if the switch is made of plastic",correct:!1}],explanation:"Water conducts electricity, so touching electrical appliances with wet hands can give you an electric shock."},{question:"A wastebasket fire at home is growing and smoke is gathering under the ceiling. There is an ABC extinguisher nearby, but you have never been trained. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Get everyone out, close the door behind you and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Use the extinguisher anyway, because the fire is still small",correct:!1},{text:"Throw a bucket of water on it, then call for help afterwards",correct:!1},{text:"Carry the burning basket outside before it spreads further",correct:!1}],explanation:"The go/no-go check fails: the fire is growing, smoke is gathering and you are not trained. Get out, close the door and call 115."},{question:"Your family needs a generator during a power cut. Which use is correct?",options:[{text:"Outdoors only, away from windows, doors and vents, never connected to a wall outlet",correct:!0},{text:"In the garage with the big door wide open so that the fumes can escape outside",correct:!1},{text:"In the basement with a window open and a fan blowing the air out",correct:!1},{text:"On the balcony, connected to a wall outlet to power the whole house",correct:!1}],explanation:"A generator runs only outdoors, away from windows, doors and vents, never inside even with the doors open, and it is never plugged into a wall outlet, because it can electrocute neighbors and electricity workers."},{question:"Which of these is unsafe at home?",options:[{text:"Plugging several appliances into one power strip",correct:!0},{text:"Fitting handrails on the stairs and lighting them well",correct:!1},{text:"Keeping medicines and chemicals in a locked cupboard",correct:!1},{text:"Writing 115, 122 and 104 where everyone can see them",correct:!1}],explanation:"Too many appliances on one power strip can overload it and start a fire; the other habits are what the lesson recommends."}],steps:[{title:"Prepare: a smoke alarm and an escape plan",detail:"Install a smoke alarm and test it every month. Plan an emergency escape route from your home, keep it clear and write 115, 122 and 104 in a visible place."},{title:"A smell of gas: no sparks, get out",detail:"Do not switch anything on or off, light no flame and do not use a phone inside. Open windows and doors on your way out if you can. Close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it without going into the area where the gas has collected; otherwise do not go near it. Leave, warn others and call 115 from outside.",critical:!0},{title:"A small fire: check before you fight it",detail:"Use an extinguisher only if the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher is the right type, your exit is behind you, the alarm is raised and 115 called, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out. Then use PASS. If the fire does not go out at once, get out and close the door. Never pour water on burning oil.",critical:!0},{title:"Electricity and generators",detail:"Do not touch electrical appliances with wet hands, repair damaged cables and do not plug many appliances into one power strip. Run a generator only outdoors, away from windows, doors and vents, and never plug it into a wall outlet or the house wiring.",critical:!0},{title:"Prevent falls and protect children",detail:"Fit handrails, light the stairs well and keep floors and stairs clear. Keep medicines and chemicals locked away, sharp objects up high and windows and balconies safe for children."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"You come home in the evening and smell gas strongly as you open the front door. The light switch is beside the door. The gas cylinder and its valve are next to the stove at the far end of the kitchen, where the smell is strongest. Your family is inside. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I do not touch the switch or go to the valve; I keep the door open, call everyone out and phone 115 from outside",correct:!0,feedback:"The valve is where the gas has collected, so you do not go near it; no switches, get everyone out and call from outside."},{text:"I switch on the light so I can see where the gas is coming from",correct:!1,feedback:"Operating a switch can make a spark and ignite the gas; leave the light alone and get everyone out."},{text:"I hold my breath, run to the stove and close the cylinder valve",correct:!1,feedback:"The valve is where the gas has collected and going there puts you in danger. Close it only if it is close and safe to reach; here, get out."},{text:"I use my phone's flashlight in the kitchen to look for the leak",correct:!1,feedback:"A phone used inside can make a spark; never use it where the gas is. Get out and call 115 from outside."}]},{prompt:"Everyone is outside and you have called 115. A neighbor says he will quickly run in to close the valve, and your son wants to fetch his phone from his room. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I keep everyone outside and away from the house, and wait to tell the 115 team where the cylinder is",correct:!0,feedback:"Nobody goes back in for a valve or for belongings; the team decides when it is safe, and your information helps them."},{text:"I let the neighbor go in, because he is quick and knows about gas",correct:!1,feedback:"Never go back in for a valve, even quickly; keep everyone outside until the 115 team says it is safe."},{text:"I let my son run in, because his room is far from the kitchen",correct:!1,feedback:"Gas can spread through the house and a light or a phone can ignite it; nobody goes back in."},{text:"I start the car parked by the kitchen window to move it away",correct:!1,feedback:"Starting an engine near the gas can ignite it; keep away from the house and wait for 115."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: A family had fitted a smoke alarm in the hallway and tested it every month. One night a pan was forgotten on the stove and a towel next to it caught fire. The alarm woke the mother. Smoke was already filling the kitchen, so she did not try to fight the fire. She woke everyone, they left by the escape route they had practiced, she closed the kitchen door behind her and called 115 from outside. The team put the fire out in the kitchen. Afterwards the family said the alarm and the plan had given them time to get out."},{id:4,icon:"HeartPulse",accent:"#dc2626",title:"First aid basics",subtitle:"Basic actions to save a life until the ambulance arrives",summary:"First aid can save a life. If someone is injured, their heart has stopped or they cannot breathe, you can help them until the ambulance arrives. The first thing to do: call 122.",blocks:[{heading:"Chest compressions (CPR) for adults",body:`When a person's heart stops (they become unconscious and are not breathing, or not breathing normally):
1. Make sure the area is safe for you.
2. Tap their shoulders and ask in a loud voice: "Are you all right?"
3. If there is no response, call 122 and send someone to bring an automated external defibrillator (AED).
4. Give 30 compressions in the center of the chest: depth 5–6 cm, rate 100–120 compressions per minute.
5. If you are trained, give 2 rescue breaths (tilt the head back, lift the chin and pinch the nose). If you are not trained, or not willing to give breaths, give chest compressions only (Hands-Only CPR), without stopping.
6. Repeat 30:2 (or compressions only) until the ambulance team arrives.

• AED: if one is available, switch it on immediately and follow the device's voice instructions.`},{heading:"Bleeding: how to stop it",body:`• Direct pressure: place a clean cloth on the wound and press firmly. Do not lift your hand until the bleeding stops.
• If the cloth becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it; place another cloth on top and keep pressing.
• Tourniquet: for very severe bleeding from an arm or leg, apply it 5–7 cm above the wound (not over the knee or elbow) and tighten it until the bleeding stops. Write down the time it was applied and never loosen it.
• If an object is stuck in the wound, do not pull it out! Apply pressure around it and call 122.`},{heading:"Burns: first aid",body:`• Minor burn: immediately cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes (not ice and not very cold water).
• Do not burst blisters, and do not put butter, oil or toothpaste on the burn.
• After cooling, cover it loosely with a clean cloth or plastic wrap.
• Severe or large burn: call 122. If clothing is stuck to the skin, do not pull it off. Keep the person warm.`},{heading:"Choking",body:`Signs: the person cannot speak, cough or breathe, and holds their throat with their hands.
• Adults and children (one year and older): give 5 blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades. Then give 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich): stand behind the person, place your fist slightly above the navel and well below the breastbone, and press quickly inward and upward. Repeat 5+5.
• Infants (under one year): lay the infant along your forearm, face down with the head lower than the chest, and give 5 back blows. Then turn the infant onto their back and give 5 chest thrusts in the center of the chest with the heel of one hand. Never give abdominal thrusts to an infant.
• If the person becomes unconscious, call 122 and start CPR.`}],tips:["Always call 122 as the first step.","Use gloves if they are available.","Keep a first aid kit at home and in the car.","Take a first aid course.","Time matters: start helping quickly."],warnings:["Before anything else, check your own safety.","If someone has fallen from a height, do not move them (their neck or back may be injured), unless the place is unsafe or they need CPR.","Do not put anything in the mouth of an unconscious person.","Do not touch a wound with dirty hands."],activity:{prompt:"Someone at home is choking on something and cannot speak or breathe. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I give 5 blows to their back, then 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich) and repeat",correct:!0,feedback:"Unable to speak or breathe means full choking; 5 back blows, then 5 abdominal thrusts, repeated, can push the object out."},{text:"I give them water to drink",correct:!1,feedback:"Drinking does not treat choking: give 5 back blows, then 5 abdominal thrusts, and repeat."},{text:"I push my fingers deep into their mouth",correct:!1,feedback:"This is not what the lesson teaches: give 5 back blows, then 5 abdominal thrusts, and repeat."},{text:"I wait until they feel better",correct:!1,feedback:"A person who cannot breathe cannot wait: start back blows and abdominal thrusts at once."}]},quiz:[{question:"In adult CPR, what is the ratio of compressions to breaths?",options:[{text:"10 compressions and 1 breath",correct:!1},{text:"30 compressions and 2 breaths",correct:!0},{text:"15 compressions and 2 breaths",correct:!1},{text:"50 compressions and 5 breaths",correct:!1}],explanation:"Adult CPR is 30 chest compressions, then 2 breaths if you are trained. If you are not trained, give compressions only (Hands-Only CPR), without stopping."},{question:"What do we do for a minor burn?",options:[{text:"We hold ice on it for 20 minutes",correct:!1},{text:"We spread butter or toothpaste on it",correct:!1},{text:"Cool running water for 20 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"We put kerosene on it to draw out the heat",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cool a minor burn under cool running water for 20 minutes. Do not put ice, butter, oil or any other substance on it."},{question:"What is the ambulance number?",options:[{text:"115",correct:!1},{text:"122",correct:!0},{text:"104",correct:!1},{text:"911",correct:!1}],explanation:"In the Kurdistan Region, 122 is the ambulance number; 115 is Civil Defense and 104 is the police."},{question:"An adult collapses at home, does not respond and is not breathing normally. You have never learned rescue breaths. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Call 122, send someone for an AED and start chest compressions only",correct:!0},{text:"Put the person in the recovery position and wait for the ambulance",correct:!1},{text:"Splash water on their face and wait to see if they wake up",correct:!1},{text:"Give two breaths anyway, then check the pulse for a minute",correct:!1}],explanation:"Not breathing normally means CPR is needed. If you have not learned breaths, give chest compressions only (Hands-Only CPR), 100–120 per minute, without stopping until help arrives."},{question:"An infant under one year is choking and cannot cough or breathe. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Give 5 back blows with the head lower than the chest, then 5 chest thrusts",correct:!0},{text:"Give 5 abdominal thrusts, the same as for an older child or an adult",correct:!1},{text:"Hold the infant upside down by the feet and shake them hard",correct:!1},{text:"Give the infant some water to wash the object down",correct:!1}],explanation:"For infants: 5 back blows with the head lower than the chest, then 5 chest thrusts in the center of the chest with the heel of one hand. Never give abdominal thrusts to an infant."},{question:"You have cooled a minor burn under running water for 20 minutes. What next?",options:[{text:"Cover it loosely with a clean cloth or plastic wrap",correct:!0},{text:"Burst the blisters so that the skin can dry out faster",correct:!1},{text:"Spread butter or toothpaste on it to soothe the pain",correct:!1},{text:"Wrap it tightly with tape so that no air can reach it",correct:!1}],explanation:"After cooling, cover the burn loosely with a clean cloth or plastic wrap. Do not burst blisters and do not put butter, oil or toothpaste on it."}],steps:[{title:"Check safety, then the person",detail:'Make sure the area is safe for you. Tap their shoulders and ask loudly: "Are you all right?"',critical:!0},{title:"No response: call 122 and send for an AED",detail:"If they do not respond and are not breathing, or not breathing normally, call 122 and send someone to bring an automated external defibrillator (AED).",critical:!0},{title:"Push hard and fast in the center of the chest",detail:"Give 30 compressions, 5–6 cm deep, at 100–120 per minute. If you are trained, give 2 breaths and repeat 30:2; if not, give compressions only without stopping. Switch on the AED as soon as it arrives and follow its voice instructions.",critical:!0},{title:"Severe bleeding: press and keep pressing",detail:"Press firmly on the wound with a clean cloth; if it soaks through, do not remove it, add another on top and keep pressing. For very severe bleeding from an arm or leg, apply a tourniquet 5–7 cm above the wound, write down the time and never loosen it. Do not pull out an object stuck in a wound.",critical:!0},{title:"Burns: cool for 20 minutes",detail:"Cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes, with no ice, butter, oil or toothpaste and without bursting blisters. Then cover it loosely with a clean cloth or plastic wrap. Call 122 for a severe or large burn."},{title:"Choking: back blows and abdominal thrusts",detail:"If the person cannot speak, cough or breathe, give 5 back blows and 5 abdominal thrusts and repeat. For an infant under one year, give 5 back blows and 5 chest thrusts, never abdominal thrusts. If the person becomes unconscious, call 122 and start CPR.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"Your father is cutting meat in the kitchen when the knife slips and cuts deep into his forearm. Blood is flowing fast. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I press firmly on the wound with a clean cloth and get someone to call 122",correct:!0,feedback:"Direct pressure is the first step for bleeding, and severe bleeding needs the ambulance; do not lift your hand to look."},{text:"I wash the wound under the tap first and then look for a bandage",correct:!1,feedback:"Washing and searching waste time while he loses blood; press on the wound now and call 122."},{text:"I give him a glass of water and tell him to sit down and rest",correct:!1,feedback:"Water does not stop bleeding; press firmly on the wound with a clean cloth and call 122."},{text:"I lift the cloth every minute to see if the bleeding has stopped",correct:!1,feedback:"Lifting the cloth lets the bleeding start again; keep your hand pressing and do not look."}]},{prompt:"You are pressing hard, but the cloth is soaked and blood keeps flowing heavily from the arm. The first aid kit has a tourniquet. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I apply the tourniquet 5–7 cm above the wound and tighten it until the bleeding stops, noting the time",correct:!0,feedback:"For very severe bleeding from an arm, a tourniquet above the wound (not over the elbow) stops it; note the time and never loosen it."},{text:"I take off the soaked cloth and start pressing again with a clean one",correct:!1,feedback:"A soaked cloth is not removed: add another on top. Here the bleeding is very severe, so the tourniquet is needed."},{text:"I put on the tourniquet and loosen it every few minutes so the arm gets blood",correct:!1,feedback:"A tourniquet is never loosened once it is on; write down the time it was applied and leave it for the ambulance team."},{text:"I tie the tourniquet tightly right over the elbow, because it holds better there",correct:!1,feedback:"A tourniquet goes 5–7 cm above the wound, not over the knee or the elbow."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: At a family meal a grandfather suddenly could not speak, cough or breathe and held his throat with both hands. His granddaughter, who had taken a first aid course, recognized choking. She stood behind him and gave 5 back blows with the heel of her hand between his shoulder blades, then 5 abdominal thrusts with her fist slightly above the navel and well below the breastbone. In the second round the piece of food came out and he began to breathe and cough. That same week two more family members signed up for a first aid course."},{id:5,icon:"Backpack",accent:"#9333ea",title:"The emergency kit",subtitle:"Preparing a bag for emergencies",summary:"An emergency kit is a bag that holds the things you need in an emergency, such as an earthquake, a flood or any other incident. Every household should have one.",blocks:[{heading:"Water and food",body:`• Water: about 4 liters per person per day, for at least 3 days (for drinking and hygiene).
• Canned food or dry food that does not need cooking.
• Crackers, dates and candy.`},{heading:"Medical supplies",body:"Bandages, clean wound dressings, medical tape, gloves, scissors, tweezers and any medicines that family members need (if any)."},{heading:"Important equipment",body:"A flashlight and batteries, a small radio, a power bank to charge your phone, a change of clothes, a blanket and a whistle to call for help."},{heading:"Documents and money",body:"Copies of identity cards and passports, some cash, a list of important phone numbers and spare keys."}],tips:["Choose a light bag that is easy to carry.","Check the contents of the kit once every 6 months and replace any water or food that has expired.","Keep the kit in an easy-to-reach place near the door.","Include items for the particular needs of each family member (children, the elderly, the sick).","Do not forget your pets."],warnings:["Make sure there is enough water and food for every family member.","Do not forget your medicines.","Include a phone and a charger.","Cash is important, because bank cards may not work."],activity:{prompt:"After an earthquake, you must evacuate your home quickly. The emergency kit is ready at home. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I take the kit and go to the assembly point",correct:!0,feedback:"Taking the ready kit to the assembly point gives you water, food and medicines without losing time."},{text:"I spend time looking around to collect other things",correct:!1,feedback:"Searching for other things wastes time in a home that may be damaged; take the ready kit and leave."},{text:"I leave the kit behind and just run",correct:!1,feedback:"Leaving the kit means losing the water, food and medicines you prepared; it is ready, so take it with you."},{text:"I stay at home and wait",correct:!1,feedback:"You must evacuate: a home that may be damaged is dangerous, so take the kit and go to the assembly point."}]},quiz:[{question:"How much water should the emergency kit hold for each person?",options:[{text:"About 1 liter per day, for at least 3 days",correct:!1},{text:"About 4 liters per day, for at least 3 days",correct:!0},{text:"Half a liter per day, for one day only",correct:!1},{text:"About 4 liters in total, for the whole family",correct:!1}],explanation:"Plan about 4 liters of water per person per day, for at least 3 days, for drinking and hygiene."},{question:"How often should we check the contents of the kit?",options:[{text:"Once every 10 years",correct:!1},{text:"Once every 6 months",correct:!0},{text:"Only when we buy it",correct:!1},{text:"It never needs checking",correct:!1}],explanation:"Check the kit once every 6 months and replace any water or food that has expired."},{question:"Which of these items is NOT needed in an emergency kit?",options:[{text:"Water",correct:!1},{text:"A flashlight",correct:!1},{text:"A television",correct:!0},{text:"Medicines",correct:!1}],explanation:"A television is not needed. The kit holds what you need in an emergency: water, food, medicines, a flashlight and a small radio."},{question:"Your family has five members, including a grandfather who takes medicine every day and a baby. Which kit plan is best?",options:[{text:"Water and food for all five for at least 3 days, plus the grandfather's medicine and the baby's needs",correct:!0},{text:"One large bottle of water for the whole family and some snacks for the children",correct:!1},{text:"Food and water only, because medicines can be bought after the emergency",correct:!1},{text:"Everything packed in heavy boxes stored in the basement to keep it safe",correct:!1}],explanation:"Every family member needs about 4 liters of water a day for at least 3 days, and the kit must hold each person's medicines and particular needs, such as the baby's."},{question:"After a disaster the power is out and the phone network is busy. Which item in the kit helps you hear official announcements?",options:[{text:"A small battery-powered radio",correct:!0},{text:"A spare set of house keys",correct:!1},{text:"A whistle to call for help",correct:!1},{text:"Copies of identity documents",correct:!1}],explanation:"A small radio with batteries works without electricity, so you can follow official announcements and instructions."},{question:"During the 6-month check you find canned food that expired last month. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Replace it with fresh food and check the rest of the kit",correct:!0},{text:"Keep it, because canned food never really goes bad",correct:!1},{text:"Throw it away and leave the space empty until later",correct:!1},{text:"Move it to the bottom of the bag and use it last",correct:!1}],explanation:"Check the kit every 6 months and replace food and water that have expired, so that the kit is ready when you need it."}],steps:[{title:"Water and food for every person",detail:"About 4 liters of water per person per day for at least 3 days, for drinking and hygiene, and canned or dry food that needs no cooking.",critical:!0},{title:"Medicines and first aid supplies",detail:"Bandages, clean dressings, tape, gloves, scissors and the medicines each family member needs. Do not forget the medicines.",critical:!0},{title:"Light, sound and power",detail:"A flashlight and batteries, a small radio, a power bank for the phone, a change of clothes, a blanket and a whistle to call for help."},{title:"Documents and money",detail:"Copies of identity cards and passports, some cash because bank cards may not work, a list of important numbers and spare keys."},{title:"Fit the kit to your family",detail:"Add what children, the elderly, the sick and pets need. Use a light bag and keep it near the door, where it is easy to reach."},{title:"Check it every 6 months",detail:"Replace water and food that have expired, check the batteries and medicines and update the phone list."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"An official announcement says heavy rain and possible flooding are expected, and families should be ready to leave if they are told to. Tonight you open your kit: the water bottles are past their date, the flashlight batteries are dead and your mother's daily medicine is not in it. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Tonight I replace the water and batteries, add my mother's medicine and put the kit back by the door",correct:!0,feedback:"The kit only helps if it is ready before you need it: fresh water, a working light and each person's medicines."},{text:"I leave it as it is and plan to buy what we need on the way if we have to leave",correct:!1,feedback:"In an emergency shops may be closed and bank cards may not work; prepare the kit now."},{text:"I pack the whole family's belongings into several heavy suitcases instead",correct:!1,feedback:"Heavy suitcases slow you down when you have to leave quickly; the plan is a light kit with the essentials."},{text:"I wait until we are told to leave and then sort out the kit",correct:!1,feedback:"When the order comes there may be no time; check and refill the kit tonight."}]},{prompt:"The next morning Civil Defense tells the families on your street to leave. Your kit is ready by the door. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I take the kit, leave with the family and follow the instructions and route that Civil Defense gives",correct:!0,feedback:"The ready kit lets you leave at once; the official instructions tell you where to go safely."},{text:"I stay to guard the house, because the water may not reach our street",correct:!1,feedback:"Staying after being told to leave puts you and the rescuers in danger; take the kit and go."},{text:"I first spend time packing the television and other valuables",correct:!1,feedback:"Valuables cost time you may not have; the kit holds what you need, so leave now."},{text:"I first drive to the river to see how high the water is",correct:!1,feedback:"Going to look at flood water is dangerous and wastes time; follow the instructions and leave."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: After a strong earthquake at night, two neighboring families had to leave their homes. The first family had a kit by the door: water, food that needs no cooking, the father's medicines, a flashlight, a radio, a power bank, copies of their identity cards and some cash. They took it and reached the assembly point within minutes. The second family spent a long time searching in the dark for documents and a flashlight. At the assembly point the first family shared water and light with them. The next week both families checked their kits together."},{id:6,icon:"PhoneCall",accent:"#16a34a",title:"Emergency numbers",subtitle:"Knowing the numbers and how to call",summary:"Knowing the emergency numbers can save a life. Everyone should memorize these numbers and know when and how to call.",blocks:[{heading:"The main numbers in the Kurdistan Region",body:`• 115: Civil Defense (fire, rescue, building collapse, floods, gas leaks and hazardous materials).
• 122: Ambulance (medical emergencies).
• 104: Police.`},{heading:"How to make the call",body:"Stay calm and speak clearly. Say the exact location first: the area, the street, a nearby landmark and the floor. Then say what has happened, how many people are hurt or trapped, and any danger (fire spreading, gas, electricity, chemicals). Give your name and your phone number. Answer the questions, follow their instructions and do not hang up until they tell you to."},{heading:"Which number to call, and when",body:`• Fire, smoke, explosion, collapse, flood or a trapped person: 115.
• Injury, serious illness or an unconscious person: 122.
• Theft, threats and security incidents: 104.
• If there is a fire or incident and also injured people: 115 and 122.`},{heading:"The exact address",body:"Give the name of the neighborhood and the street, visible landmarks nearby (a mosque, a school, a shop), and the house number and its color."}],tips:["Save the numbers in your phone.","Put a list of the numbers in a visible place at home.","Teach the numbers to children.","Teach children the home address.","Memorize your home address exactly."],warnings:["Never make false or prank calls: they keep help away from people who need it.","Do not keep the emergency line busy without a reason: other people's lives are at risk.","Stay calm and speak clearly.","The exact address is very important."],activity:{prompt:"You saw a car hit a person on the street, and the person was injured. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I call 122, give the address and report the injured person's condition",correct:!0,feedback:"An injured person needs an ambulance: calling 122 with the exact address and the person's condition brings help quickly."},{text:"I continue on my way",correct:!1,feedback:"Walking away leaves the injured person without help; call 122 and give the exact address."},{text:"I record videos",correct:!1,feedback:"Filming does not help the injured person and does not respect them; call 122 first and give the exact address."},{text:"I just watch",correct:!1,feedback:"Just watching does not help and wastes time; call 122 at once and give the exact address."}]},quiz:[{question:"What is the Civil Defense number (fire and rescue)?",options:[{text:"122",correct:!1},{text:"104",correct:!1},{text:"115",correct:!0},{text:"911",correct:!1}],explanation:"115 is Civil Defense: fire, rescue, building collapse, floods, gas leaks and hazardous materials. 122 is the ambulance and 104 is the police."},{question:"When you call, what information should you give?",options:[{text:"Your name and phone number first; the team can find the location itself",correct:!1},{text:"Exact location first, then what happened, how many are hurt or trapped, any danger, your name and number",correct:!0},{text:"Only the address, then hang up quickly so the line stays free for others",correct:!1},{text:"What happened and how many are hurt first, then your name, and the location only at the very end if they ask",correct:!1}],explanation:"Say the exact location first so that help can find you; then what has happened, how many people are hurt or trapped, any danger, and your name and phone number."},{question:"What is the ambulance number?",options:[{text:"115",correct:!1},{text:"122",correct:!0},{text:"104",correct:!1},{text:"100",correct:!1}],explanation:"122 is the ambulance number, for injuries, serious illness and unconscious people. 115 is Civil Defense and 104 is the police."},{question:"You smell gas in the stairwell of your apartment building and you have gone outside. Which number do you call?",options:[{text:"115, because a gas leak is a Civil Defense emergency",correct:!0},{text:"104, because it may be someone's fault",correct:!1},{text:"122, because people may feel unwell later",correct:!1},{text:"No one, because the gas company will notice",correct:!1}],explanation:"115 covers fire, rescue, collapse, floods, gas leaks and hazardous materials. Call from outside, away from the gas."},{question:"During an emergency call, when may you hang up?",options:[{text:"When the operator tells you that you can",correct:!0},{text:"As soon as you have given the address",correct:!1},{text:"When you hear the siren coming nearer",correct:!1},{text:"After one minute, to keep the line free",correct:!1}],explanation:"Answer the questions, follow the instructions and do not hang up until the operator tells you to; they may need more information or have instructions for you."},{question:"Which description of your location helps the team most?",options:[{text:"Behind the school, opposite the bakery, green gate, second floor",correct:!0},{text:"Somewhere in the city, near the main road, please hurry",correct:!1},{text:"Near my house, you will see people standing outside",correct:!1},{text:"In our neighborhood, the usual place, everyone knows it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Give the area, the street, visible landmarks, the house and the floor, so that the team can find you without searching."}],steps:[{title:"Choose the right number",detail:"115 for fire, smoke, an explosion, a collapse, a flood, gas or a trapped person; 122 for an injury, a serious illness or an unconscious person; 104 for theft, threats and security incidents. A fire or incident with injured people: 115 and 122.",critical:!0},{title:"Say the exact location first",detail:"Stay calm and speak clearly. Give the area, the street, a nearby landmark, the house number and the floor.",critical:!0},{title:"Say what happened and what the dangers are",detail:"Say how many people are hurt or trapped and whether there is a spreading fire, gas, electricity or chemicals."},{title:"Stay on the line",detail:"Give your name and phone number, answer the questions, follow the instructions and do not hang up until they tell you to.",critical:!0},{title:"Prepare before an emergency",detail:"Save the numbers in your phone, put a list in a visible place at home and teach children the numbers and the home address. Never make false or prank calls."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"Smoke is coming from your neighbor's kitchen window. His wife runs out and says her elderly father fell inside and cannot get up. Which number do you call?",options:[{text:"115 and 122: there is a fire and an injured person",correct:!0,feedback:"A fire or incident with injured people needs both: 115 for the fire and rescue, 122 for the injured man."},{text:"104 only, because the police will tell the others",correct:!1,feedback:"104 is for crime and security; for a fire and an injured person call 115 and 122 directly."},{text:"122 only, because a person is hurt",correct:!1,feedback:"The smoke means fire, so 115 is needed as well as 122."},{text:"I first post a message for the neighborhood so people come to help",correct:!1,feedback:"A message is not an emergency call and it loses time; call 115 and 122 now."}]},{prompt:"The 115 operator answers. You are frightened and the smoke is getting thicker. What do you say first?",options:[{text:"The exact location: the area, the street, a landmark nearby, the house and the floor",correct:!0,feedback:"Location first: if the call is cut, the team still knows where to go. Then what happened, who is hurt and what the dangers are."},{text:'I shout "Come quickly, there is a fire!" and hang up so I can go and help',correct:!1,feedback:"Without the address they cannot find you; give the location first and do not hang up until they tell you to."},{text:"My full name, my phone number and the neighbor's family details",correct:!1,feedback:"Your name comes later; the location comes first so that help can start moving."},{text:"A long description of how and where the fire may have started",correct:!1,feedback:"The cause can wait; first the location, then what is happening, who is hurt and the dangers."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: A woman found her neighbor unconscious on the floor of his shop and called 122. Before anything else she gave the location: the neighborhood, the street behind the school, the shop opposite the bakery with the green door. Then she said the man was unconscious and breathing, that he was alone and that there was no fire or gas. She gave her name and phone number and stayed on the line, following the operator's instructions until she was told she could hang up. The ambulance found the shop quickly because of the landmarks she gave."},{id:7,icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Fire extinguishers: complete training",subtitle:"Types of extinguisher, fire classes and the PASS method",summary:"A fire extinguisher is a key piece of equipment for putting out small fires in their early stages. Every adult, at home and at work, should know how to use one quickly and correctly, because portable extinguishers only work for a few seconds. If the fire becomes large, leave the extinguisher, get out and call 115.",blocks:[{heading:"Fire classes (NFPA)",body:`• Class A: ordinary solid materials (wood, paper, fabric).
• Class B: flammable liquids and gases (gasoline, kerosene, gas). Water spreads a burning liquid.
• Class C: live electrical equipment. Do not use water or foam.
• Class D: combustible metals (such as magnesium). Water causes a violent reaction and an explosion.
• Class K: cooking oils and fats. Water spreads hot oil into a large fire.`},{heading:"Types of fire extinguisher",body:`Classes are given in NFPA letters:
• Water: Class A only.
• CO₂: Class B and live electrical equipment (NFPA B and C; under EN 2, C means gas and CO₂ is not rated for gas fires). It has a short range and is not for deep-seated wood or paper fires. Hold only the handle and do not touch the nozzle: it becomes extremely cold and burns the skin. In a small room, leave and ventilate after use, because CO₂ pushes out the oxygen.
• ABC powder: for A, B and C. Not for Class D and not for Class K.
• Foam: for A and B. Do not use it on electrical equipment.
• Clean agent: for sensitive equipment such as computers; its rating depends on the model, so read the label.
• Wet chemical: for cooking oil (Class K).
• Class D: only an extinguisher or agent whose label lists the burning metal.`},{heading:"The PASS method",body:`Before you use an extinguisher (every answer must be yes):
• The fire is small and just starting, like a fire in a wastebasket or a pan.
• The extinguisher is the right type for this fire.
• Your exit is behind you and the fire is not between you and the exit.
• The alarm has been raised: others are warned and 115 has been called.
• You are trained to use the extinguisher.
• No smoke or heat is blocking your way out.
• If the fire does not go out at once, stop, get out and close the door behind you.

1. P = Pull: pull out the safety pin.
2. A = Aim: aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the top of the flames.
3. S = Squeeze: squeeze the extinguisher handle firmly.
4. S = Sweep: sweep the nozzle from side to side until the fire is out.

PASS is the general method for most hand-held extinguishers. Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units: follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions.`},{heading:"Checking the extinguisher",body:`Visual check every month, by the person in charge:
• The extinguisher is in its place, visible and easy to reach.
• The safety pin and its seal are in place.
• The label is readable and faces out.
• The hose is not cracked or punctured, and the nozzle is clear.
• The body of the extinguisher is not rusted, damaged or leaking.
• If there is a pressure gauge, its needle is in the green zone. CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge: lift one to feel that it is full.

Service by a qualified technician:
• Inspection and maintenance: once a year.
• Recharge: after any use, even a short one.
• 6-year maintenance: stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers that need a 12-year hydrostatic test are emptied and checked inside every 6 years.
• Hydrostatic test (pressure test of the cylinder): every 5 years for water, foam (AFFF), CO₂ and stainless-steel dry chemical extinguishers; every 12 years for stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers with mild steel, brazed brass or aluminum shells.

A test date is not a replacement date. An extinguisher is taken out of service if it fails an inspection or test, is damaged, obsolete or recalled, has no readable label or is a non-rechargeable unit past the service life on its label. The rules for Duhok are not yet confirmed.`},{heading:"Particular risks at home and in the community",body:`• Kitchen: cooking oil is Class K. Use a wet chemical extinguisher or a large lid.
• Electricity: outlets and cables are Class C.
• Gas heaters and cylinders: gas is Class B. The priority is to stop the gas: a qualified person closes the gas valve only if it is safe to reach. Do not put out a gas flame if the gas cannot be stopped safely: get everyone out and call 115.
• Gasoline and kerosene: store them away from the house in a well-ventilated place.
• An ABC extinguisher is good for most home fires, but not for cooking oil (K) and not for metals (D).`},{heading:"Extinguisher colors (BS EN 3 and BS 7863)",body:`Color is not the fire class. Under the European standard BS EN 3 the extinguisher body is red; in the UK a colored band (BS 7863) shows the agent:
• Cream = foam.
• Blue = dry powder.
• Black = CO₂.
• Yellow = wet chemical.
• Water has no band.

Other countries use other colors and the color practice in Duhok is not yet confirmed, so always read the label and its class symbols.`},{heading:"Class F (cooking oil): the same as Class K",body:`Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. So Class F (EN 2) and Class K (NFPA) are the same class: fires in cooking oils and fats.
• Very hot oil can catch fire by itself.
• A wet chemical extinguisher is the right choice: it forms a soap-like layer on the oil that cuts off the oxygen and cools the oil.
• Restaurants and large kitchens need a Class F/K extinguisher.`}],tips:["Every home should have at least one ABC extinguisher.","Place the extinguisher near the kitchen door, not inside the kitchen next to the stove.","Teach all adult family members how to use the extinguisher.","Check the extinguisher every month; if it has a pressure gauge, the needle must be in the green zone (CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge: lift one to feel that it is full).","Do not pull the safety pin until you need to use the extinguisher.","Start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label or in the manufacturer's instructions, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller.","Keep your back toward the exit door, so that your escape route always stays open.","Once a year, have the extinguisher serviced by a qualified person (Service).","A portable extinguisher only works for a few seconds: be ready before you start."],warnings:["Never pour water on burning oil: a large fire will spread quickly!","Never use water on electrical equipment: risk of electric shock!","Do not use the extinguisher if the fire is bigger than a wastebasket, is spreading or smoke is building up: get out and call 115!","Always aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the top!","CO₂ extinguisher: do not touch the nozzle! It becomes as cold as about 78 degrees below zero and burns the skin.","ABC powder in a closed room reduces visibility and makes breathing difficult: after using it, leave the room and open the doors and windows."],activity:{prompt:"In the kitchen at home, the oil in a frying pan on the stove has caught fire! What will you do?",options:[{text:"I pour water on it",correct:!1,feedback:"Water turns to steam in hot oil and spreads the burning oil into a large fire; never pour water on it."},{text:"If it is safe, I turn off the stove and put a large lid on the pan to cut off the oxygen, or I use a wet chemical extinguisher",correct:!0,feedback:"Turning off the stove and covering the pan cuts off the oxygen; a wet chemical extinguisher is made for cooking oil."},{text:"I pick up the pan and carry it outside",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not pick up a burning pan; turn off the stove if it is safe and put a large lid on it."},{text:"I beat it with a cloth to put it out",correct:!1,feedback:"This is not what the lesson teaches: cover the pan with a large lid or use a wet chemical extinguisher."}]},quiz:[{question:"In NFPA letters, which fire classes is a CO₂ extinguisher rated for?",options:[{text:"A and D",correct:!1},{text:"B and C",correct:!0},{text:"A and K",correct:!1},{text:"D and K",correct:!1}],explanation:"In NFPA letters, CO₂ is rated for Class B (flammable liquids) and Class C (live electrical equipment); it is not for deep-seated wood or paper fires, metals (D) or cooking oil (K)."},{question:"In the PASS method, where should the nozzle be aimed?",options:[{text:"At the top of the fire",correct:!1},{text:"At the base of the fire",correct:!0},{text:"Around the fire",correct:!1},{text:"At the ceiling of the room",correct:!1}],explanation:"In PASS, aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the top of the flames, and sweep from side to side until the fire is out."},{question:"Why is water not used on an oil fire (Class K)?",options:[{text:"Because water has no effect on oil and the fire just keeps burning",correct:!1},{text:"Because the water flashes to steam and spreads the burning oil",correct:!0},{text:"Because the oil soaks up the water and becomes even more flammable",correct:!1},{text:"Because water itself catches fire when it touches very hot oil",correct:!1}],explanation:"Water instantly turns to steam inside hot oil and throws the burning oil out into a large fire. Use a large lid or a wet chemical extinguisher instead."},{question:"Which extinguisher is suitable for most home fires (Classes A, B and C)?",options:[{text:"Water extinguisher",correct:!1},{text:"CO₂ extinguisher",correct:!1},{text:"ABC powder extinguisher",correct:!0},{text:"Foam (AFFF) extinguisher",correct:!1}],explanation:"ABC powder covers Classes A, B and C, so it suits most home fires. It is not for cooking oil (K) or metals (D)."},{question:"In the UK color code (BS EN 3 with BS 7863), what color is the band on a wet chemical extinguisher (for cooking oil)?",options:[{text:"Red",correct:!1},{text:"Black",correct:!1},{text:"Yellow",correct:!0},{text:"Blue",correct:!1}],explanation:"In the UK code, a yellow band marks wet chemical. Color is never the fire class, and the practice in Duhok is not confirmed, so always read the label."},{question:"Under NFPA 10, how often should a qualified person carry out maintenance on an extinguisher?",options:[{text:"Every week, with a full service every month",correct:!1},{text:"Once a year, with a visual check every month",correct:!0},{text:"Only once every 20 years, with no checks in between",correct:!1},{text:"Only after it has been used on a fire",correct:!1}],explanation:"The person in charge does a visual check every month; a qualified technician does inspection and maintenance once a year and recharges the extinguisher after any use."},{question:"Which of these situations passes the go/no-go check for using an extinguisher?",options:[{text:"A wastebasket fire just starting, the right extinguisher, the exit behind you, 115 called and you are trained",correct:!0},{text:"A small pan fire, a water extinguisher, the exit behind you, 115 called and you are trained",correct:!1},{text:"A sofa fire spreading fast, an ABC extinguisher, the exit behind you and you are trained",correct:!1},{text:"A small wastebasket fire, the right extinguisher, but the fire is between you and the door",correct:!1}],explanation:"Every answer must be yes: a water extinguisher is wrong for cooking oil, a fire spreading fast is not small, and the fire must never be between you and the exit."},{question:"You used the home extinguisher for a few seconds and the fire went out. What happens to the extinguisher now?",options:[{text:"A qualified person recharges or replaces it before it goes back in its place",correct:!0},{text:"It goes back on its hook, because there is still some pressure left",correct:!1},{text:"It is kept for the next fire and checked again at the yearly service",correct:!1},{text:"The pin is pushed back in to seal it and it is put back as it is",correct:!1}],explanation:"Recharge after any use, even a short one: a partly used extinguisher may fail when it is next needed."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm first",detail:"Warn everyone in the home and make sure 115 has been called before you think about the extinguisher.",critical:!0},{title:"Do the go/no-go check",detail:"Every answer must be yes: the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher is right for it (read the label), your exit is behind you, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out. If any answer is no, get out and close the door.",critical:!0},{title:"Take the right position",detail:"Keep your back toward the exit. Start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label or in the manufacturer's instructions, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller."},{title:"Use PASS",detail:"Pull the safety pin, Aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, Squeeze the handle firmly and Sweep from side to side. Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units are used as their label says."},{title:"If it does not go out at once, leave",detail:"A portable extinguisher only works for a few seconds. If the fire does not go out at once, stop, get out, close the door behind you and wait for 115 outside.",critical:!0},{title:"After use",detail:"After any use, even a short one, have a qualified person recharge the extinguisher. After using powder or CO₂ in a small room, leave and air the room."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"In the living room a power strip behind the TV starts to smoke and a small flame appears on its cable. Your family is in the room. The ABC extinguisher hangs by the kitchen door. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I shout a warning, send the family out and have 115 called, then decide",correct:!0,feedback:"Raising the alarm and calling 115 come before any attempt to fight the fire; the go/no-go check comes next."},{text:"I pour a glass of water on the flame quickly before it gets any bigger",correct:!1,feedback:"Water on live electrical equipment risks an electric shock; never use water on an electrical fire."},{text:"I pull the burning cable out of the wall socket with my bare hand",correct:!1,feedback:"Touching a burning, live cable can burn you and give you a shock; warn everyone and call 115 first."},{text:"I open all the windows wide and wait to see whether the flame goes out",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting lets the fire grow; warn everyone and call 115 now."}]},{prompt:"The family is outside and 115 has been called. The flame is still small, your exit is behind you and you practiced with an extinguisher on a course. You use the ABC extinguisher with PASS, but after a short burst the fire reaches the curtain and smoke starts to build up. What now?",options:[{text:"I stop, get out, close the door behind me and tell the 115 team what is burning",correct:!0,feedback:"If the fire does not go out at once, the rule is to stop, get out and close the door; the team needs to know it is electrical."},{text:"I keep spraying until the extinguisher is completely empty",correct:!1,feedback:"A portable extinguisher only lasts a few seconds and the fire is spreading; get out and close the door."},{text:"I go to the kitchen to fill a bucket of water for the curtain",correct:!1,feedback:"The fire involves live electrical equipment and is growing; water and delay are both dangerous. Get out."},{text:"I open the windows to let the smoke out and then try again",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke building up means the check now says no; leave and close the door instead of trying again."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: A father had taken an extinguisher course at work and kept an ABC extinguisher by the kitchen door at home. One evening a wastebasket next to the desk caught fire. He shouted a warning, his daughter took her brothers outside and called 115, and he checked: the fire was small, the extinguisher suited it, the door was behind him and no smoke blocked the way. He pulled the pin, aimed at the base and swept, and the fire went out at once. He still let the 115 team check the room, and the next day he had the extinguisher recharged."},{id:8,icon:"Heart",accent:"#db2777",title:"First aid and CPR",subtitle:"Complete training in first aid and cardiopulmonary resuscitation",summary:"First aid is the initial help given to an injured or sick person before the medical team arrives. Everyone in the community should know how to perform CPR, stop bleeding and protect an injured person until the ambulance arrives. This lesson can help protect your life and the lives of your family.",blocks:[{heading:"CPR: reviving the heart and lungs",body:`1. Check that the area is safe.
2. Tap the casualty's shoulders and ask: "Are you all right?"
3. If they do not respond and are not breathing normally, call 122 and ask for an automated external defibrillator (AED).
4. Give 30 compressions in the center of the chest: 100–120 compressions per minute, depth 5–6 cm for all adults.
5. If you are trained and willing, give 2 breaths, each lasting one second. If you are not trained, or not willing to give breaths, give chest compressions only (Hands-Only CPR), without stopping.
6. Continue 30:2 (or compressions only) until the ambulance team arrives.
7. If an AED is available, use it immediately.`},{heading:"Stopping bleeding",body:`• Direct pressure: place a clean cloth on the wound and press firmly.
• If the cloth becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it; place another cloth on top and keep pressing.
• Tourniquet: for very severe bleeding from an arm or leg, apply it 5–7 cm above the wound, write down the time it was applied and never loosen it.
• Do not pull out an object that is stuck in a wound!`},{heading:"Treating burns",body:`• Heat burns: cool running water for 20 minutes.
• Chemical burns: rinse with plenty of water for at least 20 minutes.
• Electrical burns: first cut off the electricity! Then call 122.
• Do not burst blisters, and do not use butter, oil or toothpaste.`},{heading:"Fractures and falls",body:`• Do not move the injured limb: keep it still with a stick or a board (splint).
• Do not move a person who has fallen from a height: their neck or back may be injured. Keep them still and call 122. Move them only if the place is unsafe (fire, traffic, collapse, gas, rising water), keeping the head, neck and back in line. If they need CPR, lay them on their back on a firm surface. Do not put them in the recovery position.
• Wrap ice in a cloth and place it on the swelling.`},{heading:"Electric shock",body:`• Cut off the electricity immediately! Do not touch the casualty until the power has been cut off.
• If you cannot cut off the power (household electricity only), use a dry piece of wood to move the cable away from the casualty.
• Call 122; anyone who has had an electric shock must be examined by a doctor.`},{heading:"Choking: the Heimlich maneuver",body:`If a person cannot speak, cough or breathe and is holding their throat with their hands, they are choking on something.
• Give 5 blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades.
• Give 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich): stand behind the person, place your fist slightly above the navel and well below the breastbone, grasp it with your other hand and press quickly inward and upward.
• Continue 5+5 until the object comes out or the person becomes unconscious; if they become unconscious, call 122 and start CPR.
• Infants (under one year): 5 back blows (head lower than the chest) and 5 chest thrusts. Never give abdominal thrusts to an infant.`},{heading:"The recovery position",body:`If a person is unconscious but breathing normally, and no neck or back injury is suspected, place them in the recovery position:
1. Place their arm nearest to you at a right angle, pointing toward their head.
2. Bring their far arm across their chest and place the back of that hand under their cheek.
3. Raise the knee of their far leg.
4. Holding the knee and the shoulder, roll them toward you onto their side.
5. Tilt their head back slightly so that the airway stays open.

• Call 122 and watch their breathing until the ambulance arrives.`}],tips:["Every home should have a first aid kit.","Keep the list of emergency numbers in a visible place.","Teach the whole family CPR and the Heimlich maneuver.","Keep clean cloths, a tourniquet and scissors in the kit.","122 is always available: do not be afraid to call.","The brain is quickly damaged without oxygen: start CPR quickly.","For all adults, including the elderly, the compression depth is 5–6 centimeters: do not reduce it.","For a pregnant woman or a very overweight person who is choking, give thrusts in the center of the chest, not on the abdomen."],warnings:["Do not touch the casualty if the electricity has not been cut off!","Do not pull out an object that is stuck in a wound!","Do not burst burn blisters!","Do not move a person who has fallen from a height, unless the place is unsafe or they need CPR!","Do not use abdominal thrusts (Heimlich) on infants under one year old!","Continue CPR until the ambulance team arrives: do not stop."],activity:{prompt:"A friend's hand caught fire and they have a large burn. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I put butter or oil on it",correct:!1,feedback:"Never put butter or oil on a burn; cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes and call 122."},{text:"I cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes and call 122",correct:!0,feedback:"Cool running water for 20 minutes cools the burn, and a large burn needs an ambulance (122)."},{text:"I burst the blisters",correct:!1,feedback:"Never burst burn blisters; cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes and call 122."},{text:"I do nothing",correct:!1,feedback:"A large burn needs help at once: cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes and call 122."}]},quiz:[{question:"In CPR, how many chest compressions should be given per minute?",options:[{text:"50–70",correct:!1},{text:"80–90",correct:!1},{text:"100–120",correct:!0},{text:"150 or more",correct:!1}],explanation:"Push hard and fast in the center of the chest: 100–120 compressions per minute, 5–6 cm deep for all adults."},{question:"If a sharp object is stuck in a wound, what will you do?",options:[{text:"I pull it out",correct:!1},{text:"I leave it in place and call 122",correct:!0},{text:"I leave the person and go on my way",correct:!1},{text:"I wash it with water and pull it out",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not pull out an object stuck in a wound: leave it in place, press around it and call 122."},{question:"If someone gets an electric shock, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"I take hold of their hand",correct:!1},{text:"I start CPR immediately",correct:!1},{text:"I cut off the electricity",correct:!0},{text:"I pour water on them",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cut off the electricity first: until the power is off, touching the casualty can give you a shock too. Then call 122."},{question:"What is the main sign of complete choking?",options:[{text:"Loud coughing and noisy breathing, with a red face and watery eyes",correct:!1},{text:"Cannot speak, cough or breathe, with the hands at the throat",correct:!0},{text:"A mild cough, but the person can still speak",correct:!1},{text:"A headache and dizziness after eating",correct:!1}],explanation:"In complete choking, the person cannot speak, cough or breathe and holds their throat. Give 5 back blows, then 5 abdominal thrusts."},{question:"Who is the recovery position used for?",options:[{text:"An unconscious person still breathing normally",correct:!0},{text:"An unconscious person who is not breathing at all",correct:!1},{text:"A conscious person with a minor wound on the arm",correct:!1},{text:"A healthy person who wants to rest after exercise",correct:!1}],explanation:"The recovery position is for an unconscious person who is breathing normally, with no suspected neck or back injury. A person who is not breathing needs CPR."},{question:"A person faints at home and does not wake up, but is breathing normally. They did not fall from a height and have no injury. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Place them in the recovery position, call 122 and watch their breathing",correct:!0},{text:"Sit them upright in a chair and give them some water to drink",correct:!1},{text:"Leave them lying on their back and go outside to fetch help",correct:!1},{text:"Lift their legs and pour cold water on their face to wake them",correct:!1}],explanation:"Unconscious, breathing normally and no suspected neck or back injury: recovery position, call 122 and keep watching the breathing. Put nothing in the mouth of an unconscious person."},{question:"A cleaning chemical splashes onto your child's arm and the skin turns red and painful. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Rinse it with plenty of water for at least 20 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"Wipe it with a dry cloth and cover it with a bandage",correct:!1},{text:"Put butter or oil on it to calm the burning",correct:!1},{text:"Rinse it for a few seconds and then put ice on it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Chemical burns are rinsed with plenty of water for at least 20 minutes. Do not use butter, oil or toothpaste."}],steps:[{title:"Make the scene safe",detail:"Check that the area is safe. After an electric shock, cut off the electricity before you touch the casualty; if you cannot (household electricity only), move the cable away with a dry piece of wood.",critical:!0},{title:"Check response and breathing",detail:'Tap the shoulders and ask: "Are you all right?" If there is no response and no normal breathing, call 122 and ask for an AED.',critical:!0},{title:"CPR until help arrives",detail:"30 compressions in the center of the chest, 100–120 per minute, 5–6 cm deep for all adults; 2 breaths of one second each if you are trained and willing, otherwise compressions only. Use the AED as soon as it arrives and do not stop until the ambulance team takes over.",critical:!0},{title:"After a fall from a height: keep them still",detail:"Do not move the person unless the place is unsafe or they need CPR; if you must move them, keep the head, neck and back in line. Call 122. No recovery position when a neck or back injury is suspected.",critical:!0},{title:"Unconscious but breathing: the recovery position",detail:"If the person is breathing normally and no neck or back injury is suspected, place them in the recovery position, call 122 and watch their breathing."},{title:"Bleeding and burns",detail:"Press firmly on a bleeding wound and add cloths on top; use a tourniquet for very severe bleeding from an arm or leg. Cool heat burns under cool running water for 20 minutes; rinse chemical burns with plenty of water for at least 20 minutes.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"Your neighbor falls from a ladder while working on his roof and lands on the ground in the yard. He is awake, groaning and says his neck hurts. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I keep him still where he is, call 122 and watch his breathing while I wait",correct:!0,feedback:"After a fall from a height the neck or back may be injured: do not move him unless the place is unsafe or he needs CPR."},{text:"I help him sit up slowly so that he can breathe more easily",correct:!1,feedback:"Sitting up moves the neck and back; keep him still and call 122."},{text:"I roll him onto his side in the recovery position",correct:!1,feedback:"The recovery position is not used when a neck or back injury is suspected; keep him still."},{text:"I carry him inside to a bed with the help of another neighbor",correct:!1,feedback:"Carrying him can make a neck or back injury worse; move him only if the place is unsafe."}]},{prompt:"While you wait, he stops responding and is no longer breathing normally. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I lay him on his back on firm ground, keeping the head, neck and back in line, and start CPR; someone brings an AED if there is one",correct:!0,feedback:"CPR is needed now, so turning him onto his back is right; keep the head, neck and back in line and do not stop until help arrives."},{text:"I leave him as he is, because his neck may be injured",correct:!1,feedback:"The lesson allows turning him onto his back when he needs CPR; start compressions now."},{text:"I put him in the recovery position so his airway stays open",correct:!1,feedback:"He is not breathing normally, so he needs CPR, not the recovery position."},{text:"I pour water on his face and shake him hard to wake him",correct:!1,feedback:"Shaking can make a neck injury worse and water does not help; start CPR and call 122."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: During a wedding in a community hall an older guest collapsed. A young man who had learned CPR checked that the area was safe, tapped the man's shoulders and asked loudly if he was all right. There was no response and no normal breathing. He sent one person to call 122 and another to fetch the AED from the hall entrance, then started compressions in the center of the chest. When the AED arrived, they switched it on and followed its voice. Two guests took turns with the compressions until the ambulance team took over."},{id:9,icon:"CarFront",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Road safety",subtitle:"Protecting yourself and your family on the roads",summary:"Road traffic accidents are one of the biggest causes of death and injury. Learning the traffic laws and following them can protect your life. This lesson covers the safety of pedestrians, drivers and children.",blocks:[{heading:"Pedestrian safety",body:"Always walk on the sidewalk. Before crossing the road, look both ways and cross at a pedestrian crossing. Do not look at your phone while crossing. At night, wear light-colored clothes or clothes with reflective markings."},{heading:"Driving safety",body:"Always wear your seat belt. Do not use your phone while driving. Keep to a suitable, legal speed. In winter, use suitable tires."},{heading:"Children in the car",body:"Always put children in a child car seat that suits their age and weight. Children aged 12 and under should sit in the back seats. Never leave children alone in the car."},{heading:"Motorcycles and bicycles",body:"Always wear a protective helmet. At night, lights and reflective markings are essential. Wear gloves and suitable footwear."}],tips:["Always wear your seat belt.","Do not use your phone while driving.","Children should sit in the back seats.","Wear a protective helmet on motorcycles and bicycles."],warnings:["Phone + driving = danger of death.","Speed + snow or ice = accident."],activity:{prompt:"You are driving and your phone rings. An important message has arrived. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I keep driving and read the message",correct:!1,feedback:"Phone plus driving means danger of death; stop in a safe place first, then read the message."},{text:"I stop in a safe place at the side of the road and then reply",correct:!0,feedback:"Stopping in a safe place first means you can reply without putting anyone on the road in danger."},{text:"I use the phone with one hand",correct:!1,feedback:"Using the phone with one hand is still using it while driving; stop in a safe place first."},{text:"I type a quick reply while driving",correct:!1,feedback:"Typing while driving is one of the most dangerous things you can do; stop in a safe place, then reply."}]},quiz:[{question:"Up to what age should children sit in the back seats of a car?",options:[{text:"12 years",correct:!0},{text:"6 years",correct:!1},{text:"18 years",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"Children aged 12 and under should sit in the back seats, in a child car seat that suits their age and weight."},{question:"Which of these is one of the most dangerous things to do while driving?",options:[{text:"Using a phone",correct:!0},{text:"Wearing a seat belt",correct:!1},{text:"Driving at the legal speed",correct:!1},{text:"Stopping at a red traffic light",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not use a phone while driving: phone plus driving means danger of death. A seat belt and the legal speed help keep you safe."},{question:"May children be left alone in a parked car?",options:[{text:"No, not even for a short time",correct:!0},{text:"Yes, if the windows are open a little",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, for a few minutes",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, if the car is parked in the shade",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never leave children alone in the car, not even for a short time. Always seat them in a child car seat that suits their age and weight."},{question:"You need to cross a busy road. The pedestrian crossing is a short walk away. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Walk to the crossing, look both ways and cross without using your phone",correct:!0},{text:"Run across between the cars, because it is quicker than walking",correct:!1},{text:"Cross quickly while finishing the message you are writing",correct:!1},{text:"Step out from behind a parked bus, where drivers can see you",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cross at a pedestrian crossing after looking both ways, and keep your phone away while crossing."},{question:"Your 5-year-old asks to sit on your lap in the front for a short drive. What do you say?",options:[{text:"No: you ride in the back, in a car seat that suits your age and weight",correct:!0},{text:"Yes, if we drive slowly and only go a short distance",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, if you hold on tightly to the seat belt with me",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, but only in the daytime when the roads are quiet",correct:!1}],explanation:"Children always ride in a car seat that suits their age and weight, and children aged 12 and under sit in the back, even on short trips."},{question:"You ride a bicycle home after dark. What makes you safest?",options:[{text:"A helmet, lights and reflective markings",correct:!0},{text:"Dark clothes so that you are not noticed",correct:!1},{text:"Riding fast to spend less time on the road",correct:!1},{text:"Music in your ears to stay calm and alert",correct:!1}],explanation:"Always wear a helmet; at night lights and reflective markings are essential so that drivers can see you."}],steps:[{title:"Before you drive: belts and car seats",detail:"Everyone wears a seat belt. Children ride in a car seat that suits their age and weight, and children aged 12 and under sit in the back.",critical:!0},{title:"Put the phone away",detail:"Do not use your phone while driving. If you must answer, first stop in a safe place at the side of the road.",critical:!0},{title:"Drive at a suitable speed",detail:"Keep to a suitable, legal speed. On snow and ice drive slowly and increase the distance to the car in front.",critical:!0},{title:"Walk safely",detail:"Use the sidewalk, look both ways and cross at a pedestrian crossing without looking at your phone. At night wear light-colored clothes or clothes with reflective markings."},{title:"On a motorcycle or bicycle",detail:"Always wear a helmet; use lights and reflective markings at night, and wear gloves and suitable footwear."},{title:"Never leave children alone in a car",detail:"Do not leave children alone in a parked car, not even for a short time.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"Your family is setting off on a trip. Your 8-year-old wants to sit in front, and your sister wants to hold the baby on her lap. What do you do?",options:[{text:"The children ride in the back in car seats that suit their age and weight, and everyone wears a belt",correct:!0,feedback:"Children aged 12 and under sit in the back, in a seat that suits their age and weight; a lap is not a car seat."},{text:"The 8-year-old sits in front with a belt and the baby stays on my sister's lap",correct:!1,feedback:"Both are unsafe: children 12 and under sit in the back and a baby needs a car seat, not a lap."},{text:"The children sit in the back without belts, because it is a short trip",correct:!1,feedback:"Short trips are no exception; everyone wears a belt and children use a car seat."},{text:"The baby sits on my lap in the driver's seat so I can watch him",correct:!1,feedback:"A baby on the driver's lap is very dangerous; the baby rides in a car seat in the back."}]},{prompt:"On the road you come to a crash that has just happened. A man is lying on the ground beside the road, injured and groaning, and says his back hurts. Cars are still passing. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop in a safe place, call 122 and 104 with the exact location, stay clear of the traffic and keep him still",correct:!0,feedback:"After a crash the neck or back may be injured: do not move him unless the place becomes unsafe, and give the exact location so help arrives fast."},{text:"I lift him into my car and drive him to the hospital myself",correct:!1,feedback:"Lifting him can make a neck or back injury worse; keep him still and call 122."},{text:"I film the scene for social media and then drive on",correct:!1,feedback:"Filming does not help the injured man; stop safely and call 122."},{text:"I drive on, because other drivers will surely call for help",correct:!1,feedback:"Everyone may think someone else has called; stop safely and call 122 yourself."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: A family was driving home in the evening when the car in front braked suddenly and they could not stop in time. Everyone in the car was wearing a seat belt, and the two young children were in car seats in the back. The front of the car was badly damaged, but the family got out with only minor injuries. The father switched off the engine, moved everyone away from the traffic and called 122 and 104, giving the exact location. Afterwards he said he was glad that no one had been riding without a belt."},{id:10,icon:"LifeBuoy",accent:"#0891b2",title:"Water safety and drowning prevention",subtitle:"Protecting yourself and children near water",summary:"According to the World Health Organization (WHO), drowning is one of the leading causes of death among children and young people worldwide. In the Kurdistan Region, lakes, dams and rivers are very dangerous in summer. Every family needs to learn about water safety.",blocks:[{heading:"Children and water",body:"Never leave children alone near water: a small child can drown in very shallow water (a few centimeters, such as in a bucket or a basin). Near pools and water, always keep your eyes on them."},{heading:"Swimming safely",body:"Swim only in official places where there is supervision. Always have someone else with you. Before going into the water, find out how deep it is. Wear a life jacket at lakes and rivers."},{heading:"Rescue",body:`If someone is drowning:
1. Do not go into the water yourself!
2. Reach out or throw something to them (a rope, a stick, a life ring).
3. Call 115 and 122.
4. Once they are out of the water, check their breathing. If they are not breathing normally and you are trained, start CPR with breaths: drowning stops the breathing first, so breaths matter most. If you are not trained or cannot give breaths, give chest compressions only until help arrives.
5. Anyone pulled from the water must be checked by a doctor, even if they seem well: lung problems are common afterwards.`}],tips:["Do not leave children alone near water.","Wear a life jacket at lakes and rivers.","Do not go into the water to rescue someone: throw something to them.","Learn CPR."],warnings:["A few centimeters of water are enough to drown a small child.","The water in the region's rivers and dams can be very strong and cold, even if it looks calm on the surface."],activity:{prompt:"You and your family are near a lake. Your small child wants to go into the water. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I let them go: it is normal",correct:!1,feedback:"A small child can drown even in very shallow water; never let them go in alone."},{text:"I put a life jacket on them, go in with them and watch them closely",correct:!0,feedback:"A life jacket and an adult close by, watching all the time, keep a small child safer near water."},{text:"I tell them to stay at the edge and I move away",correct:!1,feedback:"Never leave children alone near water, even at the edge; stay close and keep your eyes on them."},{text:"I watch from a distance",correct:!1,feedback:"Watching from a distance is not enough: near water, stay close and always keep your eyes on them."}]},quiz:[{question:"If someone is drowning, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"I jump into the water at once and pull them out myself",correct:!1},{text:"I throw them a rope or a stick and call 115 and 122",correct:!0},{text:"I just shout for help and wait for someone to come",correct:!1},{text:"I wait on the bank in case they reach the shore alone",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not go into the water yourself. Reach out or throw something to them, such as a rope, a stick or a life ring, and call 115 and 122."},{question:"How much water is enough for a small child to drown?",options:[{text:"A few centimeters, as in a bucket",correct:!0},{text:"Only water deeper than the child is tall",correct:!1},{text:"Only deep lakes and dams",correct:!1},{text:"At least water up to the child's waist",correct:!1}],explanation:"A few centimeters of water are enough to drown a small child, for example in a bucket or a basin. Never leave children alone near water."},{question:"Someone pulled from the water is not breathing normally. You are trained in CPR. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Start CPR that includes breaths",correct:!0},{text:"Give chest compressions only",correct:!1},{text:"Wait for them to start breathing",correct:!1},{text:"Turn them over so the water drains out",correct:!1}],explanation:"If they are not breathing normally and you are trained, start CPR with breaths: drowning stops the breathing first, so breaths matter most. If you are not trained or cannot give breaths, give chest compressions only until help arrives."},{question:"A man pulled from a lake is coughing but says he feels fine and wants to go home. What do you advise?",options:[{text:"He must be checked by a doctor, even though he seems well",correct:!0},{text:"He can go home and rest, because he is talking normally",correct:!1},{text:"He should lie in the sun for a while and then decide",correct:!1},{text:"He can swim again later if the coughing stops soon",correct:!1}],explanation:"Everyone pulled from the water must be checked by a doctor, even if they seem well: lung problems are common afterwards."},{question:"Your friend is a strong swimmer and wants to jump into a fast river to save a child. What do you tell him?",options:[{text:"Don't go in: reach or throw something that floats, and call 115 and 122",correct:!0},{text:"Go in quickly, but take off your shoes and clothes first",correct:!1},{text:"Go in only if you tie a rope around your own waist",correct:!1},{text:"Wait until the child stops moving, then swim out together",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not go into the water yourself, even if you swim well: the region's rivers can be very strong and cold. Reach or throw something to the child and call 115 and 122."},{question:"You are bathing your toddler and the doorbell rings. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Take the child out of the water and bring them with you",correct:!0},{text:"Leave the child sitting in the bath, because the water is shallow",correct:!1},{text:"Ask the four-year-old brother to watch the baby for a minute",correct:!1},{text:"Leave the child with toys and come back as fast as you can",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never leave a small child alone near water, not even for a moment: a few centimeters of water are enough to drown."}],steps:[{title:"Watch children near any water",detail:"Never leave children alone near water: a small child can drown in a few centimeters of water, such as in a bucket or a basin. Always keep your eyes on them.",critical:!0},{title:"Swim safely",detail:"Swim only in official, supervised places, never alone. Find out how deep the water is before you go in, and wear a life jacket at lakes and rivers."},{title:"Someone is drowning: do not go in yourself",detail:"Do not go into the water yourself. Reach out with something (a stick or a rope) or throw something that floats, such as a life ring.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 115 and 122",detail:"Call 115 and 122 at once, or have someone call while you reach or throw.",critical:!0},{title:"Out of the water: check breathing",detail:"If the person is not breathing normally and you are trained, start CPR with breaths; if you are not trained or cannot give breaths, give chest compressions only until help arrives.",critical:!0},{title:"Everyone pulled from the water sees a doctor",detail:"Even if they seem well, because lung problems are common afterwards.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"At a family picnic by a river, a teenager slips into the current and is struggling in the water not far from the bank. Someone shouts that a good swimmer should jump in. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I tell everyone to stay out of the water, reach out with a long branch or throw a rope or a life ring, and have someone call 115 and 122",correct:!0,feedback:"Reach or throw, do not go: going into the water puts a second life in danger. Calling 115 and 122 brings trained help."},{text:"I jump in at once, because I am a strong swimmer",correct:!1,feedback:"The lesson's rule is not to go into the water yourself: river water can be very strong and cold, even for good swimmers; reach or throw instead."},{text:"We make a human chain into the river by holding hands",correct:!1,feedback:"Going into the water puts more people in danger; reach or throw something from the bank."},{text:"I run along the bank shouting for help and wait for someone else to act",correct:!1,feedback:"Shouting alone loses time; reach or throw something now and get someone to call 115 and 122."}]},{prompt:"You pull him to the bank with the rope. He does not respond and is not breathing normally. You have never learned rescue breaths. Help is on the way. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I start chest compressions only and keep going until help arrives",correct:!0,feedback:"Breaths matter in drowning, but if you are not trained the lesson says: give chest compressions only until help arrives."},{text:"I turn him upside down to get the water out of his lungs",correct:!1,feedback:"Trying to empty out water wastes time; he needs CPR now."},{text:"I wait to see whether he starts breathing by himself",correct:!1,feedback:"Not breathing normally means CPR now; waiting wastes precious time."},{text:"I wrap him in a blanket and drive him to the hospital myself",correct:!1,feedback:"Help is on the way and he needs CPR now, not a car ride; start compressions."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: A grandmother was washing clothes in the yard, with a large basin of water next to her and her two-year-old grandson playing nearby. She went inside for a moment to answer the phone. When she came back, the boy was face down in the basin. She pulled him out at once; he coughed and cried. The family called 122, and although he seemed well, a doctor checked him that same day, as everyone pulled from water should be. Since then nobody leaves the child alone near water, not even for a moment."},{id:11,icon:"Activity",accent:"#d97706",title:"Earthquakes at home and in the community",subtitle:"Preparedness and response",summary:"Earthquakes come without warning. Family preparedness and knowing the correct actions during an earthquake save lives. Our area is not free from earthquake risk, so every family should be prepared.",blocks:[{heading:"During an earthquake",body:`1. Drop, Cover, Hold On: get under a sturdy table and hold on to its leg.
2. Stay away from windows, glass and heavy objects.
3. Do not run outside, do not stand in a doorway and do not use the elevator.
4. If you are outside: move away from buildings, trees and power lines.`},{heading:"The emergency kit",body:"Every family should prepare an emergency kit: water (about 4 liters per person per day, for at least 3 days), food that does not spoil, medicines, a battery-powered radio, a flashlight, blankets and important documents."},{heading:"After an earthquake",body:"If you smell gas or hear gas leaking: do not touch any switch or flame, open the windows on your way out, close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it without going into the area where the gas has collected and without making any spark, then get out and call 115 from outside. Do not go into damaged buildings. Expect aftershocks. If people are trapped under buildings or there are injured people, call 115 and 122."},{heading:"Make your home safer before an earthquake",body:"Prepare your home and family now, before the ground shakes:",points:["Fix tall furniture, bookcases, cupboards and large screens to the wall so that they cannot fall.","Keep heavy and breakable things on low shelves, and store chemicals and glass in low, closed cupboards.","Learn where the gas valve, the main electricity switch and the water valve are, so that you can close them after a quake if it is safe.","Agree on safe spots in every room (under a sturdy table or against an inside wall away from windows) and on a meeting place outside in the open, away from buildings and power lines.","Know the exits from your home and workplace, keep 115, 122 and 104 written near the phone and practice Drop, Cover, Hold On with your family."]},{heading:"If you are outdoors or driving",points:["Outdoors: move to an open place away from buildings, outside walls, balconies, large signs, power poles and wires.","Driving: pull over at the side of the road as soon as it is safe, away from buildings, bridges, trees and power lines.","Stay in the car with your seat belt fastened and the hazard lights on until the shaking stops.","Afterwards drive slowly and watch for cracks, fallen objects and damaged bridges, and do not cross a bridge that may be damaged."]},{heading:"Why earthquakes happen and what follows them",body:"Most earthquakes happen when large blocks of rock under the ground move and suddenly slip along a fault, releasing stored energy as waves. Damage is usually worst near the epicenter, the point on the surface above where the rock broke. The danger does not end when the shaking stops:",points:["Aftershocks can come minutes, days or longer afterwards and can bring down buildings that are already weakened.","Broken gas pipes and electricity cables can start fires.","On slopes, rocks and soil can slide, and wet, soft ground can lose its strength under buildings.","When you leave a damaged building, use the stairs, never the elevator, and go to an open place."]}],tips:["Drop, Cover, Hold On.","Always keep your emergency kit ready.","Shut off the gas only if you smell gas or hear a leak, and only if you can reach the valve without going into the gas and without making any spark.","Do not stay in damaged buildings."],warnings:["Do not run while the ground is shaking!","Do not go into damaged buildings!"],activity:{prompt:"A strong earthquake strikes. You are at home and the children are frightened. What will you do?",options:[{text:"We get under a sturdy table together with the children, cover our heads and hold on to the table leg",correct:!0,feedback:"Drop, Cover, Hold On under a sturdy table protects you and the children from falling objects until the shaking stops."},{text:"We run outside",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not run while the ground is shaking; drop, cover and hold on under a sturdy table."},{text:"We stand by the window",correct:!1,feedback:"Windows and glass can break during the shaking; stay away from them and take cover under a sturdy table."},{text:"We take the elevator down",correct:!1,feedback:"Never use the elevator in an earthquake; drop, cover and hold on under a sturdy table."}]},quiz:[{question:"During an earthquake at home, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"Get under a sturdy table and cover your head",correct:!0},{text:"Run outside as fast as you can while it shakes",correct:!1},{text:"Stand by the window so you can see what is happening",correct:!1},{text:"Take the elevator down to the ground floor",correct:!1}],explanation:"During the shaking, drop, cover and hold on under a sturdy table. Do not run outside, stand by windows or use the elevator."},{question:"You are outside when an earthquake starts. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Move away from buildings, trees and power lines",correct:!0},{text:"Run into the nearest building and get under a table",correct:!1},{text:"Stand close to a tall wall for protection",correct:!1},{text:"Shelter under a big tree until it stops",correct:!1}],explanation:"If you are outside during an earthquake, move away from buildings, trees and power lines. Do not run while the ground is shaking."},{question:"After the earthquake, may you go back into a damaged building?",options:[{text:"No, do not go into damaged buildings",correct:!0},{text:"Yes, to fetch important documents and medicines",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, as soon as the shaking has stopped",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, if it is your own home",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not go into damaged buildings, and expect aftershocks. If people are trapped or injured, call 115 and 122."},{question:"The shaking has stopped and your house has cracks in its walls. You want to fetch your documents from inside. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stay out, because aftershocks are expected",correct:!0},{text:"Go in quickly, because the shaking has already stopped",correct:!1},{text:"Go in, but only for a few minutes and stay near the door",correct:!1},{text:"Send a child in, because children are small and quick",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not go into damaged buildings; aftershocks are expected. This is why the documents belong in the emergency kit by the door."},{question:"You are visiting relatives in an upper-floor apartment when an earthquake starts. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Drop, get under a sturdy table, cover your head and hold on",correct:!0},{text:"Run to the elevator to get down to the ground floor",correct:!1},{text:"Go out onto the balcony to get into the open air",correct:!1},{text:"Stand in the doorway of the room until it stops",correct:!1}],explanation:"Drop, Cover, Hold On under a sturdy table, away from windows and glass. Do not use the elevator, do not run and do not stand in a doorway."},{question:"After an earthquake a neighbor shouts that a wall has fallen on a man and he is bleeding. Which numbers do you call?",options:[{text:"115 and 122",correct:!0},{text:"104 only",correct:!1},{text:"122 only",correct:!1},{text:"115 only",correct:!1}],explanation:"A trapped person needs 115 for the rescue and an injured person needs 122: call both."},{question:"You are driving when an earthquake starts. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I pull over away from bridges and power lines and stay in the car, belted",correct:!0},{text:"I drive fast to reach my family at home before the next shock comes",correct:!1},{text:"I stop under a bridge so that it shields the car from falling objects",correct:!1},{text:"I leave the car and run into the nearest building to take cover",correct:!1}],explanation:"Pull over away from anything that can fall and stay in the car until the shaking stops. Bridges and buildings can collapse, and speeding or running makes the danger greater."},{question:"You are setting up a new home. Which step makes it safer in an earthquake?",options:[{text:"Fix the tall bookcase and the large screen firmly to the wall",correct:!0},{text:"Put the heavy boxes on the highest shelf of the wardrobe",correct:!1},{text:"Keep the cleaning chemicals on an open high shelf next to the bed",correct:!1},{text:"Place the children’s beds under the large window for more light",correct:!1}],explanation:"Many injuries come from falling furniture and breaking glass. Fixing tall items to the wall stops them falling, while heavy things up high, chemicals and windows next to beds add danger."}],steps:[{title:"Prepare before an earthquake",detail:"Keep the emergency kit ready: water (about 4 liters per person per day for at least 3 days), food that does not spoil, medicines, a battery-powered radio, a flashlight, blankets and documents. Know where the sturdy tables are in your home."},{title:"During the shaking: Drop, Cover, Hold On",detail:"Get under a sturdy table and hold on to its leg, away from windows, glass and heavy objects.",critical:!0},{title:"Do not run, no doorway, no elevator",detail:"Do not run outside while the ground is shaking, do not stand in a doorway and do not use the elevator. If you are outside, move away from buildings, trees and power lines.",critical:!0},{title:"After the shaking: a smell of gas",detail:"If you smell or hear gas: touch no switch and light no flame, open windows on your way out, close the valve only if it is close and you can reach it without going into the gas and without any spark, then get out and call 115 from outside.",critical:!0},{title:"After the shaking: stay out of damaged buildings",detail:"Do not go into damaged buildings and expect aftershocks. If people are trapped or injured, call 115 and 122.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"In the evening your family is watching TV in the living room, next to a large window, when strong shaking starts. There is a sturdy dining table in the room. What do you do?",options:[{text:"We drop, get under the sturdy table away from the window, cover our heads and hold on to its legs",correct:!0,feedback:"Drop, Cover, Hold On under a sturdy table protects you from falling objects and breaking glass until the shaking stops."},{text:"We run down the stairs and out of the building as fast as we can",correct:!1,feedback:"Running while the ground shakes can make you fall or be hit; drop, cover and hold on."},{text:"We stand together in the doorway, because it is the strongest place",correct:!1,feedback:"The lesson teaches not to stand in a doorway; get under a sturdy table."},{text:"We take the elevator down to get outside quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Never use an elevator in an earthquake; drop, cover and hold on."}]},{prompt:"The shaking has stopped. There are cracks in the walls and you smell gas coming from the kitchen. The gas cylinder is inside the kitchen. The children are frightened. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"We touch no switches, open windows on our way out, leave the valve because it is in the gas area, get everyone out and call 115 from outside",correct:!0,feedback:"The valve is closed only if it is close and reachable without going into the gas and without sparks; here, get out, stay out of the damaged house and call 115."},{text:"I switch on the lights to look at the cracks before we decide",correct:!1,feedback:"A switch can make a spark and ignite the gas; touch nothing electrical and get out."},{text:"I go into the kitchen with my phone's flashlight to find the leak",correct:!1,feedback:"Going into the gas area with a phone risks a spark; leave and call 115 from outside."},{text:"We stay inside, because aftershocks are always weaker",correct:!1,feedback:"Aftershocks are expected and the house is damaged and full of gas; get everyone out."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: A strong earthquake struck at night. A mother had practiced Drop, Cover, Hold On with her children, so when the shaking began they got under the living room table and held on. When the shaking stopped, she smelled gas. She switched on no lights, opened the front door on the way out and took the children and the emergency kit outside. The valve was in the kitchen, where the smell was strongest, so she did not go near it. She called 115 from the street. The family stayed out of the cracked house until it had been checked and spent the night with neighbors, using the water and blankets from their kit."},{id:12,icon:"Siren",accent:"#dc2626",title:"The work of Civil Defense",subtitle:"The duties of the 115 teams and how you can help them",summary:"Civil Defense is an important government agency that protects the lives of citizens and private and public property. In the Kurdistan Region, 115 is the Civil Defense number for fire, rescue and disasters, and its teams are ready day and night. Citizens should know how to call 115 and how to help the teams, not hinder them.",blocks:[{heading:"The main duties of Civil Defense",body:`• Firefighting: in homes, offices, factories, farmland and forests.
• Rescue: from vehicles in accidents, from under rubble and from water.
• Floods and disasters: evacuating people, pumping out water and helping those affected.
• Hazardous materials (HazMat): gas leaks and chemicals.
• Training and awareness: lessons in schools and government offices.
• Safety inspections: in markets, restaurants, halls and high-rise buildings.`},{heading:"Official structure",body:`• In the Kurdistan Region, Civil Defense comes under the Ministry of Interior.
• Each governorate has a Civil Defense Directorate, such as the Duhok Civil Defense Directorate, with stations in the city and the districts.
• Departments: firefighting, rescue, first aid, training and technical departments.
• In major disasters, Civil Defense works together with the ambulance service (122), the police (104) and other agencies.`},{heading:"Their equipment and vehicles",body:`• Fire engines: carry water, hoses and pumps.
• Ladder trucks: reach high floors.
• Rescue equipment: hydraulic tools for cutting through the metal of vehicles and freeing trapped people (Jaws of Life).
• Breathing apparatus (BA): gives the firefighter clean air inside smoke.
• Special clothing for protection from heat.
• Radios for communication between teams.`},{heading:"How to call 115",body:`1. Dial 115.
2. Stay calm and speak clearly.
3. Say: the exact address (neighborhood, street and a visible landmark nearby), what has happened (a fire? an accident? is someone trapped?), how many people are injured or trapped, any particular hazard (gas, chemicals, electricity), and your name and phone number so that they can call you back.
4. Do not hang up until they tell you to.
5. If it is safe, keep the road clear for the fire engines.
6. Send someone out to the street to show the team the way.`},{heading:"How the community helps 115",body:`• On the road: when you hear a siren, calmly move to the right and stop so that the road is clear.
• Do not make false calls: every false call takes a team away from a real emergency.
• Keep access routes clear: do not park cars in front of gates or in narrow alleys.
• Witnesses: if you saw the incident, stay and give your information to the team.
• Do not crowd around the team or take photos, so that you do not get in their way.
• If the team asks for help, help them.
• If you see that 115 is needed, do not delay: call.
• Stay calm and do not spread fear and confusion.`},{heading:"Working and volunteering with Civil Defense",body:`• Applications for employment are made through official Civil Defense announcements.
• Requirements for age, education, physical fitness and tests are set by the Directorate: for up-to-date information, contact the Duhok Civil Defense Directorate.
• New staff receive basic training in firefighting, rescue and first aid.
• Citizens can take part in awareness lessons and training and become helpers in their own neighborhood during emergencies.`}],tips:["Memorize 115, and make sure every family member does too.","Know your home address exactly (neighborhood, street, house number).","When you hear a siren, move to the right and stop, not in the middle of the road.","Use 115 only for emergencies, so that the line stays open for people who need it.","If you suspect a fire or another danger, call: it is better for the team to come and find no problem.","Display the house number and the neighborhood name on your door so that the team can reach you easily.","Write a home escape plan and practice it with your family."],warnings:["Do not make false calls to 115: they waste the teams' time and put lives at risk.","Do not crowd around 115 vehicles or take photos: you get in the way of their work.","Do not film or share videos of victims: respect them.","When you hear a siren, do not drive behind emergency vehicles, and keep the road clear for them."],activity:{prompt:"A car accident has happened in front of your house. 2 people are trapped inside the car and no one else is there. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I immediately call 115 and 122 and give the exact address. If there is no fire, I do not pull out the trapped casualties, so that their neck and back are not injured. I stay in a safe place and follow the instructions of 115.",correct:!0,feedback:"Calling 115 and 122 brings rescue and medical help, and not moving trapped casualties protects their neck and back."},{text:"I quickly try to pull out the trapped casualties",correct:!1,feedback:"Pulling trapped casualties out can injure their neck and back; unless there is a fire, call 115 and 122 and follow instructions."},{text:"I record a video and post it on social media",correct:!1,feedback:"Filming and posting victims does not help and does not respect them; call 115 and 122 first."},{text:"I go to find someone else to help and do not call",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not delay: you are the only one there, so call 115 and 122 yourself, immediately."}]},quiz:[{question:"In the Kurdistan Region, what is the Civil Defense emergency number?",options:[{text:"991",correct:!1},{text:"107",correct:!1},{text:"115",correct:!0},{text:"122",correct:!1}],explanation:"In the Kurdistan Region, 115 is the Civil Defense number for fire, rescue and disasters. 122 is the ambulance."},{question:"When you see a fire engine with its siren on, what do you do?",options:[{text:"I calmly move to the right and stop to leave the road clear",correct:!0},{text:"I follow close behind it to get through the traffic much faster",correct:!1},{text:"I stop in the middle of my lane and wait for it to pass",correct:!1},{text:"I slow down to take a video of it on my phone",correct:!1}],explanation:"Moving calmly to the right and stopping keeps the road clear for the fire engine. Never drive behind it, block it or stop to film."},{question:"When you call 115, what is the most important thing to say first?",options:[{text:"Only your name",correct:!1},{text:"The address and what has happened",correct:!0},{text:'I say "Come quickly" and hang up',correct:!1},{text:"I ask how long they will take to arrive",correct:!1}],explanation:"The team must know where to go and what has happened. Then give the number of people hurt, any hazards and your name and number, and do not hang up until told."},{question:"What does a BA (Breathing Apparatus) do?",options:[{text:"It is a small fire extinguisher that the firefighter carries on the back",correct:!1},{text:"It is a breathing device giving the firefighter clean air in smoke",correct:!0},{text:"It is a radio that lets the firefighter talk to the team outside",correct:!1},{text:"It is a siren on the fire engine that warns traffic to move aside",correct:!1}],explanation:"A breathing apparatus (BA) gives the firefighter clean air to breathe inside smoke."},{question:"If there is a fire and also injured people, which number do you call?",options:[{text:"Only 104",correct:!1},{text:"Only 122",correct:!1},{text:"115 and 122",correct:!0},{text:"I do not call and I wait",correct:!1}],explanation:"A fire needs Civil Defense (115) and injured people need an ambulance (122), so call both."},{question:"You are reporting a fire to 115 and want to hang up quickly to go back and help. What is the right thing to do?",options:[{text:"Stay on the line in a safe place and answer until 115 says you can hang up",correct:!0},{text:"Say only that there is a fire and hang up so that the 115 line stays free for others",correct:!1},{text:"Give your name and number, hang up and wait for 115 to call you back",correct:!1},{text:"Hang up and call 122 and 104 as well, so that every service comes faster",correct:!1}],explanation:"The operator needs the details to send the right help and may give you instructions. Do not hang up until you are told to; 115 works with 122 and the police when they are needed."}],steps:[{title:"Make sure you are safe and choose the right number",detail:"Call from a safe place. Fire, rescue, floods, gas or hazardous materials: 115. An injured or sick person: 122. Crime or traffic incidents: 104. Fire and injured people together: 115 and 122.",critical:!0},{title:"Give the location first",detail:"The neighborhood, the street and a visible landmark nearby, and the floor if it is inside a building. If the call is cut off, the team can still reach the place.",critical:!0},{title:"Say what happened and stay on the line",detail:"What happened, how many people are injured or trapped, any particular danger (gas, electricity, chemicals), and your name and phone number. Answer the questions and do not hang up until you are told to.",critical:!0},{title:"Clear the way and guide the team",detail:"If it is safe, send someone to the street to show the team the way. Ask car owners to move cars parked in front of gates and in narrow alleys, and keep the access route clear."},{title:"Stand back and let the team work",detail:"Do not crowd around the team and do not film victims. If you saw what happened, stay and give your information to the team, and help if they ask you to."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"At night you see smoke coming out of a window of a neighbor’s house in your alley. Nobody answers the door. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I call 115 at once: the address and a landmark, smoke is coming from the house and people may be inside",correct:!0,feedback:"An early call with an exact location brings the team fast, and saying that people may be inside makes rescue their first task."},{text:"I break the door down and go into the smoke to search for them",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke can make you pass out quickly, and an open door feeds the fire with air. Call 115 and say where people may be."},{text:"I film a video and post it in the neighborhood group so that everyone knows",correct:!1,feedback:"A video does not bring the team and wastes precious time. Call 115 first."},{text:"I wait to see whether the smoke gets worse, so that I do not make an unnecessary call",correct:!1,feedback:"If you suspect a fire, call: it is better for the team to come and find no problem than to arrive late."}]},{prompt:"115 tells you the team is on its way. The alley is narrow, cars are parked on both sides and people are gathering in front of the house. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I send someone to the main road to guide the team, ask car owners to move their cars and keep people back from the house",correct:!0,feedback:"A clear road and someone to guide the team get the fire engines to the house fast, and people who stand back do not get in the team’s way."},{text:"I park my car at the entrance of the alley so that the team knows which house it is",correct:!1,feedback:"A car at the alley entrance blocks the fire engines. Send a person to wave them in instead."},{text:"I tell everyone to stay in front of the house so that the team can see where it is",correct:!1,feedback:"A crowd blocks the team and puts people at risk from smoke and breaking windows. Keep people back."},{text:"I hang up and stand near the house to film",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not hang up until 115 tells you to; filming gets in the team’s way and does not respect victims."}]}],realCase:"Late one night a woman saw smoke coming from a shop at the end of her street. She called 115 from the pavement and began with the street name and the large mosque next to the shop. She said that the owner usually slept upstairs. She stayed on the line and answered every question. Her son went to the main road and waved the fire engine in, while neighbors moved two cars that were blocking the alley. The firefighters found the owner on the upper floor and brought him out. They said later that the clear address and the guide on the road had saved valuable time."},{id:13,icon:"FlameKindling",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Types of fire",subtitle:"Not all fires are the same: each type needs its own method",summary:"Under the American system (NFPA 10), there are five classes of fire: A, B, C, D and K. Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. Each class involves a different burning material and needs a suitable extinguisher. Choosing the wrong extinguisher can make the fire bigger. This lesson will teach you how to recognize the type of fire and what to use.",blocks:[{heading:"Class A: solid materials (wood, paper, fabric)",body:`Very common at home: paper, cardboard, curtains, fabric, plastic and wooden furniture. Extinguisher: water or ABC powder. Aim at the base of the fire, starting from the distance shown on the extinguisher label, with your exit behind you.
• If your clothes catch fire: stop, drop and roll.`},{heading:"Class B: flammable liquids and gases (gasoline, kerosene, alcohol)",body:`These liquids catch fire quickly, and their vapors can ignite from a distance. Do not use water: water spreads the burning liquid and makes the fire bigger. Extinguisher for liquids: foam, CO₂ or ABC powder. Spread the foam gently over the surface of the liquid so that it covers it.
• Alcohol, acetone and other polar solvents: CO₂, ABC powder or alcohol-resistant (AR) foam; ordinary foam breaks down on them.
• Gas fire (LPG, natural gas): the priority is to stop the gas. A qualified person closes the gas valve only if it is safe to reach. Do not put out a gas flame if the gas cannot be stopped safely: unburned gas can build up and explode. If it is not safe, get everyone out, raise the alarm and call 115. Foam is not the general choice for gas fires. Under EN 2, gas fires are Class C.
• BLEVE: if a fire heats a gas cylinder or a tank, it can burst in a large explosion. Move away from it and call 115.`},{heading:"Class C: electrical equipment (NFPA)",body:`Computers, televisions, refrigerators, outlets and electrical cables.
• Never use water! An electric shock can kill.
• First cut off the electricity (pull out the plug or switch off the main power supply to the house), then use CO₂, a clean agent or ABC powder.
• ABC powder damages electronic equipment; CO₂ is better for sensitive equipment.
• In the European system (EN 2), electrical fire is not a separate class.`},{heading:"Class D: combustible metals (magnesium, titanium, sodium, lithium metal)",body:`These metals burn at very high temperatures.
• Do not use water or CO₂ on burning metals: they cause a violent reaction and a bigger fire.
• Use only a Class D extinguisher or agent whose label lists that metal; the agent differs from metal to metal (lithium, for example, needs an agent listed for lithium). Never use water, ordinary powder or foam. Dry sand only if the site fire plan or the metal's safety data sheet (SDS) names it and it is completely dry. Call 115.
• The lithium-ion (Li-ion) batteries in phones, laptops, power banks and electric bicycles contain no metallic lithium and are not Class D: if they catch fire, cool them with plenty of water and call 115. Class D is only for lithium-metal batteries.`},{heading:"Class K (NFPA) / F (EN 2): cooking oil",body:`A fire in hot oil in a frying pan or a pot on the stove.
• Never use water! Water turns to steam instantly inside hot oil and spreads the burning oil in an explosion.
• ABC powder is not suitable for this class.
• Use a wet chemical extinguisher, or turn off the stove and put a large lid or a fire blanket over the pan to cut off the oxygen.
• Do not pick up the pan or carry it outside.`},{heading:"Stages of fire development",body:`1. Incipient: the fire is small and there is little smoke. Only at this stage is it safe to use an extinguisher.
2. Growth: the heat increases and the fire spreads.
3. Fully developed: the whole room is on fire.
4. Decay: the fire dies down, but if air suddenly enters the room, there is a risk of an explosive ignition (backdraft).

• Flashover: when the hot smoke layer under the ceiling reaches about 500–600 degrees Celsius, everything in the room catches fire at the same time.`}],tips:["Before buying, read the fire classes written on the extinguisher label.","Keep at least one ABC extinguisher at home, and a wet chemical extinguisher or a fire blanket for the kitchen.","Under NFPA 10, in workplaces the travel distance to a Class A extinguisher must not exceed 23 meters.","According to NFPA, cooking is the leading cause of home fires: do not leave the kitchen while cooking.","Electrical equipment fires: never use water. The first step is to cut off the electricity.","Lithium-ion batteries: if one gets hot, swells or gives off smoke, move it away from flammable materials, move away from it and call 115.","Cooking oil: turn off the stove and put a large lid over the pan. This is the first step in putting out that kind of fire."],warnings:["Water on burning oil = steam explosion and a large fire.","Water on electrical equipment = fatal shock.","ABC powder on a computer = damage to the equipment.","Cool a burning lithium-ion battery with plenty of water and call 115: the fire can flare up again.","A fire bigger than a trash can = get out immediately, do not use an extinguisher."],activity:{prompt:"You are in the kitchen. The oil in a frying pan has caught fire and the stove is still on. Your family is in the bedroom. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I turn off the stove, put a large lid on the pan and warn my family; if the fire does not go out, we leave and call 115",correct:!0,feedback:"Turning off the stove and the lid cut off heat and oxygen, and warning your family lets everyone get out if needed."},{text:"I pour water on it to cool it down",correct:!1,feedback:"Water on burning oil causes a steam explosion and a large fire; never pour water on it."},{text:"I pick up the pan and carry it outside",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not pick up the pan or carry it outside; turn off the stove and put a large lid on it."},{text:"I run out alone and do not warn my family",correct:!1,feedback:"Your family is in danger too; warn them, and if the fire does not go out, leave together and call 115."}]},quiz:[{question:"A computer in your room has caught fire. Which method is correct?",options:[{text:"I pour water on it at once, before cutting the power",correct:!1},{text:"I cut the power first, then use CO₂, a clean agent or ABC powder",correct:!0},{text:"I cut the power first, then spray it with AFFF foam",correct:!1},{text:"I cut the power first, then use a wet chemical extinguisher for cooking oil",correct:!1}],explanation:"A computer is electrical equipment: cut off the electricity first, then use CO₂, a clean agent or ABC powder. Water can cause a fatal electric shock."},{question:"The oil in a frying pan on a gas stove has caught fire. What is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"I pour a glass of cold water on it to cool the oil down",correct:!1},{text:"I close the gas valve and put a large lid on the pan",correct:!0},{text:"I spray ABC powder on it from close up",correct:!1},{text:"I carry the burning pan outside as fast as I can",correct:!1}],explanation:"Turning off the gas stops the heat, and a large lid cuts off the oxygen. Water causes a steam explosion, and ABC powder is not suitable for cooking oil."},{question:"The lithium-ion battery of a phone has caught fire. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I cover it with a thick blanket, leave it in the room and close the door firmly behind me",correct:!1},{text:"I pour ABC powder on it once and, when the flames stop, put it back on charge",correct:!1},{text:"If safe, I move it away from things that burn, cool it with lots of water and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"I spray CO₂ on it once and leave it where it is to cool down",correct:!1}],explanation:"If it is safe, move the battery away from flammable materials, cool it with plenty of water and call 115. ABC powder and CO₂ do not fully stop this fire."},{question:"Flashover happens when the hot smoke layer under the ceiling reaches about what temperature?",options:[{text:"200 degrees Celsius",correct:!1},{text:"300 degrees Celsius",correct:!1},{text:"500–600 degrees Celsius",correct:!0},{text:"1000–1200 degrees Celsius",correct:!1}],explanation:"Flashover happens when the hot smoke layer under the ceiling reaches about 500–600°C: everything in the room catches fire at the same time."},{question:"According to NFPA, which activity is the leading cause of home fires?",options:[{text:"Reading books",correct:!1},{text:"Washing clothes by hand",correct:!1},{text:"Cooking (the kitchen)",correct:!0},{text:"Sleeping",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to NFPA, cooking is the leading cause of home fires, so do not leave the kitchen while cooking."},{question:"Oil in a pot on the stove caught fire, and you want to buy an extinguisher for this kind of fire. Which class should the label show?",options:[{text:"Class K in NFPA, which is Class F under EN 2: a wet chemical extinguisher",correct:!0},{text:"Class A: a water extinguisher, because the pot is in the kitchen and water is close",correct:!1},{text:"Class C: a CO₂ extinguisher, because the stove runs on electricity or gas",correct:!1},{text:"Class D: a metal-fire powder extinguisher, because the pot is made of metal",correct:!1}],explanation:"A cooking-oil fire is Class K in NFPA (NFPA K = EN F). Use a wet chemical extinguisher, or a lid or fire blanket. Water causes a steam explosion, CO₂ can spread the burning oil, and Class D is for burning metals, not a metal pot."}],steps:[{title:"Find out what is burning",detail:"Solids (A), liquids or gases (B), live electrical equipment (C in NFPA), metals (D) or cooking oil (K in NFPA, which is Class F under EN 2). If you do not know what is burning, do not use an extinguisher: get out and call 115.",critical:!0},{title:"Do the go/no-go check before any attempt",detail:"The fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher suits this class (read the label), your exit is behind you, people are warned and 115 has been called, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out. If any answer is no, get out.",critical:!0},{title:"Cut off the source if it is safe",detail:"Cooking oil: turn off the stove and put a large lid or a fire blanket over the pan. Electrical equipment: cut off the electricity. Gas fire: a qualified person stops the gas only if the valve is safe to reach; never put out a gas flame whose gas cannot be stopped, but get everyone out and call 115.",critical:!0},{title:"Use only an agent that suits the class",detail:"Never water on cooking oil or on live electrical equipment. ABC powder is not for cooking oil (K/F) or metals (D). Read the class letters on the label; a color is never the class.",critical:!0},{title:"If it does not go out at once, get out",detail:"Close the door behind you, take everyone with you and tell 115 from outside what is burning. If a gas cylinder is near the fire, keep away from it: heat can make it burst."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"In the sitting room, the extension cord behind the TV starts to smoke and a small flame appears. The TV is still plugged in. There is an ABC extinguisher in the hall. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I throw a glass of water on it at once, before the fire grows",correct:!1,feedback:"Water on a live cord can cause a fatal shock. Cut off the electricity first."},{text:"I switch off the main power if I can reach it safely, warn the family and call 115, then do the go/no-go check",correct:!0,feedback:"Cutting the power turns it into an ordinary fire and removes the shock risk. Then use the ABC extinguisher with PASS only if every answer is yes."},{text:"I pull the burning cord out of the socket with my hand and throw it outside",correct:!1,feedback:"Touching a burning, live cord can burn you or give you a shock. Switch off the power at the main switch."},{text:"I put a wet towel over it to cut off the oxygen",correct:!1,feedback:"A wet towel brings water onto live equipment and can cause a shock. Cut off the electricity first."}]},{prompt:"The power is off, but the flames have caught the curtain next to the TV and are climbing toward the ceiling; smoke is gathering in the room. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"The fire is no longer small: I get everyone out, close the door and tell 115 from outside that the fire has grown",correct:!0,feedback:"A fire climbing to the ceiling with smoke gathering is a no in the go/no-go check. A closed door slows the fire down."},{text:"I use the ABC extinguisher from close up, because the power is off now",correct:!1,feedback:"Cutting the power does not make a growing fire and gathering smoke safe. The fire is past the incipient stage: get out."},{text:"I open the windows to let the smoke out and keep fighting the fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Open windows feed the fire with fresh air and it grows faster. Get out and close the door."},{text:"I go to the kitchen for a bucket of water and come back into the room",correct:!1,feedback:"Going back into a room filling with smoke can trap you. A fire like this is a job for the 115 teams."}]}],realCase:"A teenager’s power bank began to hiss and smoke while it was charging on his bed, and then it caught fire. His father safely pulled the charger out of the wall socket, got the family out of the room and called 115. He knew that lithium-ion batteries are not Class D and that ABC powder does not fully stop their fire, so he poured a large bucket of water over it. The flames went out, then the smoke returned, so he kept cooling it and closed the door. The 115 team checked the power bank and warned that it could heat up again. The lesson: know what is burning before you use any agent."},{id:14,icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#0891b2",title:"How to use a fire extinguisher",subtitle:"Types of extinguisher, the PASS method and when to leave the extinguisher and get out",summary:"Fire extinguishers are found in homes, offices and public places, but many people do not know how to use them. A portable extinguisher only works for a few seconds, so you must be prepared before a fire starts. This lesson teaches you the PASS method, how to choose the right extinguisher and, most important of all, when you should not use the extinguisher and should get out.",blocks:[{heading:"Main types of extinguisher",body:`Classes in NFPA letters:
• Water: Class A only.
• Foam: A and B.
• CO₂: Class B and live electrical equipment (NFPA B and C; under EN 2, C means gas and CO₂ is not rated for gas fires). Short range; not for deep-seated wood or paper fires; in a small room, leave and ventilate after use.
• ABC powder: A, B and C, the most common type; not for D and not for K.
• Wet chemical: K, cooking oil.
• Clean agent: for sensitive electronic equipment; its rating depends on the model, so read the label.

Color is not the fire class. Under BS EN 3 the extinguisher body is red; in the UK a colored band (BS 7863) shows the agent: cream = foam, blue = dry powder, black = CO₂, yellow = wet chemical; water has no band. Other countries use other colors and the color practice in Duhok is not yet confirmed, so always read the label and its class symbols.

Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F.

For the home: an ABC extinguisher, plus a wet chemical extinguisher or a fire blanket for the kitchen.`},{heading:"PASS: step by step",body:`Before you start, every answer must be yes: the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher is the right type, your exit is behind you, the alarm has been raised and 115 called, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out.

1. P = Pull: pull out the safety pin.
2. A = Aim: aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the top. Start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller.
3. S = Squeeze: squeeze the handle firmly and steadily.
4. S = Sweep: sweep slowly from side to side until the fire is out.

• If the fire does not go out at once, get out and close the door.
• Small extinguishers empty quickly, often in a few seconds; the exact discharge time is on the label.
• PASS is the general method for most hand-held extinguishers. Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units: follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions.`},{heading:"Where to stand: safety comes first",body:`Always keep your back toward the door (your exit route). Start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label or in the manufacturer's instructions, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller. If the heat becomes too great, move back. Never let the fire get between you and the door.
• Smoke and heat can kill quickly: if the room fills with smoke, get out immediately.`},{heading:"When not to use an extinguisher",body:`• The fire is bigger than a trash can or is spreading quickly.
• The room is filling with smoke.
• You do not know what material is burning.
• Your exit route is not behind you.
• No one has called 115 yet (call first).
• The fire is getting close to a gas cylinder or a liquid fuel tank.
• Flammable liquids have spread over a large area.

In all of these situations: first get out, then call 115.`},{heading:"Checking the extinguisher at home",body:`Every month (visual check, by the person in charge):
1. The extinguisher is in its place and visible, and the way to it is clear.
2. The safety pin and its seal are intact.
3. The body of the extinguisher is clean, free of rust, undamaged and not leaking; the hose and nozzle are clear.
4. The instruction label is easy to read and faces out.
5. If there is a pressure gauge, its needle is in the green zone. CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge: lift one to feel that it is full.

Service by a qualified technician:
• Inspection and maintenance: once a year, recorded on the extinguisher's tag.
• Recharge: after any use, even a short one.
• 6-year maintenance: stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers that need a 12-year hydrostatic test are emptied and checked inside every 6 years.
• Hydrostatic test (pressure test of the cylinder): every 5 years for water, foam (AFFF), CO₂ and stainless-steel dry chemical extinguishers; every 12 years for stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers with mild steel, brazed brass or aluminum shells.

A test date is not a replacement date. An extinguisher is taken out of service if it fails an inspection or test, is damaged, obsolete or recalled, has no readable label or is a non-rechargeable unit past the service life on its label. The rules for Duhok are not yet confirmed.`},{heading:"Where should extinguishers be placed?",body:`• At home (at least one): near the kitchen door and, if available, one on every floor.
• In workplaces (NFPA 10): the travel distance to an extinguisher must not exceed 23 meters for Class A, and 9 or 15 meters for Class B (depending on the size of the extinguisher).
• The extinguisher sign must be visible from a distance.
• Hang the extinguisher on the wall at a height that is easy to reach (its top no higher than about 1.5 meters), not on the floor and not inside closed cupboards.`}],tips:["When buying: an ABC extinguisher you can lift and use (the weight is on the label) for the home, and a wet chemical extinguisher or a fire blanket for the kitchen.","Before buying, check the label for the fire classes and the standard mark (such as EN 3).","Practice PASS in a training session with a training extinguisher.","Read on the label how many seconds your extinguisher works for: most small extinguishers empty quickly.","Take part in Civil Defense training when it is held in your neighborhood or workplace.","Start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller.","Always keep your back toward the door."],warnings:["A small extinguisher only works for a few seconds: be ready.","The nozzle of a CO₂ extinguisher becomes very cold: do not touch it.","ABC powder on a computer can damage the equipment.","A fire bigger than a trash can = get out, do not use an extinguisher.","Lithium-ion batteries: ABC powder and CO₂ do not fully stop the fire. Cool the battery with plenty of water and call 115."],activity:{prompt:"You are at home. In the bedroom, a phone charger plugged into an outlet has caught fire. The fire is small and you have 2 extinguishers within reach: a 2 kg ABC extinguisher and a wet chemical extinguisher. What will you do?",options:[{text:"First I cut off the electricity (I pull out the plug if it is safe, or I switch off the main power). I warn my family and call 115. Then, with my back toward the door, I take the ABC extinguisher and use PASS, starting from the distance shown on its label; if the fire does not go out at once, I get out and close the door.",correct:!0,feedback:"Power off, family warned, 115 called, exit behind you and the right extinguisher: every check is yes before PASS."},{text:"I use the wet chemical extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"Wet chemical is for cooking oil (Class K); for this electrical fire, cut the power, then use the ABC extinguisher."},{text:"I pour water on it",correct:!1,feedback:"Never use water on electrical equipment: it can cause a fatal electric shock."},{text:"I run away and do not tell anyone",correct:!1,feedback:"Leaving without warning anyone puts your family in danger; warn them and call 115, even if you do not fight the fire."}]},quiz:[{question:"In PASS, what does the letter P stand for?",options:[{text:"Push (push the handle down hard)",correct:!1},{text:"Pull (pull out the safety pin)",correct:!0},{text:"Point (point the nozzle at the flames)",correct:!1},{text:"Press (press down to break the seal)",correct:!1}],explanation:"P means Pull: pull out the safety pin. Then Aim at the base of the fire, Squeeze the handle and Sweep from side to side."},{question:"Roughly how long does a small ABC powder extinguisher (2 kg) work for?",options:[{text:"About 3–5 minutes, which is long enough for a whole room",correct:!1},{text:"Only a few seconds; the exact time is on its label",correct:!0},{text:"About 1 minute, whatever its size or type",correct:!1},{text:"Without limit, until you let go of the handle",correct:!1}],explanation:"A small extinguisher empties in only a few seconds, so be ready before you start. The exact discharge time is on its label."},{question:"How do you know how far from the fire to start?",options:[{text:"Always half a meter away, so the powder reaches the flames and none of it is wasted",correct:!1},{text:"From the extinguisher label; keep your exit behind you and move closer only as the fire gets smaller",correct:!0},{text:"Always 7–10 meters away for every extinguisher, whatever its type, its size or the fire in front of you",correct:!1},{text:"From the size of the room: the bigger the room, the farther back you stand",correct:!1}],explanation:"There is no fixed distance: start from the distance on the extinguisher label, keep your exit behind you and move closer only as the fire gets smaller."},{question:"When should you not use the extinguisher, and get out instead?",options:[{text:"Never: once it is in your hands, always keep fighting the fire",correct:!1},{text:"When the fire is bigger than a trash can or smoke fills the room",correct:!0},{text:"Only when the fire starts in the kitchen, because kitchens have gas",correct:!1},{text:"Only when the fire is in a bedroom where people are sleeping",correct:!1}],explanation:"If the fire is bigger than a trash can, is spreading, or the room is filling with smoke, do not use the extinguisher: get out and call 115."},{question:"Which extinguisher is suitable for a cooking oil fire?",options:[{text:"Water (Class A)",correct:!1},{text:"ABC powder (Classes A, B and C)",correct:!1},{text:"Wet chemical (Class K/F)",correct:!0},{text:"CO₂ (Classes B and C)",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cooking oil fires are Class K (NFPA) or F (EN 2). A wet chemical extinguisher forms a layer that cuts off the oxygen and cools the oil; never use water."},{question:"You put out a small fire with your home extinguisher and used only a little of it. What should happen to the extinguisher?",options:[{text:"A qualified technician recharges it before it goes back into service",correct:!0},{text:"Hang it back on the wall, since most of the agent is still inside",correct:!1},{text:"Keep it for the next fire and have it checked at the yearly service",correct:!1},{text:"Shake it well so that the powder settles, then put it back in its place",correct:!1}],explanation:"After any use, even a short one, the extinguisher must be recharged: its pressure may drop and it may not work in the next fire."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm and call 115 first",detail:"Shout “Fire!” and warn everyone. Someone calls 115, even if you think you can put the fire out.",critical:!0},{title:"Do the go/no-go check",detail:"The fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher is the right one, your exit is behind you, 115 knows, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out. If any answer is no, do not use the extinguisher: get out.",critical:!0},{title:"Check the extinguisher and take your position",detail:"Check the fire class letter on the label, that the safety pin and tamper seal are intact and, if there is a pressure gauge, that the needle is in the green zone. Keep your back to the door and start from the distance shown on the label."},{title:"Use PASS",detail:"Pull the safety pin, aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, squeeze the handle steadily and sweep from side to side. Move closer only as the fire gets smaller. Small extinguishers empty in a few seconds.",critical:!0},{title:"If it does not go out at once, get out and close the door",detail:"Even if the fire goes out, keep watching it from a safe place until the team arrives: it can flare up again. Tell the 115 team what happened and which extinguisher you used. After any use, the extinguisher must be recharged by a qualified technician.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"In a home office, a wastebasket has caught fire from a cigarette. The fire is small. Your brother has gone to call 115. You hold an ABC extinguisher and the door is behind you. What do you check before you use it?",options:[{text:"The letter A on the label, the pin and seal, the pressure gauge and that no smoke blocks my way; then PASS from the label distance",correct:!0,feedback:"Paper is Class A and ABC powder suits it. A quick check makes sure the extinguisher works and your way out is open."},{text:"I go right up to the basket so that no powder is wasted and spray it from close up",correct:!1,feedback:"Getting too close can scatter the fire and burning paper and burn you. Start from the distance shown on the label."},{text:"I aim at the top of the flames, where the fire is highest",correct:!1,feedback:"A fire is put out at its base. Aim at the base and sweep from side to side."},{text:"I wait until the fire is a little bigger so that I can see it better",correct:!1,feedback:"Only a fire in its incipient stage can be fought with an extinguisher. Waiting lets it get out of hand."}]},{prompt:"You use PASS. After a few seconds the extinguisher is empty. The flames are lower, but the papers on the desk have caught fire and the smoke is getting thicker. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I get out, close the door and tell 115 that the fire is not out and that nobody is left in the room",correct:!0,feedback:"If the fire does not go out at once, it is time to leave. A closed door slows the fire and smoke, and your information prepares the team."},{text:"I open the window to let the smoke out and look for water",correct:!1,feedback:"An open window feeds the fire, and looking for water wastes time while the smoke builds. Get out and close the door."},{text:"I beat the burning papers with a blanket until the team arrives",correct:!1,feedback:"Beating can scatter the burning paper, and staying in a room filling with smoke puts you in danger. Get out."},{text:"I hang the empty extinguisher back in its place and go to fetch another one from downstairs",correct:!1,feedback:"An empty extinguisher must be recharged, not hung back up, and going back into a smoky room is dangerous."}]}],realCase:"In a small shop, rubbish in a bin behind the counter caught fire. The staff had practiced their plan before. One shouted “Fire!” and called 115. The other did the go/no-go check: the fire was small, the door was behind her and the ABC extinguisher showed the letter A. She started from the distance on the label and put the fire out with PASS. She then kept watch until the team arrived, and the extinguisher was recharged. In the shop next door, the extinguisher’s pin was stuck because it had never been checked; that extinguisher was replaced."},{id:15,icon:"HeartHandshake",accent:"#e11d48",title:"First aid for citizens",subtitle:"Primary assessment, CPR, AED, bleeding, burns and choking",summary:"A person's life can be saved by an ordinary citizen who knows what to do. After cardiac arrest, every minute without CPR and without an AED reduces the chance of survival. Following the AHA 2025 guidelines, this lesson explains the primary assessment (DRABC), cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR), the automated external defibrillator (AED), the Heimlich maneuver, stopping bleeding, burns and some other emergencies. Every citizen should learn this information.",blocks:[{heading:"DRABC: the primary assessment",body:`• D = Danger: is there any danger, such as electricity, fire or traffic? Before anything else, make sure you are safe.
• R = Response: tap their shoulders and ask loudly: "Are you all right?" If there is no response, call 122 and ask for an AED.
• A = Airway: tilt the head back and lift the chin.
• B = Breathing: for no more than 10 seconds, look to see whether the chest is moving and whether breathing is normal.
• C = CPR: if they are not breathing normally, start chest compressions immediately. Members of the public do not need to check for a pulse.`},{heading:"CPR: 30:2 or Hands-Only",body:`• Position: the center of the chest, one hand on top of the other, arms straight.
• Adults (including the elderly): depth 5–6 cm, rate 100–120 compressions per minute.
• 30 compressions + 2 breaths if you are trained; if not, give compressions only (Hands-Only). Children, infants and people who drowned especially need breaths: if you are trained and able, give CPR with breaths; if you cannot, compressions alone are better than nothing.
• Do not stop until the AED arrives, the ambulance team takes over or you can no longer continue.
• Children (from one year old to puberty): with one or both hands, depth about 5 cm (one third of the depth of the chest).
• Infants (under one year): with both thumbs and the hands encircling the chest, or with the heel of one hand; depth about 4 cm. The two-finger method is no longer taught (AHA 2025).
• For children and infants, when two rescuers perform CPR together: 15:2.`},{heading:"AED: automated external defibrillator",body:`AEDs are found in public places such as government offices, hospitals and large shopping centers. Anyone can use one, because the device gives spoken instructions. Using it early greatly increases the chance of survival.
1. Switch the device on.
2. Place the 2 pads on the bare chest, as shown in the picture on the pads.
3. Listen to the device's voice instructions.
4. Call out: "Nobody touch the patient!"
5. If the device tells you to, press the shock button.
6. Immediately continue CPR for 2 minutes and follow the device's instructions.

• During the shock, no one should touch the casualty!`},{heading:"Heimlich: choking",body:`Signs: the person cannot speak, cough or breathe, and holds their throat with their hands.
1. Give 5 blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades (back blows).
2. Then give 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich): place your fist slightly above the navel and well below the breastbone, and press quickly inward and upward.
3. Repeat 5+5 until the object comes out; if the person becomes unconscious, call 122 and start CPR.

• Infants (under one year): 5 back blows (head lower than the chest) + 5 chest thrusts. Never give abdominal thrusts.
• Pregnant women or very overweight people: give thrusts in the center of the chest, not on the abdomen.`},{heading:"Bleeding: Stop the Bleed",body:`1. Direct pressure: place a clean cloth on the wound and press firmly and continuously. If the cloth becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it; place another cloth on top.
2. Wound packing: if the wound is deep, pack clean wound gauze into the wound and then press firmly.
3. Tourniquet: for life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg, apply it 5–7 cm above the wound (not over the knee or elbow) and tighten it until the bleeding stops. Write down the time it was applied, and never loosen or remove it: only the medical team removes it.

• Do not pull a sharp object out of a wound!
• Eye injury: do not press on the eye.
• Head injury: if a skull fracture is suspected, press only gently.
• Call 122.`},{heading:"Burns and other emergencies",body:`• Burns: 20 minutes of cool running water (not hot water and not ice).
• Fainting: lay the person on their back and raise their legs slightly.
• Person with diabetes: if they are conscious and able to swallow, give them something sweet.
• Panic attack: stay calm and help them to breathe slowly.
• Head or neck injury: do not move the person; keep them still and call 122. Move them only if the place is unsafe or they need CPR, keeping the head, neck and back in line. Do not put them on their side (recovery position).
• Fracture: do not move the limb; keep it still and place wrapped ice on the swelling.
• Electric shock: first cut off the electricity.
• Snake bite: move away from the snake and keep the person calm and still. Keep the bitten arm or leg still with a splint, and remove rings, watches and tight clothing before it swells. Call 122 and get the person to a hospital quickly. Do not cut or suck the wound, do not tie a tight band or tourniquet, do not put ice on it and do not use traditional or herbal remedies.
• Poisoning: call 122 and keep the container or label to show the doctors. Do not make the person vomit, and do not give milk, oil, salt water or other home remedies; give nothing to eat or drink unless a doctor tells you to. If kerosene, gasoline or lamp oil was swallowed, vomiting is especially dangerous because it can go into the lungs. If the poison is on the skin or in the eyes, rinse with plenty of running water and take off soiled clothing. If the person is drowsy or unconscious but breathing normally, lay them on their side.`},{heading:"Drowning: once the person is out of the water",body:"Do not go into the water unless you are trained in water rescue: reach with a pole or rope or throw something that floats, and call 115 and 122. Once the person is out of the water:",points:["Lay them on their back on a firm, flat surface.","Check response and breathing for no more than 10 seconds.","If they are not breathing normally, start CPR at once; people who drowned especially need rescue breaths if you are trained.","If they vomit, turn them onto their side keeping the head, neck and body in line, clear the mouth and carry on.","Remove wet clothes if you can and cover them with a dry blanket to keep them warm.","Everyone rescued from drowning must be checked in hospital, even if they seem well: call 122."]},{heading:"Bee, wasp and scorpion stings",points:["Move away from the place, such as a nest, so that you are not stung again.","If a sting is left in the skin, scrape it out sideways with a fingernail or the edge of a card; do not squeeze it with tweezers.","Wash the place with soap and water, and put a cold cloth or ice wrapped in cloth on it to ease pain and swelling.","Remove rings, watches and anything tight near the sting before it swells.","Scorpion sting: keep the person calm and still, call 122 and get them to a hospital quickly; do not cut or suck the sting.","Trouble breathing, swelling of the face or throat, or fainting after a sting is an emergency: call 122 and help the person use their own adrenaline auto-injector if they have one."]},{heading:"Small wounds, burns and the first aid kit",points:["Wear disposable gloves if you have them.","Once a small wound has stopped bleeding, rinse it with clean running water, wash the skin around it with soap and cover it with a clean dressing.","Burns: remove rings, watches and tight clothing near the burn before it swells, then cover it loosely with a clean cloth that does not shed fibers, and do not burst blisters.","Keep a first aid kit in a known place that is easy to reach: clean dressings and bandages of different sizes, plasters, small scissors, disposable gloves, a light emergency blanket and a thermometer. Check it and replace what has been used or is out of date."]}],tips:["122 for an ambulance and 115 for fire and rescue: memorize them.","Take a CPR and first aid course, and renew it every few years.","Know where the AEDs are near your home and your workplace.","After cardiac arrest, every minute counts: start CPR quickly.",`The song "Stayin' Alive" helps you remember the rhythm of 100–120 compressions per minute.`,"Keep a first aid kit at home, in the car and at work.","In the first aid kit: clean cloths, gloves, a tourniquet, scissors and a list of emergency numbers."],warnings:["Never touch a person who has had an electric shock until the electricity has been cut off.","If a back or neck injury is suspected, do not move a person who has fallen, unless the place is unsafe or they need CPR.","Tourniquet: write down the time it was applied, and never loosen or remove it.","Do not put hot water, ice, butter or toothpaste on a burn.","AED pads must not overlap; for children under 8 years old, use child pads if available."],activity:{prompt:"On the street, you see a 55-year-old person collapse. They are not responding and not breathing. There is an AED at the entrance of a nearby government office, and another person is there. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I make sure the area is safe and check for a response. I call 122 and send the other person to bring the AED. I start CPR (30:2) immediately and use the AED as soon as it arrives.",correct:!0,feedback:"Calling 122, sending for the AED and starting CPR at once matter, because every minute without CPR lowers the chance of survival."},{text:"I only send someone to bring the AED and wait without doing CPR",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting without CPR wastes minutes that matter; start compressions at once while the other person brings the AED."},{text:"I give them water",correct:!1,feedback:"Never give anything by mouth to an unconscious person; call 122 and start CPR."},{text:"I record a video from a distance",correct:!1,feedback:"Filming does not help; this person needs CPR and an AED now, so call 122 and start compressions."}]},quiz:[{question:"In CPR, what is the compression depth for adults?",options:[{text:"2 cm",correct:!1},{text:"5–6 cm",correct:!0},{text:"10 cm",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"For all adults, including the elderly, press 5–6 cm deep at 100–120 compressions per minute. Do not reduce the depth."},{question:"Who can use an AED?",options:[{text:"Only doctors, because it needs a medical license",correct:!1},{text:"Anyone: the device gives spoken instructions",correct:!0},{text:"Only firefighters and paramedics with a permit",correct:!1},{text:"No one outside a hospital, because it is dangerous",correct:!1}],explanation:"Anyone can use an AED: the device gives spoken instructions, and using it early greatly increases the chance of survival."},{question:"How is CPR performed on infants (under one year)?",options:[{text:"With both hands, one on top of the other as for adults, pressing about 5–6 cm deep each time",correct:!1},{text:"With two thumbs (hands around the chest) or the heel of one hand, about 4 cm deep",correct:!0},{text:"With two fingers in the middle of the chest, pressing about 2 cm deep",correct:!1},{text:"CPR is not performed on infants; only breaths are given",correct:!1}],explanation:"For infants, use both thumbs with the hands encircling the chest, or the heel of one hand, about 4 cm deep. The two-finger method is no longer taught (AHA 2025)."},{question:"After applying a tourniquet, what should you do?",options:[{text:"I loosen it after 10 minutes so blood can reach the limb again",correct:!1},{text:"I note the time it went on and never loosen it before the medical team arrives",correct:!0},{text:"I release it every half hour and then tighten it again to protect the limb",correct:!1},{text:"I remove it as soon as the bleeding stops, even before the medical team arrives",correct:!1}],explanation:"Write down the time the tourniquet was applied and never loosen or remove it; only the medical team removes it."},{question:"An infant under one year old is choking on something and is still conscious. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Abdominal thrusts (Heimlich) immediately",correct:!1},{text:"5 back blows + 5 chest thrusts",correct:!0},{text:"CPR immediately",correct:!1},{text:"I leave them alone",correct:!1}],explanation:"For a conscious choking infant: 5 back blows with the head lower than the chest, then 5 chest thrusts. Never give abdominal thrusts to an infant."},{question:"After an electric shock from a fallen cable, a person is lying on the ground, still touching the cable. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I do not touch them until the power is cut off, and I call 115 and 122",correct:!0},{text:"I pull them away from the cable by the arm, then start CPR",correct:!1},{text:"I pour water on their burns to cool them, then touch them",correct:!1},{text:"I start CPR at once while they are still on the cable, because every minute counts",correct:!1}],explanation:"A fallen cable may still be live, and anyone who touches the person can be shocked too. Keep away, call 115 and 122, and help only after the power has been cut off."},{question:"You have pulled a neighbor’s boy out of a pool. He is now breathing normally and says he feels fine. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I keep him warm and call 122, because he still needs to be checked in hospital",correct:!0},{text:"I send him home to rest, because he is breathing and talking now",correct:!1},{text:"I give him a hot drink and let him go back to swimming after a while",correct:!1},{text:"I slap his back hard to get the rest of the water out of his chest",correct:!1}],explanation:"A person rescued from drowning can develop chest problems hours later, so a doctor must always see them. Slapping the back does not remove water and wastes time."},{question:"A bee has stung a man on the arm and the sting is still in the skin. He has no trouble breathing. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I scrape the sting out sideways with the edge of a card, then wash the place",correct:!0},{text:"I squeeze the sting hard with tweezers and pull it out quickly",correct:!1},{text:"I cut the skin and suck out the venom with my mouth so that it does not spread",correct:!1},{text:"I tie a tight band above the sting so that the venom does not reach the heart",correct:!1}],explanation:"Scrape the sting out sideways: squeezing it can push more venom into the skin. Cutting, sucking and tight bands do not help and cause harm. If breathing becomes difficult, call 122."}],steps:[{title:"Danger: protect yourself first",detail:"Before you go near, look for electricity, fire, traffic or gas. If the place is dangerous, do not go near and call 115 and 122.",critical:!0},{title:"Response: check and call 122",detail:"Tap their shoulders and ask loudly: “Are you all right?” If there is no response, shout for help, call 122 (on speaker if you can) and ask someone else to bring an AED if there is one nearby.",critical:!0},{title:"Airway and breathing",detail:"Tilt the head back and lift the chin. For no more than 10 seconds, look to see whether the chest moves and the breathing is normal. Occasional gasps are not normal breathing."},{title:"If breathing is not normal: CPR",detail:"Start chest compressions at once: center of the chest, straight arms, 5–6 cm deep, 100–120 a minute. 30:2 if you are trained; if not, compressions only. Do not stop until the AED or the ambulance team arrives.",critical:!0},{title:"Use the AED as soon as it arrives",detail:"Switch the device on, put the pads on the bare chest and follow its voice. Nobody touches the casualty during the shock. Restart CPR at once after the shock.",critical:!0},{title:"If they are breathing normally",detail:"If there is no suspected injury to the neck, back, hip or pelvis, put them on their side (recovery position); if there is, do not move them. Give nothing by mouth, keep checking their breathing and stay with them until 122 arrives."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"During a family dinner, your uncle (60) suddenly collapses to the floor. He does not respond when you tap his shoulders and shout. Several relatives are there. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I splash water on his face to wake him up",correct:!1,feedback:"Water does not wake an unresponsive person and wastes precious time. Shout for help and call 122."},{text:"I shout for help, ask one relative to call 122 and another to bring an AED if there is one nearby, and check his breathing",correct:!0,feedback:"An unresponsive person needs medical help and an AED fast. Sharing the tasks saves time and keeps you with him."},{text:"With the relatives’ help, we carry him to the car and rush to hospital",correct:!1,feedback:"Nobody can give proper CPR in a car, and the ambulance can start care on the spot. Call 122 and help where you are."},{text:"I put a pillow under his head and wait to see if he wakes up by himself",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting wastes time; his heart may have stopped. Call 122 and check his breathing."}]},{prompt:"He is not breathing normally; there are only occasional short gasps. 122 is on the phone. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"He is breathing, so I put him on his side and wait",correct:!1,feedback:"Occasional gasps are not normal breathing and can be a sign of cardiac arrest. Start CPR at once."},{text:"I start chest compressions at once: 5–6 cm, 100–120 a minute, taking turns with a relative so that we do not tire",correct:!0,feedback:"Early CPR keeps blood flowing to the brain until the AED or ambulance arrives, and taking turns keeps the compressions strong."},{text:"Before anything else, I feel for a pulse in his neck for a full minute",correct:!1,feedback:"Members of the public do not need to check for a pulse, and a minute without CPR costs a lot. Abnormal breathing is enough to start CPR."},{text:"I press gently and less deep, because he is older and his ribs may break",correct:!1,feedback:"The depth for all adults, older people included, is 5–6 cm. Gentle compressions do not move blood to the brain."}]}],realCase:"A middle-aged woman collapsed in a market and did not respond. A stallholder shouted for help and called 122, and a customer saw that she was not breathing normally and started chest compressions at once, hands only because he was not trained in rescue breaths. Someone else brought an AED from a nearby government office. The device advised a shock; everyone stood clear and the shock was given, and compressions restarted at once until the ambulance team arrived and took over. The doctors said later that early CPR and the AED had given her a real chance."},{id:16,icon:"DoorOpen",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Surviving a fire at home",subtitle:"Smoke alarms, the family escape plan and what to do if you are trapped",summary:"A fire in a home can spread very quickly: once the smoke alarm sounds, you may have as little as two minutes to get out safely. Working smoke alarms, an escape plan that the whole family has practiced and a few simple rules save lives: get out, stay out, close the doors behind you and call 115 from outside.",blocks:[{heading:"Smoke alarms: the early warning",body:`A smoke alarm can wake you when there is smoke, even in the middle of the night.
• Install smoke alarms in every bedroom, outside the sleeping areas and on every floor of the home, on the ceiling or high on a wall, following the manufacturer's instructions.
• Keep them away from the cooking stove and the bathroom door, so that cooking fumes and steam do not set them off.
• Test every alarm once a month with its test button.
• Change replaceable batteries at least once a year, and at once if the alarm keeps giving a short chirp (a low battery).
• Replace the whole alarm when it is 10 years old, or earlier if the manufacturer's instructions say so.
• Never remove the battery or switch an alarm off because cooking sets it off.`},{heading:"The family escape plan",body:`Make the plan together and practice it with everyone who lives in the home.
1. Draw a simple map of the home that shows every door and window.
2. Find two ways out of every room, if possible: the door and a second way, such as a window that opens.
3. Keep corridors, stairs and exits clear. Doors and window bars on the way out must open quickly from the inside; keep any key in a fixed place next to the door.
4. Choose a meeting point outside, in front of the home and a safe distance away, such as a tree or an electricity pole, where everyone is counted.
5. Decide who will help children, older people and people with disabilities to get out.
6. Practice the plan at least twice a year, at night and in the daytime.`},{heading:"When the alarm sounds: get out and stay out",body:`• Shout "Fire!" to warn everyone and leave at once by the nearest safe way. Do not stop to collect belongings.
• If there is smoke, get low and go under it to your way out: the cleaner air is near the floor.
• Before you open a closed door, feel the door and its handle with the back of your hand. If it is hot, or smoke is coming in around it, keep it closed and use your second way out.
• Close the doors behind you as you leave: a closed door slows the spread of smoke, heat and fire.
• Use the stairs, never the elevator.
• Go to the meeting point and call 115 from outside. Give the address and a nearby landmark first.
• Never go back inside for people, pets or belongings. If anyone is missing, tell the firefighters at once where they may be.`},{heading:"If you are trapped: close the door, seal it, call and signal",body:`If smoke or fire blocks both ways out:
1. Stay in a room with a window and close the door between you and the fire.
2. Put towels, blankets or clothes around the door and over air vents to keep the smoke out.
3. Call 115 and say exactly where you are: the address, the floor and the room. Do not hang up until you are told to.
4. Signal at the window with a light-colored cloth or a flashlight, so that the firefighters can see you. If you open the window for air, close it again if smoke comes in.
5. Stay low and wait for the firefighters. Do not jump from an upper floor.`},{heading:"Clothes on fire and burns",body:`• If your clothes catch fire: stop, drop to the ground, cover your face with your hands and roll over and over until the flames are out. Do not run.
• If someone cannot drop and roll, put out the flames with a heavy blanket or coat.
• Cool the burn at once under cool running water for 20 minutes. Do not put ice, butter, toothpaste or creams on it, and do not burst blisters.
• Remove rings, watches and clothing near the burn, but not anything stuck to the skin. Then lay clean plastic wrap or a clean cloth loosely over it.
• Call 122 if the burn is large or deep, is on the face, hands, feet or genitals, was caused by electricity or a chemical, or if the person has trouble breathing.`}],tips:["At night, close the doors of the bedrooms and the kitchen: a closed door slows the spread of smoke and fire.","Keep a flashlight and your phone next to your bed.","Make sure your house number or a clear landmark can be seen from the street, day and night.","Write 115, 122 and your full address with a landmark in a place where everyone can see them.","Teach children the escape plan and the meeting point: they get out, tell an adult and never try to fight a fire."],warnings:["Never go back into a burning building, not even for a family member: tell the firefighters where the person is.","Do not walk upright through smoke, and never open a door that feels hot.","Never use the elevator in a fire."],activity:{prompt:"At night the smoke alarm wakes you. You touch your bedroom door with the back of your hand and it is hot. Your bedroom is on the third floor and has a window. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I open the door quickly and run through the corridor to the stairs.",correct:!1,feedback:"A hot door means fire or very hot smoke on the other side; opening it can let them into your room. Keep it closed and use your second way out or wait at the window."},{text:"I keep the door closed, seal the gaps with towels or clothes, call 115 with the address, floor and room, and signal at the window with a light-colored cloth.",correct:!0,feedback:"The closed, sealed door protects you from smoke and fire, and 115 needs your exact location to reach you quickly."},{text:"I jump from the window at once, before the smoke reaches the room.",correct:!1,feedback:"A jump from the third floor can kill or badly injure you. Seal the door, call 115 and signal at the window: the firefighters can reach you there."},{text:"I hide under the bed and wait quietly for the smoke to pass.",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke does not pass by itself, and the firefighters must be able to find you. Stay by the window, signal and tell 115 where you are."}]},quiz:[{question:"Where should smoke alarms be installed in a home?",options:[{text:"Only in the kitchen, directly above the cooking stove",correct:!1},{text:"In every bedroom, outside the sleeping areas and on every floor",correct:!0},{text:"Only in the living room, next to the front door, where everyone can hear them",correct:!1},{text:"Inside a cupboard, where dust and children cannot reach them",correct:!1}],explanation:"Smoke alarms belong in every bedroom, outside the sleeping areas and on every floor. Directly above the stove, cooking fumes set them off and people are tempted to switch them off."},{question:"How often should you test each smoke alarm with its test button?",options:[{text:"Once every five years",correct:!1},{text:"Once a month",correct:!0},{text:"Only when you change the battery",correct:!1},{text:"Never: testing wears the alarm out",correct:!1}],explanation:"Test every alarm once a month. Change replaceable batteries at least once a year, and replace the whole alarm at 10 years or as the manufacturer's instructions say."},{question:"You have escaped from the burning house, but a family member is still missing. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stay outside, call 115 and tell the firefighters where the person may be",correct:!0},{text:"Go back inside quickly to look for them before the firefighters arrive at the house",correct:!1},{text:"Wait silently at the meeting point until the fire has gone out",correct:!1},{text:"Send a child back in, because children are small and quick",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never go back into a burning building. Firefighters have protective clothing and breathing equipment; telling them at once where the person may be is the fastest help."},{question:"Smoke and fire block both ways out of your room. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Open the door and the window wide to let the smoke out of the room",correct:!1},{text:"Jump from the window at once, even from an upper floor",correct:!1},{text:"Close the door, seal the gaps and call 115 with your location",correct:!0},{text:"Hide in the wardrobe, where the smoke cannot reach you",correct:!1}],explanation:"A closed and sealed door keeps smoke out longer. Tell 115 the address, floor and room, then signal at the window so that the firefighters can find you."},{question:"Your clothes catch fire. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Run outside quickly so that the wind blows the flames out",correct:!1},{text:"Stop, drop, cover your face and roll until the flames are out",correct:!0},{text:"Stand still and wait for someone to bring water from the kitchen",correct:!1},{text:"Take off the burning clothes while running to the bathroom",correct:!1}],explanation:"Stop, drop and roll smothers the flames; do not run. Afterwards, cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes and call 122 for a serious burn."},{question:"The smoke alarm goes off every time you cook. What is the right fix?",options:[{text:"Keep the alarm working and move it farther from the stove, following the maker’s instructions",correct:!0},{text:"Take the battery out while you cook and put it back afterward",correct:!1},{text:"Cover the alarm with a plastic bag while you cook so that smoke cannot reach it",correct:!1},{text:"Switch it off for good, because the bedroom doors are closed at night",correct:!1}],explanation:"An alarm that does not work wakes nobody. Do not remove the battery or cover the alarm; place it away from the cooking stove as the maker’s instructions say. Closed doors help, but they do not replace the alarm."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_home_escape_plan.webp",title:"A family practices its home escape plan",description:"Parents and children leave the home low under the smoke, close the door behind them and meet at the meeting point outside."}],steps:[{title:"Shout “Fire!” and get out at once",detail:"Warn everyone and leave by the nearest safe way, following the family plan. Do not stop for belongings, and help children and older people as the plan says.",critical:!0},{title:"Stay low and test doors",detail:"Go under the smoke to your way out. Before you open any closed door, feel the door and its handle with the back of your hand; if it is hot, keep it closed and use your second way out.",critical:!0},{title:"Close doors behind you and use the stairs",detail:"A closed door slows the spread of smoke and fire. Never use the elevator."},{title:"Count everyone at the meeting point and call 115",detail:"Call 115 from outside: the address and a landmark first, then say whether everyone is out or someone is still inside."},{title:"Never go back inside",detail:"Not for people, pets or belongings. If someone is missing, tell the firefighters at once where they may be: the floor and the room.",critical:!0},{title:"If you are trapped: close, seal, call and signal",detail:"In a room with a window, close the door, seal the gaps around it with towels or clothes, call 115 with the address, floor and room, and signal at the window. Do not jump from an upper floor.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"In the middle of the night the smoke alarm in the hallway goes off and you smell smoke. Your two children are asleep in the next room and your mother is downstairs. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I shout “Fire!”, wake everyone, test doors with the back of my hand and get the children out as the plan says",correct:!0,feedback:"An early warning and a practiced plan save escape time, and testing doors stops you from opening a hot one."},{text:"I first go to see where the fire is, then wake the family",correct:!1,feedback:"Looking for the fire wastes escape time and can lead you into the smoke. Wake everyone first and get out."},{text:"I switch the alarm off, because it may just be cooking",correct:!1,feedback:"At night and with a smell of smoke, the alarm is a real warning. Switching it off takes the warning away from the family."},{text:"I collect the documents and gold first so that nothing burns",correct:!1,feedback:"Belongings can be replaced; lives cannot. Do not stop to collect things; wake everyone and get out."}]},{prompt:"Everyone has reached the meeting point except your mother. A neighbor says she may still be in her back room. Smoke is coming out of the downstairs windows. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I run back in through the smoke to bring her out",correct:!1,feedback:"Going back through the smoke can kill you too and makes the rescue harder. Firefighters have protective clothing and breathing apparatus (BA)."},{text:"I call 115, say at once which room my mother is in and stop anyone else from going back inside",correct:!0,feedback:"Exact information about where she is lets the firefighters reach her fast, and nobody else is put in danger."},{text:"I wait quietly; she may have left by another door",correct:!1,feedback:"Guessing is not enough: if someone is missing, the firefighters must know at once where they may be."},{text:"I send my teenage son in through the back window, because he is faster",correct:!1,feedback:"Speed gives no protection from smoke; you would put another person in danger. Let the firefighters do the rescue."}]}],realCase:"A family practiced their escape plan twice a year, once at night. One night the smoke alarm went off. The father felt the bedroom door with the back of his hand and found it hot, so he kept it closed, and everyone left through the ground-floor balcony window, the second way out in their plan. At the tree in front of the house, their meeting point, everyone was counted. The eldest daughter called 115 from outside and gave the address and a landmark first. Nobody went back for belongings. The firefighters stopped the fire in the sitting room."},{id:17,icon:"Fuel",accent:"#b45309",title:"Heaters, gas cylinders, carbon monoxide and generators",subtitle:"Safe heating, LPG cylinders, the invisible gas CO and generators at home and in the neighborhood",summary:"In winter and during power cuts, many homes use gas and kerosene heaters, LPG cylinders and generators. Used wrongly, they can cause fires, explosions and carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning; CO is a gas that you cannot see or smell. This lesson gives the safe rules for each of them and explains what to do if you smell gas or if someone shows signs of CO poisoning.",blocks:[{heading:"Gas and kerosene heaters",body:`• Use a heater in good condition and follow the manufacturer's instructions. Use only the fuel the manufacturer names: never put gasoline in a kerosene heater.
• Keep anything that can burn (curtains, bedding, clothes, furniture) at least 1 meter (3 feet) from the heater, and keep children away from it.
• Put the heater on a flat, firm floor, away from doors and from places where people walk.
• A heater without a flue (a pipe that takes the exhaust outside) releases its exhaust into the room: keep the room door open to the rest of the house and open a window a little for fresh air.
• Turn portable gas and kerosene heaters off before you sleep and when you leave the room.
• Refuel a kerosene heater outside, and only after it has been turned off and has cooled down.
• If a heater flares up or catches fire, do not carry it: switch it off with its shut-off switch if that is safe; otherwise get everyone out, close the door and call 115.`},{heading:"LPG cylinders",body:`• Use cylinders, regulators and hoses in good condition; do not use any part that is damaged, cracked or leaking.
• Keep the cylinder upright, in a ventilated place, away from heaters, stoves, direct sun and other heat.
• LPG is heavier than air: leaking gas collects near the floor and in low places. Do not keep cylinders in a basement, and do not store spare cylinders in bedrooms or near the way out.
• Have the regulator, hose and connections fitted and checked by a qualified technician, and replace a hose that is cracked, hard or old.
• Never look for a leak with a flame, a match or a lighter. A technician checks connections with soapy water: bubbles show a leak.
• Close the cylinder valve when you are not using the gas.`},{heading:"If you smell gas",body:`1. Do not light any flame and do not switch lights or appliances on or off. Do not use a phone inside, and do not start a generator or a car nearby.
2. Open doors and windows on your way out, if you can do it quickly.
3. Close the gas valve only if it is close to you and you can reach it without going into the area where the gas has collected and without making any spark; otherwise do not go near it.
4. Get everyone out and warn the neighbors.
5. Call 115 from outside, and do not go back in until the specialists say it is safe.`},{heading:"Carbon monoxide (CO): the invisible poison",body:`Carbon monoxide (CO) is made when fuel burns: in heaters, stoves, water heaters, generators, car engines and charcoal. It has no color and no smell.
• Signs of CO poisoning: headache, dizziness, weakness, nausea or vomiting, chest pain and confusion. If several people in the same place feel ill at the same time, think of CO.
• People who are asleep can die from CO before they notice any sign.
• Install a CO alarm near the sleeping areas, test it every month and follow the manufacturer's instructions.
• Have heaters, water heaters and chimneys checked every year by a qualified technician.
• Never heat the home with a gas oven, and never bring a charcoal brazier or a barbecue indoors.`},{heading:"If the CO alarm sounds or people feel ill",body:`• Get everyone out into fresh air at once; turn the heater off on the way only if it is quick and safe.
• From outside, call 122 for anyone with signs, even if they start to feel better, and call 115.
• Do not go back inside to rescue anyone: wait for the trained teams.
• Nobody goes back in until the specialists say it is safe and the appliance has been checked.`},{heading:"Generators at home and in the neighborhood",body:`• Run a portable generator only outdoors, at least 6 meters (20 feet) from windows, doors and vents, with the exhaust pointing away from buildings. Never run it inside a house, garage, basement, shop or storeroom, or on a closed balcony, even with the doors and windows open.
• Before refueling, turn the generator off and let it cool down: fuel spilled on hot parts can catch fire. Keep fuel outside the living areas in a proper closed container.
• Never plug a generator into a wall outlet or connect it to the house wiring (backfeeding): it can kill neighbors and electricity workers. Only a qualified electrician may connect a generator, through a transfer switch.
• Neighborhood generators: do not touch their cables or switches yourself. Report loose or damaged cables, sparks or a smell of burning to the operator, and call 115 in an emergency. Only a qualified electrician should fit the switch that changes your home between the neighborhood generator and the public grid.`}],tips:["Install a smoke alarm and a CO alarm in the home and test them every month.","Have heaters and gas appliances checked every year by a qualified technician.","Keep a safe zone of at least 1 meter (3 feet) around heaters, especially for children.","Close the cylinder valve when you are not using the gas.","Keep heater fuel and generator fuel outside the living areas, in proper closed containers away from heat."],warnings:["Never run a generator, a charcoal brazier or a barbecue indoors, even with the doors and windows open.","Never look for a gas leak with a flame.","Never plug a generator into a wall outlet or connect it to the house wiring."],activity:{prompt:"On a cold night, the kerosene heater has been burning for hours in the closed sitting room. Your mother and your son both have a headache and feel dizzy and sick. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I give them headache tablets, open the window a little and we stay in the warm room.",correct:!1,feedback:"Tablets do not remove CO, and a small opening is not enough once people have signs. Everyone must get out into fresh air, and you must call 122."},{text:"I get everyone out into fresh air at once, turn the heater off on the way only if it is quick and safe, and call 122 and 115 from outside; nobody goes back in until it is checked.",correct:!0,feedback:"Two people with the same signs near a burning heater point to CO poisoning: fresh air and a call to 122 come first, and the heater must be checked before it is used again."},{text:"I turn the heater off and let them sleep, because rest helps a headache.",correct:!1,feedback:"People who are asleep can die from CO without waking up. Get everyone out into fresh air and call 122."},{text:"I add more kerosene so that the heater burns better and makes less smoke.",correct:!1,feedback:"More fuel does not stop CO. The people need fresh air and medical help now: get them out and call 122."}]},quiz:[{question:"How far should curtains, bedding and other things that burn be kept from a heater?",options:[{text:"About 10 centimeters is enough",correct:!1},{text:"At least 1 meter (3 feet)",correct:!0},{text:"Any distance, if the heater is new",correct:!1},{text:"No distance, if the heater is on low",correct:!1}],explanation:"Keep anything that can burn at least 1 meter (3 feet) from heaters, and keep children away from them too."},{question:"What is the safe way to check an LPG hose and its connections for a leak?",options:[{text:"Hold a lit match near the connection and watch for a flame",correct:!1},{text:"Turn on the stove and listen to the sound of the flame",correct:!1},{text:"A qualified technician checks them with soapy water",correct:!0},{text:"Smell the hose closely with the cylinder valve fully open",correct:!1}],explanation:"A flame can ignite leaking gas and cause a fire or an explosion. A qualified technician checks connections with soapy water: bubbles show a leak."},{question:"Which of these can be a sign of carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning?",options:[{text:"A strong smell of gas coming from the heater",correct:!1},{text:"Headache and dizziness in several people at once",correct:!0},{text:"Blue smoke that you can see clearly near the floor",correct:!1},{text:"A skin rash that appears about a week later",correct:!1}],explanation:"CO has no color and no smell. Headache, dizziness, weakness and nausea, especially in several people at once near fuel-burning equipment, are warning signs. A smell of gas means a gas leak, not CO."},{question:"Where can a portable generator be run safely?",options:[{text:"Outdoors, at least 6 meters (20 feet) from windows, doors and vents",correct:!0},{text:"In the garage, as long as the big garage door stays wide open",correct:!1},{text:"On a closed balcony, with the window next to it open for fresh air",correct:!1},{text:"Inside the house, next to an open window, with a fan blowing the smoke out",correct:!1}],explanation:"Generator exhaust contains CO, which can kill within minutes indoors. Run a generator only outdoors, at least 6 meters (20 feet) from windows, doors and vents, with the exhaust pointing away from buildings."},{question:"How may a generator be connected to the electrical wiring of a home?",options:[{text:"By plugging it into a wall outlet with a cable that has two plugs",correct:!1},{text:"Only by a qualified electrician, through a transfer switch",correct:!0},{text:"By the owner, directly to the fuse box, during a power cut",correct:!1},{text:"By any neighbor who has connected a generator before",correct:!1}],explanation:"Plugging a generator into a wall outlet or the house wiring (backfeeding) can electrocute neighbors and electricity workers. Only a qualified electrician may connect it, through a transfer switch."},{question:"You come home and smell gas in the kitchen. The cylinder valve is next to the stove, in the room where the smell is strongest. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I do not go to the valve; with no switches or phone inside, I get everyone out and call 115 from outside",correct:!0},{text:"I go in quickly, close the valve and switch on the kitchen fan to clear the gas",correct:!1},{text:"I switch on the light to see where the gas is leaking, then close the valve",correct:!1},{text:"I strike a match near the hose to find where the gas is escaping",correct:!1}],explanation:"The gas valve is closed only if it is close and you can reach it without going into the area where the gas has collected. Any switch, light, fan or match can make a spark and ignite the gas. Get out and call 115 from outside."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_heaters_gas_generators.webp",title:"Safe heating and a generator outdoors",description:"A heater with a clear safe zone around it, an upright gas cylinder with a checked hose, a CO alarm on the wall and a generator running outdoors, away from the windows."}],steps:[{title:"CO symptoms or a CO alarm: get everyone out at once",detail:"Headache, dizziness, nausea or weakness in several people near a running heater or generator: get everyone out into fresh air. Turn the heater off on the way only if it is quick and safe.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 122 and 115 from outside",detail:"Call 122 for anyone with symptoms, even if they feel better in the fresh air, and call 115.",critical:!0},{title:"Nobody goes back inside",detail:"Do not go back in to rescue anyone: wait for the trained teams. Nobody goes in until the specialists say it is safe and the appliance has been checked.",critical:!0},{title:"Generators outdoors only",detail:"At least 6 meters (20 feet) from windows, doors and vents, with the exhaust pointing away from buildings. Never inside a house, garage or basement or on a closed balcony, even with the doors and windows open. Never plug it into a wall outlet.",critical:!0},{title:"Use heaters safely",detail:"Keep things that burn at least 1 meter (3 feet) from the heater, turn portable heaters off before you sleep, refuel a kerosene heater only outside after it has cooled, and fit a CO alarm near the sleeping areas."},{title:"If you smell gas",detail:"No flames, switches or phone inside. Close the gas valve only if it is close to you and you can reach it without going into the area where the gas has collected and without any spark; otherwise do not go near it. Get everyone out and call 115 from outside.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"During a long power cut, a neighbor lends you his portable generator. Your brother wants to put it in the garage with the door half open so that it is not stolen. What do you do?",options:[{text:"We put it in the garage but leave the door fully open",correct:!1,feedback:"An open door does not clear CO from a garage, and it can seep into the house. A generator never runs inside a garage."},{text:"We run it outside, at least 6 meters from windows, doors and vents, exhaust pointing away, and we do not plug it into a wall outlet",correct:!0,feedback:"This is the only safe way: CO stays away from the home, and not plugging into the wall protects electricity workers and neighbors."},{text:"We put it on the closed balcony, because it is safe from thieves",correct:!1,feedback:"Exhaust from a generator on a balcony carries CO through windows and doors into your home and your neighbors’ homes."},{text:"We put it in the kitchen next to an open window with a fan running",correct:!1,feedback:"A window and a fan do not clear colorless, odorless CO. A generator runs outdoors only."}]},{prompt:"The generator is outside, but the wind is blowing its exhaust toward a window. In the morning your daughter wakes with a headache and nausea, and your wife feels dizzy. There is no CO alarm in the home. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I give them painkillers and we have breakfast to see whether they improve",correct:!1,feedback:"Two people with the same symptoms near a fuel-burning device point to CO. Painkillers do not remove CO; get everyone out and call 122."},{text:"I get everyone out into fresh air at once, call 122 and 115 from outside, and nobody goes back in until it is checked",correct:!0,feedback:"With CO symptoms, getting out and calling come first. Afterward, the generator must be moved farther from the windows and a CO alarm fitted."},{text:"I open the window and we stay inside, because the generator is outside",correct:!1,feedback:"The generator is outside, but its exhaust has got in. Once there are symptoms, opening a window is not enough: get everyone out."},{text:"I first go and switch off the generator and check its exhaust, then come back to them",correct:!1,feedback:"Get the people out of the CO first; the generator can wait. Call 122 and 115."}]}],realCase:"On a cold night, a family brought a charcoal brazier into the bedroom to save fuel, and the windows were closed. In the morning the grandmother and a small child were very drowsy and had headaches. A neighbor who had come to visit noticed that several people were ill together and thought of CO. He got everyone out into fresh air at once, called 122 and 115 from outside, and nobody went back in. The team checked the house and the doctors examined everyone. Afterward the family fitted a CO alarm and never brought a brazier indoors again."},{id:18,icon:"Building",accent:"#4f46e5",title:"Living in apartments and high-rise buildings",subtitle:"Exits, stairs, fire doors and what to do when there is a fire in your building",summary:"In an apartment building, a fire in one apartment can put everyone in danger: smoke spreads through corridors and stairs, and the way out is longer. Knowing your exits, keeping the stairs and fire doors working and knowing when to leave and when to stay in your apartment can save your family. Always follow your building's plan and the instructions of Civil Defense.",blocks:[{heading:"Know your building before a fire",body:`• Learn where all the exit stairs on your floor are, in case the nearest one is blocked by fire or smoke. Count the doors from your apartment to each stairway, so that you can find it in the dark or in smoke.
• Ask the building management what fire safety the building has (fire alarm, extinguishers, emergency lighting, evacuation plan) and how residents are told to respond to an alarm.
• Make an escape plan for your apartment and agree on a meeting point outside the building.
• Find out which neighbors on your floor will need help to go down the stairs, such as older people and people with disabilities, and agree with the management and the neighbors who will help them.`},{heading:"Stairs, corridors and fire doors",body:`• Never store anything in corridors, on the stairs or in front of exit doors: no furniture, shoes, bicycles, gas cylinders or fuel.
• Fire doors and stair doors must close by themselves and stay closed. Never wedge them open or tie them back, and report damaged doors, closers or seals to the management.
• Exit and stairway doors must not be locked or blocked, and must open from the inside without a key.
• Do not run generator cables, extension cords or hoses through stair doors or fire doors: they stop the doors from closing.`},{heading:"Use the stairs, never the elevator",body:`In a fire, use the stairs to get out. Do not use the elevator unless the firefighters tell you to: it can stop and trap you between floors in the smoke.

If someone cannot use the stairs, do not take them into the elevator. Tell 115 and the firefighters at once exactly where the person is: the floor and the apartment number.`},{heading:"If the fire is in your apartment",body:`1. Warn everyone in the apartment and get out at once.
2. Close the apartment door behind you: the closed door keeps the smoke and fire inside.
3. Warn the neighbors: knock on their doors and shout "Fire!", and press the fire alarm if the building has one.
4. Go down by the stairs, not by the elevator.
5. Call 115 from outside: give the address, the floor and the apartment number, and go to the meeting point.`},{heading:"If the fire is elsewhere in the building",body:`• When the alarm sounds or you smell smoke, feel your apartment door with the back of your hand before you open it.
• If the door is cool and the corridor and stairs are clear, leave by the nearest stairway, close the doors behind you and go to the meeting point.
• If the door is hot, or the corridor or stairs are full of smoke, stay in your apartment: keep the door closed, put wet towels or sheets around it and over the vents, and call 115 with the address, the floor and the apartment number.
• Go to a window or balcony away from the smoke and signal with a light-colored cloth or a flashlight. Open a window a little for fresh air, but close it if smoke comes in.
• Listen to announcements and follow your building's plan and the instructions of Civil Defense and the firefighters: rescue from a tall building can take a long time.`},{heading:"Balconies, gas cylinders, generators and cables",body:`• Do not store spare gas cylinders, fuel, old furniture or rubbish on balconies. Where a gas cylinder is kept for cooking, keep it upright, away from heat and direct sun, with the valve closed when it is not in use.
• Never run a generator on a balcony: its exhaust carries CO into your home and your neighbors' homes through windows and doors.
• Generator and neighborhood power cables must be fitted by a qualified electrician. Report loose, damaged or hot cables in the building to the management.
• Put cigarettes out completely in a deep ashtray; never throw them from a balcony or a window.`}],tips:["Know at least two exit stairways from your floor.","Keep your keys, phone and a flashlight next to your bed, and take them with you when you leave.","Take part in the building's fire drills as if they were real.","Ask the management to check the fire alarm, emergency lighting, extinguishers and fire doors regularly.","Install smoke alarms inside your apartment and test them every month."],warnings:["Never use the elevator during a fire unless the firefighters tell you to.","Never wedge a fire door or a stair door open.","Do not go into a corridor or stairway full of smoke: stay in your apartment, seal the door and call 115."],activity:{prompt:"You live on the seventh floor. The fire alarm sounds. Your apartment door feels cool, but when you open it a little, the corridor is full of thick smoke. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I take the elevator down quickly, because it is faster than seven floors of stairs.",correct:!1,feedback:"Never use the elevator in a fire: it can stop and trap you in the smoke. Stay in your apartment and call 115."},{text:"I run through the smoke to the stairs while holding my breath.",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke is poisonous, and you can lose your way in it. Close the door, seal the gaps and call 115 from your apartment."},{text:"I close the door again, seal the gaps with wet towels, call 115 with the address, floor and apartment number, and signal from a window away from the smoke.",correct:!0,feedback:"Thick smoke in the corridor blocks the way out. Your closed apartment door protects you while 115 sends help to your floor."},{text:"I leave the apartment door open so that I can hear the firefighters arrive.",correct:!1,feedback:"An open door lets smoke into the apartment. Keep it closed and sealed, and tell 115 where you are."}]},quiz:[{question:"Why must fire doors and stair doors in a building stay closed?",options:[{text:"They keep the corridors warm in winter and save electricity for everyone",correct:!1},{text:"They slow the spread of smoke and fire and protect the escape stairs",correct:!0},{text:"They stop strangers and children from walking between the floors at night",correct:!1},{text:"They are mainly for decoration, so it does not matter if they stay open",correct:!1}],explanation:"A closed fire door slows smoke, heat and fire and keeps the stairway usable for escape. Never wedge it open, and report a door that does not close by itself."},{question:"The fire is inside your apartment. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Get out, close the door behind you, warn the neighbors and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Leave the apartment door open so that the smoke can escape into the corridor",correct:!1},{text:"Stay inside and try to fight a large fire with buckets of water",correct:!1},{text:"Take the elevator down, then ask the building guard to deal with it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Closing the door of the burning apartment keeps smoke and fire out of the corridor and stairs that your neighbors need. Warn them, use the stairs and call 115 from outside."},{question:"What should be done with shoes, bicycles or gas cylinders left in the corridor or on the stairs?",options:[{text:"Keep them neatly against one wall so that people can still pass",correct:!1},{text:"Leave them there in summer and move them only in winter",correct:!1},{text:"Cover them with a cloth so that they do not catch fire",correct:!1},{text:"Remove them: corridors and stairs must stay clear for escape",correct:!0}],explanation:"In a fire, people escape through smoke and darkness, and anything in a corridor or on the stairs can block them or burn. Nothing is stored in corridors, on stairs or in front of exits."},{question:"When may you use the elevator during a fire in your building?",options:[{text:"When you live above the fifth floor",correct:!1},{text:"Only when the firefighters tell you to use it",correct:!0},{text:"When the smoke is only on the lower floors",correct:!1},{text:"When you are carrying children or heavy belongings",correct:!1}],explanation:"Use the stairs. An elevator can stop and trap people in smoke; the firefighters decide whether an elevator may be used."},{question:"Smoke in the corridor traps you in your apartment. What do you tell 115?",options:[{text:"Only your name, because they will find the building themselves",correct:!1},{text:"The address, floor, apartment number and how many people are with you",correct:!0},{text:"Only that there is smoke; they will call you back later to ask for the details",correct:!1},{text:"Nothing yet: you wait quietly until someone knocks on the door",correct:!1}],explanation:"The firefighters need to know exactly where you are: the address, floor and apartment, and how many people are there. Do not hang up until you are told to."},{question:"Neighbors keep the stair door wedged open with a brick so that they can pass easily. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I remove the brick so that the door closes by itself, and report it to the management",correct:!0},{text:"I leave it, because an open door makes escape faster in a fire",correct:!1},{text:"I tie the door back with wire so that the brick does not fall",correct:!1},{text:"I lock the door at night so that nobody wedges it again",correct:!1}],explanation:"Stair doors and fire doors must close by themselves so that smoke cannot get into the stairway. Exit doors must not be locked and must open from the inside without a key."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_apartment_fire_safety.webp",title:"Escaping safely from an apartment building",description:"Residents go down the stairs past a closed fire door, while a family in another apartment waits by the window and signals with a light-colored cloth."}],steps:[{title:"Test the apartment door before you open it",detail:"When the alarm sounds or you smell smoke, feel the door and its handle with the back of your hand. If it is hot, do not open it.",critical:!0},{title:"The way is clear: leave by the stairs",detail:"If the door is cool and the corridor and stairs are clear, leave by the nearest exit stairway and close the doors behind you. Never use the elevator unless the firefighters tell you to.",critical:!0},{title:"Warn the neighbors on your way",detail:"Knock on their doors, shout “Fire!” and press the building’s fire alarm if there is one. If someone cannot go down the stairs, tell 115 at once which floor and apartment they are in."},{title:"Hot door or smoky corridor: stay in",detail:"Keep the door closed, put wet towels or sheets around it and over the vents, and call 115 with the address, the floor and the apartment number.",critical:!0},{title:"Signal and follow instructions",detail:"Signal from a window or balcony away from the smoke with a light-colored cloth or a flashlight. Listen to announcements and follow the instructions of Civil Defense and the firefighters."},{title:"Outside: meeting point and information for the firefighters",detail:"Go to the meeting point and count your family. Tell the firefighters who is still inside the building and on which floor and apartment. Do not go back in until they say it is safe."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"You live on the fifth floor. The fire alarm sounds and you smell smoke. Your door is cool and the corridor is clear, but your elderly neighbor across the hall uses a walker and cannot manage the stairs quickly. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I take her down in the elevator, because it is faster than the stairs",correct:!1,feedback:"The elevator can stop during a fire and trap you both in the smoke. Do not take her in the elevator."},{text:"I warn her and help her down the stairs if she can manage with me; if she cannot, her door is closed and I tell 115 her floor and apartment number at once",correct:!0,feedback:"Helping her down the stairs is best if she can manage; if not, a closed door protects her from smoke and 115 knows where to find her."},{text:"I leave her; it is the building management’s job, not mine",correct:!1,feedback:"Nobody may know she is there. Your warning and telling 115 where she is can save her life."},{text:"I wedge the stair door open with a chair so that she can pass easily later",correct:!1,feedback:"A wedged stair door lets smoke into the stairway and blocks everyone’s way out. Fire doors must stay closed."}]},{prompt:"You are going down the stairs. At the third floor, the stairway fills with thick smoke rising from below. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I hold my breath and hurry down through the smoke",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke is toxic and you can lose your way or pass out in it. Do not go into a stairway full of smoke."},{text:"I leave the stairway through a stair door onto a clear floor, close the door and use another stairway; if no way is clear, I go into a closed room away from the smoke and call 115",correct:!0,feedback:"Knowing all the exit stairways gives you a second way out, and a closed door keeps the smoke away from you until 115 arrives."},{text:"I take the elevator from this floor to get past the smoke",correct:!1,feedback:"The elevator can take you to the fire floor or stop between floors. Do not use it."},{text:"I stay on the stairs, open the stairway window and wait",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke rises fast in a stairway; staying there puts you in its path. Leave the stairs and find another way or a closed room."}]}],realCase:"A fire started in a ground-floor store of a tall building, and smoke went into the main stairway. A family on the eighth floor tested their door and saw that the corridor was full of smoke. They stayed in the apartment, sealed the gaps around the door with wet towels, called 115 with the address, floor and apartment number, and signaled with a flashlight from a balcony away from the smoke. A neighbor went into the smoky stairway and collapsed on a lower floor, struggling to breathe; the firefighters found him and brought him out. The family was later brought out safely."},{id:19,icon:"Leaf",accent:"#c2410c",title:"Crop, stubble and vegetation fires",subtitle:"Preventing fires at harvest time and staying safe when a field fire comes near",summary:"In summer, dry wheat and barley fields, stubble and grass catch fire easily, and the wind can spread a field fire very quickly. These fires are often started by people: a cigarette, a spark or a hot exhaust from machinery, or a fire lit to burn stubble. This lesson explains how to prevent them and what to do when a fire comes near your field, your village or your home.",blocks:[{heading:"Why field fires are dangerous",body:`• Dry crops, stubble and grass catch fire easily, especially on hot, dry and windy days.
• The wind pushes the fire forward and carries burning embers ahead of the flames, where they can start new fires.
• Fire spreads fastest uphill and in the direction the wind is blowing.
• Smoke hides the way, and a fast fire can surround people, animals and vehicles in the field.`},{heading:"Prevention at harvest time",body:`• Before and during the harvest, clean chaff, straw and dust off harvesters and tractors every day, especially around the engine, the exhaust and the turbocharger: dust and straw that reach these hot parts can catch fire.
• Check machines for worn bearings, slipping belts and fuel or oil leaks, and repair faults before you start work.
• Keep water and a suitable fire extinguisher on the harvester and with the team.
• Plow or clear a firebreak (a strip of bare soil with nothing that can burn) around the field and between the fields and the village.
• Stop working with machines when it is very hot, dry and windy.
• Do not drive or park cars and tractors on dry grass or stubble: a hot exhaust can set it on fire.`},{heading:"Do not light fires near fields",body:`• Burning stubble or rubbish in the open can escape with the wind into neighboring fields, orchards and villages. Before you burn anything, check with Civil Defense and the local authorities whether and when burning is allowed; never burn on a dry or windy day, and never leave a fire alone.
• Never throw cigarettes from cars or drop them in fields or grass, and do not smoke while you work in the harvest.
• Do not light cooking fires or barbecues near crops or dry grass, and put any fire out completely with water before you leave.
• Take glass bottles and rubbish home: glass left in a field can focus sunlight on dry grass.`},{heading:"When a fire comes near",body:`• Leave early. Do not wait until the fire is close: smoke and flames can block the road. If the authorities tell you to leave, go at once.
• Only try to put out a very small fire that has just started, with water or soil, if your way out is behind you and the wind is not blowing the fire toward you. If it does not go out at once, leave.
• Move out of the fire's path: never in the direction the wind is pushing it and never uphill above it. Use roads and paths, and go toward ground with little or nothing to burn, such as plowed land.
• Call 115 from a safe place and give the exact location (the village, the road, the field or a landmark) and say whether the fire is moving toward houses or people.
• Warn other farmers and the village by phone, not by running in front of the fire.`},{heading:"Protecting villages and homes",body:`• Keep the area around houses, barns and animal pens clear of dry grass, straw, firewood and rubbish for at least 9 meters (30 feet).
• Keep stacks of straw and hay away from houses and roads, and do not store fuel next to them.
• Agree in the village on who calls 115, where people gather and how older people, children and animals will be moved.
• Keep roads and gates clear so that fire engines can reach the village and the fields.
• When a fire is near, follow the instructions of Civil Defense and do not return until they say it is safe.`}],tips:["Clean harvesters and tractors every day of the harvest, especially around the engine and the exhaust.","Keep water and a fire extinguisher on every harvester.","Watch the weather: hot, dry and windy days are the most dangerous.","Save 115 in your phone and learn a clear landmark for each of your fields.","Tell the farmers around you as soon as you see smoke."],warnings:["Never throw a cigarette into dry grass or stubble, or from a car window.","Never try to stop a large field fire yourself: leave and call 115.","Never run uphill or in the direction the wind is blowing to escape a fire."],activity:{prompt:"You are harvesting wheat with a harvester on a hot, windy afternoon. You see smoke and small flames in the stubble behind the machine, and the wind is blowing them toward the next field. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop, move the machine and the people out of the fire's path, call 115 with the exact location and warn the nearby farmers; I use water or the extinguisher only while the fire is still very small and my way out is clear.",correct:!0,feedback:"A field fire grows fast in the wind. Getting people out of its path and calling 115 early come first; only a very small fire may be attacked, and only with a safe way out."},{text:"I drive the harvester through the flames to reach the other side of the field.",correct:!1,feedback:"Driving through fire can trap you and set the machine on fire. Move out of the fire's path and call 115."},{text:"I keep harvesting, because the stubble fire will burn itself out.",correct:!1,feedback:"The wind can carry the fire into crops, orchards and villages. Stop, get people out of its path and call 115 at once."},{text:"I run ahead of the fire, in the direction the wind is blowing, to warn the next farm.",correct:!1,feedback:"Running where the wind is pushing the fire puts you in its path. Warn others by phone and move out of the fire's path."}]},quiz:[{question:"Which part of a harvester is the most important to keep clean of chaff and dust?",options:[{text:"The driver's seat, the cabin windows and the mirrors",correct:!1},{text:"The engine area, around the hot exhaust and turbocharger",correct:!0},{text:"The tires and the steps that lead up to the cabin door",correct:!1},{text:"The grain tank, after it has been emptied at the end of the day",correct:!1}],explanation:"Chaff and dust blown onto the hot exhaust or turbocharger can catch fire. Clean the engine area every day of the harvest, and more often on hot days."},{question:"A field fire is coming toward you, pushed by the wind. Which way do you escape?",options:[{text:"Straight ahead in the direction the wind is blowing, as fast as possible",correct:!1},{text:"Up the nearest hill, so that you can see where the fire is going",correct:!1},{text:"Out of its path along a road, not uphill and not with the wind",correct:!0},{text:"Into the standing wheat, where the tall crop hides you from the smoke",correct:!1}],explanation:"Fire runs fastest with the wind and uphill. Move out of its path along roads or paths toward ground with little to burn, and call 115."},{question:"What should you tell 115 when you report a field fire?",options:[{text:"Only that there is smoke somewhere, and then hang up quickly to save time",correct:!1},{text:"The exact place (village, road or landmark) and where the fire is heading",correct:!0},{text:"The name of the field's owner, the type of crop and how much it is worth",correct:!1},{text:"Nothing more, because Civil Defense can see every field fire from far away",correct:!1}],explanation:"Civil Defense needs to find the fire quickly: give the village, the road and a landmark, say whether houses or people are in danger, and do not hang up until you are told to."},{question:"Why is burning stubble dangerous?",options:[{text:"Stubble does not burn well, so the fire always goes out at once by itself",correct:!1},{text:"It is only dangerous at night, when people cannot see the smoke",correct:!1},{text:"The fire can escape with the wind into nearby fields and villages",correct:!0},{text:"It is dangerous only for the person who lights it, not for others",correct:!1}],explanation:"Fires lit for farming can spread out of control, especially on dry and windy days. Check with Civil Defense and the local authorities before any burning, and never leave a fire alone."},{question:"Which of these can start a field fire?",options:[{text:"A cigarette thrown from a car window into dry grass",correct:!0},{text:"A green tree standing in the shade at the edge of the field",correct:!1},{text:"Rain falling on the stubble after the harvest",correct:!1},{text:"A closed water tank parked beside the field",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cigarettes, sparks and hot exhausts from vehicles and machines, and fires lit in the open, start many field fires. Never throw cigarettes out, and do not park on dry grass."},{question:"After the harvest you want to burn the stubble in your field. What is the right first step?",options:[{text:"Ask Civil Defense and the local authorities whether and when burning is allowed",correct:!0},{text:"Burn it on a windy morning so that the smoke blows away from the village faster",correct:!1},{text:"Light it in several places at once so that the burning is over quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Light it in the evening and leave, because stubble fires go out by themselves at night",correct:!1}],explanation:"Burning stubble can escape with the wind into neighboring fields, orchards and villages. First ask whether and when it is allowed, never burn on a dry or windy day and never leave a fire alone."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_crop_fire_prevention.webp",title:"Preventing a fire in a wheat field",description:"A farmer cleans the engine area of a harvester next to a plowed firebreak, with a water tank and a fire extinguisher ready at the edge of a dry wheat field."}],steps:[{title:"Get out of the fire’s path early",detail:"Do not wait until the fire is close. Never go where the wind is pushing it and never uphill above it. Use roads and paths toward ground with little to burn, such as plowed land.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 115 from a safe place",detail:"Give the exact location: the village, the road, the field or a landmark, and say whether the fire is moving toward houses or people.",critical:!0},{title:"Warn others by phone",detail:"Warn nearby farmers and the village by phone, not by running in front of the fire."},{title:"Only a very small fire that has just started",detail:"With water or soil, if your way out is behind you and the wind is not blowing the fire toward you. If it does not go out at once, leave."},{title:"Move people and animals as the village plan says",detail:"Help older people, children and people with disabilities. Keep roads and gates clear so that fire engines can reach the village and the fields."},{title:"Return only when Civil Defense says so",detail:"Follow the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authorities. If they tell you to leave, go at once, and do not return until they say it is safe.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"On a windy evening, from the village you see smoke rising from the wheat fields at the foot of the slope below the village. The wind is pushing the fire uphill toward the houses. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I call 115: the village name, where the fields are and that the fire is moving toward the houses; then I warn the village by phone and start the village plan",correct:!0,feedback:"An early call with an exact location gives the 115 teams time, and the village plan gets everyone ready together."},{text:"All the men go down with buckets to put the fire out at its front",correct:!1,feedback:"Buckets do not stop a large field fire in the wind, and going down the slope toward the fire puts people in its path."},{text:"I drive close to the fire to take photos and see how big it is",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke can hide the road and a fast fire can surround a car. Call 115 from where you are."},{text:"I wait to see whether the wind changes direction",correct:!1,feedback:"Fire moves fastest uphill and with the wind. Waiting wastes the time you need to get ready."}]},{prompt:"Civil Defense tells the village to leave. Your elderly neighbor refuses; she wants to stay and water her garden. The shortest road passes next to the burning fields. What do you do?",options:[{text:"We leave at once by the agreed road away from the fire, I take my neighbor with me and I tell 115 if anyone refuses to leave",correct:!0,feedback:"Leaving early by the agreed road keeps people away from smoke and flames, and 115 needs to know who is still in the village."},{text:"We take the short road next to the burning fields, because it is faster",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke and flames can close that road suddenly. Choose the road away from the fire, even if it is longer."},{text:"I stay with her to protect the house with a garden hose",correct:!1,feedback:"A garden hose will not stop a large field fire. When Civil Defense says to leave, leave at once."},{text:"I open the animal pens and run uphill to the hill above the village",correct:!1,feedback:"Fire races uphill; running up the hill puts you in its path. Take the road away from the fire."}]}],realCase:"During the harvest, a farmer cleaned the chaff and dust from around the engine and exhaust of his harvester every day and carried water and an extinguisher on it. His neighbor did not. On a hot, windy day, the stubble behind the neighbor’s harvester caught fire. The driver stopped, moved the machine and the workers out of the fire’s path, and called 115 with the village name and a clear landmark. The villagers had already plowed a firebreak around the village and kept the roads open; the fire slowed at the firebreak until the fire engines arrived."},{id:20,icon:"Bomb",accent:"#b45309",title:"Mines and unexploded ordnance: stay safe",subtitle:"Recognize the danger, keep away, report",summary:"Mines, cluster munitions and other unexploded ordnance can stay dangerous for decades, even when they look old, rusty or harmless. Everyone can stay safe by following a few rules: do not touch, keep away, keep others away and report.",blocks:[{heading:"What can be dangerous",body:"Explosive ordnance comes in many sizes, shapes and colors. It can lie on the ground, be buried in soil, hide in grass, rubble or old buildings, or hang in trees. Treat these as dangerous:",points:["Mines: often buried or hidden, and very hard to see.","Small bomblets from cluster munitions: some are brightly colored and can look like toys.","Unexploded shells, rockets, mortar bombs and grenades.","Any metal or plastic object of unknown shape in a place where there was fighting.","Old, rusty or broken items: they can still explode."]},{heading:"If you see a suspicious object",points:["Stop and do not go closer.","Do not touch, move, kick or pick it up; do not throw anything at it and never try to burn it.","Warn the people around you and keep everyone away, especially children.","Remember the place and nearby landmarks.","Move well away before you use a phone, then call 115 and follow the instructions of Civil Defense.","Never carry the object to your home, a police station or a Civil Defense station."]},{heading:"Warning signs and fenced areas",body:'Warning signs for explosive hazards are usually red or orange, often with a skull and crossbones and the word "Danger" or "Mines". A dangerous area can also be marked with red-painted stones, fences, wire, string or tape. Signs in your area may look different.',points:["Respect every sign and fence, and never cross them.","Never remove, move or take a sign, even an old or broken one.","No sign does not mean safe: most dangerous areas are not marked.","Be careful near overgrown fields, unused paths, ruins and abandoned military positions; use main roads and well-used paths."]},{heading:"If you think you are in a mined area",body:`You see a mine, a warning sign or a strange object near your feet, or there has been an explosion:
1. Stop at once. Stay still and do not move your feet.
2. Call out for help, and warn others not to come to you.
3. Wait for a trained team, even if it takes a long time.

Never try to walk back in your own footprints, test the ground with a stick or place marks: these are not safe ways out. If someone is injured, do not run to them: call for help and wait for trained rescuers.`},{heading:"Advice for people at higher risk",points:["Farmers: if you find a strange object while plowing or digging, stop work, keep everyone away and report it; do not farm land that is marked or that others avoid.","Shepherds: do not take animals into marked or suspected areas, and never follow an animal that has gone in.","Builders and well diggers: if digging uncovers a strange object, stop at once, get everyone away and report it.","Families returning home after fighting: before you use a house, field or road, ask the authorities whether it has been checked.","Scrap collectors: never collect, cut, open or sell military scrap; it can explode and endanger you, the buyer and the neighbors."]},{heading:"Talking with children",body:"Children are curious, and some objects look like toys. Talk with them calmly and often, without frightening them, and praise them when they tell you about something they saw. Teach them:",points:["If you did not drop it, do not pick it up.","Never touch, kick or throw stones at a strange object, and never go closer.","Stay away from signs, fences, ruins and old military places.","Tell an adult at once.","If you think you are in a dangerous place: stop, stand still, shout for help and wait."]}],tips:["Save 115 in your phone and teach it to your family.","Find out which places near your home, fields and grazing land are dangerous; ask the authorities when you are not sure.","When you report, give the place, nearby landmarks and whether people are close to the object.","Do not trust rumors that an area has been cleared; ask the authorities.","Use main roads and well-used paths, especially in areas where there was fighting."],warnings:["Never touch, move, collect or sell explosive ordnance or military scrap, even if it looks old, rusty or already used.","In a mined area, never walk back in your footprints, test the ground or place marks: stop, stay still, call for help and wait.","Do not run to an injured person in a mined area: call for help and wait for trained rescuers."],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_uxo_keep_away.webp",title:"Keep away and report",description:"A farmer keeps his family well away from a suspicious object in a field before calling 115."}],activity:{prompt:"While plowing a field, a farmer sees a rusty metal object in the soil. His son wants to pick it up and sell it as scrap. What should they do?",options:[{text:"They stop work, keep everyone away, remember the place and call 115 once they are well away",correct:!0,feedback:"Old or rusty ordnance can still explode. Stopping work, keeping everyone away and reporting from a safe place protects the family and lets trained teams deal with it."},{text:"They lift it carefully onto the tractor and take it to the Civil Defense station",correct:!1,feedback:"Moving or carrying it can make it explode, on the way or at the station. Leave it where it is and report it from a safe place."},{text:"They plow around it and carry on, because it is old and rusty",correct:!1,feedback:"Age and rust do not make ordnance safe, and plowing nearby can set it off. Stop work, keep away and report it."},{text:"They cover it with soil and put a stone next to it as a mark",correct:!1,feedback:"Covering or marking it means staying next to the danger. Keep away, remember the place and report it to 115."}]},quiz:[{question:"There are no warning signs near an overgrown field where there was fighting. Is it safe to enter?",options:[{text:"No: most dangerous areas are not marked, so stay on used paths and ask the authorities",correct:!0},{text:"Yes: every dangerous area in the region has clear red signs, so a field without them is completely safe",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, once the grass has been burned, because the fire destroys all the mines hidden in the soil",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, if older people in the village say the field was cleared of mines many years ago",correct:!1}],explanation:"A missing sign does not mean safe: most affected areas are not marked. Burning does not make an area safe, and rumors are not a check. Use main roads and well-used paths and ask the authorities."},{question:"You realize you may have walked into a mined area. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Stop, keep your feet still, call for help and wait for a trained team",correct:!0},{text:"Walk back slowly and carefully in your own footprints to the road you came from",correct:!1},{text:"Push a stick into the ground in front of you to find a safe way out of the area",correct:!1},{text:"Run as fast as you can to the nearest road so you spend less time in the area",correct:!1}],explanation:"Stopping and waiting keeps you from stepping on another mine. Footprints are hard to see and walking back on them is not safe, testing the ground is a job for specialists, and you cannot outrun an explosion."},{question:"Your neighbor collects old shells and sells them to a scrap dealer. What is the safest advice?",options:[{text:"Stop collecting at once, do not move the items himself and report them to 115",correct:!0},{text:"Keep collecting, but take only the items that look empty, old or rusty and leave the rest",correct:!1},{text:"Open the items first to take out anything dangerous, then sell the metal",correct:!1},{text:"Keep them in a cool, closed room at home until the dealer comes for them",correct:!1}],explanation:"Military scrap can explode at any time, even if it looks empty or rusty, and it endangers the collector, the buyer and the neighbors. Do not move it: report it so that trained teams can deal with it."},{question:"Children find a strange, colorful object near the school. What should adults teach them?",options:[{text:"Do not touch it or go closer, keep friends away and tell an adult at once",correct:!0},{text:"Pick it up slowly and carefully and take it to the teacher to check what it is",correct:!1},{text:"Throw small stones at it from far away to see whether it is dangerous",correct:!1},{text:"Take a clear close-up photo of it and send it to the family group on the phone",correct:!1}],explanation:"Some explosive items look like toys. Touching, carrying, throwing things at it or going closer for a photo can make it explode; keeping away and telling an adult keeps everyone safe."},{question:"Where should you be when you call 115 about a suspicious object?",options:[{text:"Well away from it, in a safe place, after warning others to keep away",correct:!0},{text:"Right next to it, so that you can describe its shape, size and color exactly",correct:!1},{text:"Close to it with the phone camera on, so that you can send a clear picture",correct:!1},{text:"Holding it in your hand, so that you can read any writing or numbers on its side",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never go closer to look or take pictures. Phones and radios should not be used close to explosive ordnance, so move well away, then report the place and nearby landmarks."},{question:"There has been an explosion in a field, and an injured person inside it is shouting for help. You are on the road. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I do not go in; I call 115 and 122, keep people back and tell him to keep still",correct:!0},{text:"I go in along the way the injured person walked, because that way is safe",correct:!1},{text:"I test the ground in front of me with a stick and slowly make my way to him",correct:!1},{text:"I throw him a rope and drag him across the ground to the road",correct:!1}],explanation:"The way the injured person walked is not safe, testing the ground is a job for specialists, and dragging him across the ground can set off another mine. Stay where you are, call 115 and 122 and wait for trained rescuers."}],steps:[{title:"A suspicious object: stop and do not touch",detail:"Do not go closer. Do not touch, move, kick or pick it up, and do not throw anything at it. Being old or rusty does not make it safe.",critical:!0},{title:"Warn people and keep them away",detail:"Especially children. Remember the place and nearby landmarks, but do not put any marks next to it.",critical:!0},{title:"Move away and call 115",detail:"Move well away before you use a phone, then call 115: the place, nearby landmarks and whether people are close to it. Follow the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authorities."},{title:"In a mined area: stop and stay still",detail:"If you see a mine, a warning sign or a strange object near your feet, or there has been an explosion: stop at once and do not move your feet.",critical:!0},{title:"Call for help and wait",detail:"Warn others not to come to you, and wait for a trained team even if it takes a long time. Never try to walk back in your own footprints, test the ground with a stick or place marks.",critical:!0},{title:"Do not run to an injured person",detail:"If someone is injured in a mined area, do not go to them: stay where you are, call 115 and 122, talk to them calmly so that they keep still, and wait for trained rescuers.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"A shepherd boy’s sheep wander past a row of red-painted stones into an overgrown field. The boy wants to run after them. You are his uncle, standing on the path. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop him and keep him on the path, nobody enters the field, and I report the place to 115 from a safe spot",correct:!0,feedback:"Red-painted stones can mark a dangerous area. Never follow an animal that has gone in; a person’s life matters more than the sheep."},{text:"I go in carefully and step only in the sheep’s tracks, because they crossed safely",correct:!1,feedback:"The sheep crossing safely does not show that the ground is safe. Stay outside the markers and report it."},{text:"I throw stones toward the sheep to scare them back",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not throw anything into a dangerous area: a stone can hit explosive ordnance. Stay on the path and report it."},{text:"I let him go in and come back quickly, because he has grazed sheep here many times",correct:!1,feedback:"Past visits do not prove that the ground is safe; mines can be hidden. Nobody goes in."}]},{prompt:"While you are calling, the boy slips past you and goes a few steps into the field. There is an explosion near the sheep. The boy is standing where he is, crying. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I calmly tell him to stay still and not move his feet, keep talking to him, do not go in, alert 115 and 122 and we wait for the trained team",correct:!0,feedback:"Stopping, staying still and waiting is the safe way. Calm talk helps him keep still, and a trained team knows how to reach him."},{text:"I tell him to walk slowly back to the path in his own footprints",correct:!1,feedback:"Never teach walking back in footprints: they are hard to see and are not a safe way out. He must stay still and wait."},{text:"I go in quickly, pick him up and carry him out fast",correct:!1,feedback:"Going in yourself can set off another explosion and injure you both. Stay on the path and wait for trained rescuers."},{text:"I tell him to run to the path as fast as he can before another explosion",correct:!1,feedback:"Running in a mined area raises the risk of stepping on another mine. He must stay still and wait."}]}],realCase:"A family went back to their village after the fighting. Before plowing, the father asked the authorities whether the land had been checked. One day his son saw a rusty metal object in the yard. The father stopped him, moved everyone away, remembered the spot and the tree next to it, and called 115 once they were well away. A neighbor offered to buy it as scrap, but the father did not let anyone touch it. A trained team came and dealt with it. Afterward the father talked calmly with the children about it, without frightening them."},{id:21,icon:"CloudFog",accent:"#6d28d9",title:"Hazardous release near you: shelter in place or evacuate",subtitle:"Go in, stay in, tune in",summary:"A fire, a factory accident or a damaged tanker can release toxic gas, smoke or dust. Often the safest place is inside a building. This lesson explains how to shelter in place, when to evacuate and what to do if a substance gets on you.",blocks:[{heading:"Go in, stay in, tune in",body:"If the authorities announce a hazardous release, or you see a strange cloud or smoke, smell a strong chemical odor or hear a warning siren near you:",points:["Go in: get inside the nearest solid building at once, and bring children and pets in with you.","Stay in: stay inside until the authorities say it is safe to go out, even to help others.","Tune in: follow official announcements on radio, television or your phone, and do what they say.","Some dangerous gases have no smell: follow the instructions even if you notice nothing."]},{heading:"How to shelter in place",points:["Close and lock all outside doors and windows.","Switch off air conditioners, air coolers, fans and ventilation, and close the vents.","Go to an inner room with few windows, above ground level if you can (not a basement), and close the inner doors.","If you have them, seal gaps around doors, windows and vents with tape and plastic sheeting.","Do not eat or drink anything that may have been exposed.","In a car: close the windows and vents, switch off the air conditioner and heater, and go into the nearest building."]},{heading:"When to evacuate",body:"Evacuate when the authorities tell you to, or when the substance is inside your building and you can get out without passing through it.",points:["If you are told to evacuate, leave at once and follow the instructions you are given.","Move upwind (toward the side the wind is coming from) and, if you can, to higher ground: some gases are heavier than air and collect in low places.","Never pass through the cloud, the smoke or the spilled substance.","If you cannot reach clean air without passing through it, stay as far from it as you can and shelter in place."]},{heading:"If a substance gets on you: remove, remove, remove",body:`1. Remove yourself: move away from the substance into fresh air.
2. Remove outer clothing: take off clothing the substance touched; try not to pull it over your head; do not pull off clothing stuck to the skin; put it in a closed plastic bag.
3. Remove the substance: blot or brush it off the skin with dry tissue or cloth; if the skin itches or burns, rinse it with plenty of water. If it gets into your eyes, rinse them with clean water.

Do not eat, drink, smoke or touch your face. Tell the responders that you were exposed and follow their instructions for a medical check. If someone has trouble breathing or is badly hurt, call 115 and 122.`},{heading:"Never go to look",points:["Do not go toward the scene to look, film or take photos: the substance can reach you without warning.","Do not go outside to help others until the authorities say it is safe; call 115 and 122 instead.","Report a release, a strange cloud or a spill to 115 from a safe place.","Share only official announcements; rumors spread fear and wrong advice."]}],tips:["Choose your shelter room now: an inner room with few windows, above ground level.","Keep tape, scissors, plastic sheeting and a battery-powered radio in your emergency kit.","Learn how to switch off your air conditioner, air cooler and ventilation quickly.","Agree with your family where you will shelter and how you will reach each other.","Phone neighbors who may not have heard the announcement; do not go outside to tell them."],warnings:["Never go toward a cloud, smoke or a spill to look or take photos!","Do not leave your shelter until the authorities say it is safe, even to help others.","When you evacuate, never walk or drive through the cloud or the spilled substance."],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_shelter_in_place.webp",title:"Shelter in place",description:"A family shelters in an inner room with the doors and windows closed and follows official announcements."}],activity:{prompt:"A fire at a nearby warehouse is sending thick, strange-smelling smoke toward your neighborhood, and an official announcement tells people to stay indoors. Your children are playing in the yard. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I bring the children inside, close the doors and windows, switch off the cooler and ventilation, go to an inner room and follow the announcements",correct:!0,feedback:"This is shelter in place: indoors, with outside air shut out, the family breathes much less smoke, and the announcements tell you when it is safe or whether you must leave."},{text:"I drive the family toward the warehouse road to see how big the fire is, then decide",correct:!1,feedback:"Going toward the scene takes you into the smoke. Stay indoors and follow official announcements."},{text:"I open all the windows and switch on the fans so the smoke passes through the house quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Open windows and fans bring the smoke inside. Close everything and switch off fans, coolers and ventilation."},{text:"I walk with the children through the smoke to a relative's house in another neighborhood",correct:!1,feedback:"Walking through the smoke exposes the whole family. Evacuate only when you are told to, and never through the cloud."}]},quiz:[{question:'What does "go in, stay in, tune in" mean?',options:[{text:"Get inside and stay inside until it is declared safe; follow official announcements",correct:!0},{text:"Go into the affected area, stay there to help the injured and keep calling your relatives for news",correct:!1},{text:"Go up to the roof, stay there to watch the cloud and switch on all the fans to clear the air",correct:!1},{text:"Get into your car, stay in it with the engine and air conditioner running and listen to music",correct:!1}],explanation:"Indoors you breathe in much less of the substance. Stay inside until the authorities say it is safe, and follow official announcements on radio, television or your phone."},{question:"While you shelter at home, what should you do with the air conditioner or air cooler?",options:[{text:"Switch it off, together with fans and ventilation, and close the vents",correct:!0},{text:"Run it at full speed so that it pushes the gas back out through the windows and doors",correct:!1},{text:"Leave it on, because its filter removes chemicals from the indoor air",correct:!1},{text:"Keep it on only in the room where the family is sheltering together",correct:!1}],explanation:"Air conditioners, coolers, fans and ventilation can pull outside air, and the substance with it, into the house. Switch them off and close the vents."},{question:"When should you evacuate during a hazardous release?",options:[{text:"When the authorities tell you to, or when it is inside your building and you can get out safely",correct:!0},{text:"As soon as you hear about the release, even if the only way out of the area passes through the cloud",correct:!1},{text:"Only after you have gone outside to see for yourself which way the cloud is moving",correct:!1},{text:"Whenever your neighbors leave, whatever the official announcements are saying",correct:!1}],explanation:"Often staying inside is safer than going out. Leave when you are told to, or when the substance has reached you inside and you can get out without passing through it."},{question:"If you have to evacuate, which way is safest?",options:[{text:"Upwind and, if possible, to higher ground, without passing through the cloud",correct:!0},{text:"Downwind, so that the wind helps to carry you and your family quickly away from the cloud",correct:!1},{text:"Down into a valley or an underpass, where the air is calm and still",correct:!1},{text:"Toward the source, because the fire there burns up the dangerous gas",correct:!1}],explanation:"Upwind, the wind carries the substance away from you, and some gases are heavier than air and collect in low places. Never walk through the cloud."},{question:"A liquid chemical splashes on your arm and sleeve. What should you do first?",options:[{text:"Get to fresh air, remove the contaminated outer clothing and blot the liquid off",correct:!0},{text:"Wipe it with your bare hand and carry on, because a small amount cannot cause harm",correct:!1},{text:"Cover it with oil or toothpaste to stop it from soaking into the skin",correct:!1},{text:"Keep the clothing on so that it protects your skin until you reach the hospital for treatment",correct:!1}],explanation:"Remove yourself, remove outer clothing, remove the substance. Taking off contaminated clothing removes most of the contamination; rinse with plenty of water if the skin itches or burns, and never put home remedies on the skin."},{question:"You are driving when the radio announces a hazardous release nearby and tells people to shelter. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I close the windows and vents, turn off the air and heater, and go into the nearest building",correct:!0},{text:"I drive fast toward the release to get past it before the cloud reaches the road",correct:!1},{text:"I open the windows so that any fumes leave the car, and keep driving home",correct:!1},{text:"I stop at the roadside and get out of the car to watch which way the cloud moves",correct:!1}],explanation:"In a car, closing the windows and vents and switching off the air system keeps outside air away from you, and a solid building protects you better. Driving toward the release or getting out of the car exposes you to the substance."}],steps:[{title:"Go in",detail:"When the authorities announce it, or you see a strange cloud or smell a strong chemical odor: get inside the nearest solid building at once, and bring children and pets in with you.",critical:!0},{title:"Keep outside air out",detail:"Close and lock all outside doors and windows. Switch off air conditioners, air coolers, fans and ventilation, and close the vents.",critical:!0},{title:"Go to an inner room",detail:"A room with few windows, above ground level if you can (not a basement). Close the inner doors and, if you have them, seal gaps around doors, windows and vents with tape and plastic sheeting."},{title:"Tune in and stay in",detail:"Follow official announcements on radio, TV or your phone. Stay inside until the authorities say it is safe, even to help others; phone them instead.",critical:!0},{title:"Evacuate only when told to",detail:"Or when the substance is inside your building and you can get out without passing through it. Take the route you are given, toward the side the wind is coming from and to higher ground if you can, and never pass through the cloud or the spill.",critical:!0},{title:"If the substance gets on you",detail:"Move away, take off outer clothing and remove the substance from your skin; if the skin itches or burns, rinse it with plenty of water. Tell the responders that you were exposed. If someone has trouble breathing, call 115 and 122."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"A chemical tanker has overturned on the main road near your home, and a yellowish cloud with a sharp smell is drifting toward your street. There is no announcement yet. You are at home with your elderly father, and your wife is driving home. What do you do?",options:[{text:"We stay in, I close the windows and switch off the cooler, we go to an inner room, I report it to 115 from inside and phone my wife to keep away from the cloud",correct:!0,feedback:"This is “go in, stay in” even before an announcement. Reporting to 115 from a safe place and warning your wife by phone put nobody at risk."},{text:"I go to the road to see what the tanker is carrying",correct:!1,feedback:"Going to look takes you into the cloud, and the substance can reach you without warning. Stay inside."},{text:"I open all the windows so that air moves through the house and the smell goes",correct:!1,feedback:"Open windows bring the cloud inside. Close them all and switch off the air equipment."},{text:"I put my father in the car and we drive fast past the tanker to get out of the area",correct:!1,feedback:"Leaving without an announcement and through the cloud is the most dangerous choice. Shelter inside and follow announcements."}]},{prompt:"Later, the smell starts coming into the inner room under the door. An official radio announcement tells your neighborhood to evacuate by the northern road, away from the tanker’s road. The wind is coming from the north. What do you do?",options:[{text:"We leave at once with my father by the northern road, toward the side the wind is coming from, and never go through the cloud",correct:!0,feedback:"When the substance is getting in and the announcement says to evacuate, it is time to go. The given route and the upwind side take you away from the cloud."},{text:"We stay in, because at first we were told to stay in",correct:!1,feedback:"The instruction has changed and the substance is getting in. Follow the new announcement now and leave."},{text:"We take the southern road, because it is the shortest way to the main road, even though the cloud is there",correct:!1,feedback:"Never pass through the cloud. Take the given route away from the substance and toward the upwind side."},{text:"I wait until the smell gets very strong, then decide",correct:!1,feedback:"Some substances do harm with little or no smell. When the announcement says to leave, leave at once."}]}],realCase:"A fire at a chemical store sent thick smoke toward a neighborhood. An official radio announcement told people to stay indoors. One family brought the children in from the yard, closed the windows, switched off the air cooler and taped the gaps around the door of an inner room on the first floor. Their neighbor went near the store to film; he came back coughing, with burning eyes, and needed a medical check. When the announcement said that it was safe, the family opened the windows and aired the rooms."},{id:22,icon:"FlaskConical",accent:"#7e22ce",title:"Poisoning at home",subtitle:"Safe storage, swallowed poison, chemical splashes and carbon monoxide",summary:"Medicines, cleaning products, kerosene, petrol and pesticides are found in most homes, and each of them can poison a person. Young children are most at risk, because they put things in their mouths and cannot read labels. This lesson explains how to store these products safely, what to do if someone swallows something harmful, how to rinse chemicals from the eyes and skin, and how to act when carbon monoxide (CO) is suspected.",blocks:[{heading:"Store dangerous products safely",body:"Safe storage is the best protection:",points:["Keep medicines, cleaning products and pesticides locked away or high up, out of children's sight and reach.","Keep every product in its original container with its label, so that it is never mistaken for a drink.","Never put kerosene, petrol, chemicals or medicines in drink bottles, cups or food containers.","Close the container after each use and put it away at once; do not leave it on the floor or a table, even for a moment.","Never mix cleaning products with each other (for example, bleach with another cleaner): the mixture can release dangerous fumes."]},{heading:"If someone swallows something harmful",body:`1. Take the product away from the person and keep it away from children.
2. Call 122 at once, even if the person seems well: some poisons cause harm only later. Keep the container or label to show the ambulance team and the doctors.
3. Do not make the person vomit.
4. Do not give milk, oil, salt water or other home remedies, and give nothing to eat or drink unless a doctor tells you to.
5. If the person becomes drowsy or unconscious but is breathing normally, lay them on their side (recovery position) and keep checking their breathing. If they stop breathing normally, start CPR (see the lesson "First aid and CPR").`},{heading:"Kerosene, petrol and lamp oil",body:`These liquids are especially dangerous if swallowed. They are thin and slippery and can easily go down into the lungs, even in a small amount, and cause serious breathing problems and pneumonia. That is why making the person vomit is especially dangerous. Coughing, choking or difficult breathing after swallowing them can mean that the liquid has reached the lungs: call 122 at once.

• Buy and keep fuel only in proper fuel containers, locked away from children.`},{heading:"Chemicals in the eyes or on the skin",points:["Protect yourself first: do not touch the chemical with bare hands, and wear gloves if you have them.","Eyes: rinse at once with plenty of clean running water for at least 15 minutes, holding the eyelids open. Do not let the water run into the other eye.","Skin: brush off any dry powder, take off soiled clothing, watches and jewelry, then rinse the skin with plenty of running water for at least 15 minutes.","Call 122 for advice and keep the product label. Call at once if the person has trouble breathing, feels unwell or a large area of the body was affected."]},{heading:"Carbon monoxide (CO)",body:`CO is a poisonous gas with no color and no smell. It comes from heaters, stoves, generators and engines that burn fuel. Headache, dizziness, nausea, weakness or confusion near such equipment can be warning signs.

• Get everyone out into fresh air at once and call 115 and 122 from outside.
• Do not go back inside until you are told that it is safe.
• Never run a generator or a car engine inside a house or garage, even with the doors open, and install a CO alarm near the bedrooms.`}],tips:["Write 122 (ambulance) and 115 (Civil Defense) next to your phone, and teach them to everyone in the family.","Walk through your home at a small child's eye level and remove anything poisonous that a child could reach.","When you call 122 about a poisoning, have the container in your hand so that you can read the product name.","Read the label before you use any cleaning product, and follow its instructions.","Keep children away while you use cleaning products, pesticides or fuel."],warnings:["Never make a person who has swallowed poison vomit, and never give them milk, oil or salt water.","Never store kerosene, petrol or chemicals in drink bottles: a child may drink them.","Do not go into a room full of fumes or suspected carbon monoxide to rescue someone; get out and call 115."],activity:{prompt:"Your three-year-old nephew is coughing and crying. Next to him is an open drink bottle in which the family kept kerosene for the heater. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I take the bottle away, call 122 at once and keep the bottle to show the ambulance team. I do not make him vomit and I give him nothing to drink.",correct:!0,feedback:"Coughing after swallowing kerosene can mean that it reached the lungs. Calling 122 quickly, keeping the bottle and not causing vomiting protect the child."},{text:"I put my finger in his throat to make him vomit the kerosene out.",correct:!1,feedback:"Vomiting is especially dangerous with kerosene, because the liquid can go into the lungs. Call 122 instead."},{text:"I give him a glass of milk to weaken the poison.",correct:!1,feedback:"Milk does not make kerosene harmless and may make him vomit. Give nothing by mouth unless a doctor says so; call 122."},{text:"I wait to see whether the coughing stops before I call anyone.",correct:!1,feedback:"Coughing is a warning sign that kerosene may be in the lungs, and waiting wastes time. Call 122 now."}]},quiz:[{question:"Where should medicines and cleaning products be kept?",options:[{text:"Locked away or high up, in their original containers",correct:!0},{text:"Under the kitchen sink, where the whole family can find them quickly",correct:!1},{text:"In clean drink bottles, so that they take up less space",correct:!1},{text:"On a low bathroom shelf, with the caps left loose",correct:!1}],explanation:"Locked or high storage and the original labeled container keep children away from these products and stop anyone from mistaking them for a drink."},{question:"Someone has swallowed a cleaning product. Which action is correct?",options:[{text:"Call 122, keep the container and give nothing by mouth",correct:!0},{text:"Make them vomit at once, so that the product leaves the stomach quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Give them a glass of milk or oil to protect the stomach from the product",correct:!1},{text:"Give them salt water to drink, then wait to see whether they feel better",correct:!1}],explanation:"Vomiting and home remedies can cause more harm. Call 122, keep the container and give nothing to eat or drink unless a doctor tells you to."},{question:"Why is swallowed kerosene or petrol especially dangerous?",options:[{text:"It can slip into the lungs and cause serious breathing problems",correct:!0},{text:"It harms only the stomach, so the person is safe once they have vomited",correct:!1},{text:"It is dangerous only when a whole cup is swallowed, never a small sip",correct:!1},{text:"It becomes harmless after a few minutes, once it mixes with water",correct:!1}],explanation:"Kerosene and petrol can go down into the lungs, even in small amounts, and vomiting makes this more likely. Call 122 at once."},{question:"A chemical has splashed into someone's eye. How long should you rinse it with running water?",options:[{text:"At least 15 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"About 30 seconds",correct:!1},{text:"One quick splash is enough",correct:!1},{text:"Only until it stops stinging",correct:!1}],explanation:"Rinse at once with plenty of running water for at least 15 minutes, holding the eyelids open, then call 122 for advice."},{question:"While a heater is running indoors, several family members have a headache and feel dizzy. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Get everyone out, then call 115 and 122 from outside",correct:!0},{text:"Give everyone a painkiller and let them rest in the same warm room",correct:!1},{text:"Open one window a little and keep the heater running all night",correct:!1},{text:"Turn the heater up so the room warms quickly and the dizziness passes",correct:!1}],explanation:"These can be signs of carbon monoxide poisoning. CO has no color or smell: get everyone out first, call from outside and do not go back in until you are told it is safe."},{question:"Your neighbor says to give a child who swallowed bleach a glass of milk “to protect the stomach.” What do you say?",options:[{text:"No: we give nothing by mouth unless a doctor says so, and we call 122 at once",correct:!0},{text:"Yes: milk weakens the bleach and is safe to give at once",correct:!1},{text:"Better to give salt water so that the child vomits the bleach out",correct:!1},{text:"Give a spoon of oil to coat the throat before the ambulance comes",correct:!1}],explanation:"Milk, salt water and oil do not stop the poison and can add harm or cause vomiting. Give nothing by mouth unless a doctor says so; call 122 and keep the container."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_poisoning_home.webp",title:"Safe storage at home",description:"Medicines and cleaning products locked away in their original containers, out of children's reach"}],steps:[{title:"Take the product away and protect yourself",detail:"Take the product away from the person and from children. Do not touch a chemical with bare hands; wear gloves if you have them."},{title:"Call 122 at once",detail:"Even if the person seems well: some poisons cause harm only later. Hold the container or label so that you can read the product name and show it to the team.",critical:!0},{title:"Do not make them vomit and give nothing by mouth",detail:"Give no milk, oil, salt water or home remedies, and nothing to eat or drink unless a doctor says so. If it was kerosene, gasoline or lamp oil, vomiting is especially dangerous because it can reach the lungs.",critical:!0},{title:"Eyes or skin: rinse with plenty of water",detail:"Rinse with clean running water for at least 15 minutes. Take off soiled clothing, watches and jewelry. Eyes: hold the eyelids open and do not let the water run into the other eye."},{title:"If they become drowsy or unconscious",detail:"If they are breathing normally and there is no suspected injury to the neck, back, hip or pelvis, put them on their side (recovery position) and keep checking their breathing. If normal breathing stops, start CPR.",critical:!0},{title:"Fumes or CO: do not go in",detail:"Do not go into a room full of fumes or suspected CO to rescue someone. Get out everyone who can leave, and call 115 and 122 from outside.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"Your grandmother’s blood-pressure tablets were left on a low table. You find your two-year-old daughter holding the open bottle, and some tablets are missing. She seems fine and is playing. What do you do?",options:[{text:"She looks fine, so I watch her for a few hours",correct:!1,feedback:"Some poisons and medicines cause harm only later. Do not wait: call 122 at once."},{text:"I call 122 at once and keep the bottle to give the medicine’s name and how many tablets may be missing; I do not make her vomit or give her anything by mouth",correct:!0,feedback:"An early call, the medicine’s name and the number of tablets help the doctors decide what to do. Vomiting and home remedies add harm."},{text:"I put my finger down her throat so that she vomits the tablets",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not make her vomit: it can cause harm and the material can go into the lungs. Call 122."},{text:"I give her a glass of milk to protect her stomach",correct:!1,feedback:"Milk and home remedies do not stop the poison and can make her vomit. Give nothing by mouth unless a doctor says so."}]},{prompt:"While you wait for the ambulance, your daughter becomes very sleepy and then stops responding, but she is breathing normally. She did not fall or hit her head. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I put her on her side (recovery position), keep checking her breathing and tell 122; if she stops breathing normally, I start CPR",correct:!0,feedback:"With no suspected neck or back injury and normal breathing, the recovery position keeps her airway open. 122 needs to know that her condition has changed."},{text:"I splash cold water on her face and shake her to keep her awake",correct:!1,feedback:"Shaking and water will not wake a poisoned child and can do harm. Put her on her side and watch her breathing."},{text:"I give her sugar water to wake her up",correct:!1,feedback:"Never put anything in the mouth of a drowsy or unconscious person: it can go into the lungs. Put her on her side."},{text:"I lay her on her back with a high pillow under her head",correct:!1,feedback:"On her back with a high pillow, the tongue or vomit can block her airway. The recovery position is safer."}]}],realCase:"A woman mixed two cleaning products in a small bathroom to get a stronger clean. Soon she began to cough and felt tightness in her chest. Her daughter took her outside into fresh air at once, closed the bathroom door without going back in, and called 122. She brought both containers so that she could read their names. A neighbor said to give her milk, but the daughter gave her nothing until the doctor said so. After the check-up, the family decided never to mix cleaning products again and to keep the bathroom aired."},{id:23,icon:"Ambulance",accent:"#be123c",title:"Medical emergencies: recognize and call",subtitle:"Stroke, heart attack, seizure, low blood sugar, severe allergy, shock and unconsciousness",summary:"In a sudden illness, the most important things a citizen can do are to recognize the warning signs, call 122 at once and stay with the person. This lesson teaches the signs of a stroke (FAST), a heart attack, a seizure, low blood sugar, a severe allergic reaction and shock, what to do for an unconscious person, and what to say when you call 122. It does not replace a first aid course.",blocks:[{heading:"Stroke: think FAST",body:`A stroke happens when part of the brain suddenly stops getting enough blood. Treatment works best when it starts early, so act at once:
• F = Face: ask the person to smile. Does one side of the face droop?
• A = Arms: ask them to raise both arms. Does one arm drift down or feel weak?
• S = Speech: is their speech slurred or strange, or do they not understand you?
• T = Time: if you see any of these signs, call 122 at once.

Note the time the signs began, or when the person was last seen well, and tell the ambulance team. Call even if the signs go away. Do not give food or drink, because a stroke can make swallowing difficult.`},{heading:"Heart attack",body:`Warning signs can include chest pain, pressure or squeezing; pain that spreads to an arm, the neck, the jaw or the back; shortness of breath; sweating; and nausea.
• Call 122 at once. Let the ambulance take the person to hospital; do not let them drive.
• Stop all activity: help the person sit or rest in the position that is most comfortable for them, and stay with them.
• If they become unresponsive and stop breathing normally, start CPR and send someone to bring an AED.`},{heading:"Seizure",body:`During a seizure the person may fall, stiffen and jerk, or stare and not respond. Most seizures stop by themselves within a few minutes.
• Stay with the person and note the time the seizure starts.
• Move hard or sharp objects away and put something soft and flat, such as a folded jacket, under their head.
• Do not hold them down and put nothing in their mouth. Give no food, drink or medicine until they are fully awake.
• When the jerking stops, turn them onto their side (recovery position) if no neck or back injury is suspected, and check their breathing.

Call 122 if:`,points:["It is the person's first seizure.","It lasts more than 5 minutes, or another seizure starts before the person recovers.","It happens in water, or the person is injured or has trouble breathing.","The person is pregnant, or is a baby under 6 months old.","The person does not wake up properly within 5–10 minutes after the seizure stops."]},{heading:"Low blood sugar, severe allergy and shock",points:["Low blood sugar (often in a person with diabetes): sudden confusion, shaking, sweating or strange behavior. If the person is awake and able to swallow, give them sugar, sweets or a sweet drink. Call 122 if they cannot swallow, have a seizure or are not better within 10 minutes.","Severe allergic reaction (anaphylaxis): swelling of the lips, face or throat, difficulty breathing, wheezing, a rash, dizziness or fainting, often minutes after a food, a medicine or an insect sting. Call 122 at once. If the person has their own adrenaline auto-injector, help them use it as its instructions show.","In a severe allergic reaction, let the person sit up if breathing is hard; otherwise let them lie down. Do not let them stand up or walk, even if they feel better.","Shock (after serious bleeding, burns or a severe illness): pale, cold and clammy skin, a fast heartbeat, dizziness or confusion. Call 122, lay the person on their back, keep them warm and give nothing to eat or drink. If they vomit or become drowsy, turn them onto their side unless a neck or back injury is suspected."]},{heading:"Unconscious: is the person breathing normally?",body:'Tap their shoulders and ask loudly: "Are you all right?" If there is no response, call 122 (or have someone else call) and look at the chest for no more than 10 seconds.',points:["Breathing normally, and no fall, crash or blow to the head, neck or back: put the person on their side in the recovery position, give nothing by mouth and keep checking their breathing.","Breathing normally, but a neck, back, hip or pelvis injury is possible: do not move them unless the place is unsafe; keep them still and keep watching their breathing.",'Not breathing, or only gasping: start CPR at once and send someone for an AED (see the lesson "First aid and CPR").']},{heading:"Calling 122: what to say",body:`Stay calm and answer the call-taker's questions:
1. Where you are: area, street, a nearby landmark, building and floor.
2. What happened and what you see, for example: "A man of about 60; one side of his face is drooping."
3. How many people are ill or hurt, and whether there is any danger.
4. Your name and phone number.

Do not hang up until you are told to. Stay with the person, and keep their medicines and medical papers ready for the ambulance team.`}],tips:["Learn FAST and teach it to your family: face, arms, speech, time.","Take a first aid and CPR course, and renew it every few years.","If someone in your family has diabetes, epilepsy, a heart condition or a severe allergy, learn their emergency plan and where they keep their medicines and devices.","Keep a written list of each family member's illnesses, medicines and allergies, and give it to the ambulance team.","Stay with the person until the ambulance team takes over."],warnings:["Never put anything in the mouth of a person who is having a seizure or who is not fully awake.","Do not give medicines on your own; only help a person use their own prescribed adrenaline auto-injector or inhaler.","Do not wait to see whether the signs of a stroke or a heart attack go away: call 122 at once."],activity:{prompt:"At a family gathering, your 62-year-old uncle suddenly cannot lift his right arm and his speech is slurred. He says he only needs to lie down for a while. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I call 122 at once, tell them what I see and the time the signs began, and stay with him until the ambulance arrives.",correct:!0,feedback:"Arm weakness and slurred speech are stroke signs (FAST). Treatment works best when it starts early, and the time the signs began helps the doctors."},{text:"I let him lie down and rest, and we call a doctor tomorrow if he is not better.",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting loses time: a stroke needs treatment as early as possible. Call 122 now, even if he feels better."},{text:"I drive him to a pharmacy so that he can buy a medicine for blood pressure.",correct:!1,feedback:"A pharmacy cannot treat a stroke, and medicines taken without a doctor may harm him. Call 122 so that the ambulance takes him to hospital."},{text:"I give him sweet tea and wait half an hour to see whether his speech improves.",correct:!1,feedback:"Sweet tea does not treat a stroke, and waiting loses time that matters. Call 122 at once."}]},quiz:[{question:"What does the T in FAST mean?",options:[{text:"Time: call 122 at once and note when the signs began",correct:!0},{text:"Temperature: check first whether the person has a fever",correct:!1},{text:"Tablets: give the person their usual medicine first",correct:!1},{text:"Tea: give the person a warm drink to calm them down",correct:!1}],explanation:"FAST stands for face, arms, speech and time. Time means calling 122 at once; the time the signs began helps the doctors choose the treatment."},{question:"A person is having a seizure on the floor. What is correct?",options:[{text:"Move hard objects away, time it and put nothing in their mouth",correct:!0},{text:"Hold their arms and legs firmly so that the jerking stops sooner",correct:!1},{text:"Put a spoon between their teeth so that they do not bite their tongue",correct:!1},{text:"Pour cold water on their face to wake them and stop the seizure",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not hold the person down or put anything in their mouth. Protect them from injury, time the seizure and turn them on their side when the jerking stops."},{question:"When must you call 122 for a seizure?",options:[{text:"If it lasts more than 5 minutes or it is the person's first seizure",correct:!0},{text:"Only if the person asks you to call once the seizure has ended",correct:!1},{text:"Never, because seizures always stop by themselves without help",correct:!1},{text:"Only if the seizure goes on for longer than half an hour without stopping",correct:!1}],explanation:"Call 122 for a first seizure, a seizure longer than 5 minutes, repeated seizures, a seizure in water, an injury, breathing trouble, pregnancy, a baby under 6 months, or if the person does not wake up properly."},{question:"A person with diabetes is confused and sweating but is awake and able to swallow. What helps?",options:[{text:"Give them sugar or a sweet drink; call 122 if they do not improve",correct:!0},{text:"Give them an extra dose of their insulin to bring their sugar back",correct:!1},{text:"Let them sleep it off in a quiet room without anything to eat",correct:!1},{text:"Pour a sweet drink into their mouth even if they become drowsy",correct:!1}],explanation:"Low blood sugar is treated with sugar by mouth only if the person is awake and can swallow. Call 122 if they cannot swallow, have a seizure or are not better within 10 minutes."},{question:"An unconscious person is breathing normally, and there is no sign of a fall or a blow to the neck or back. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Call 122, turn them on their side and keep checking breathing",correct:!0},{text:"Sit them up and give them water so that they wake up more quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Start chest compressions at once, even though they are breathing",correct:!1},{text:"Leave them alone on their back and go to look for their family",correct:!1}],explanation:"The recovery position keeps the airway open in an unconscious person who breathes normally and has no suspected neck, back, hip or pelvis injury. Give nothing by mouth; if breathing stops, start CPR."},{question:"At a bus stop, a man suddenly grips his chest, is sweating and says the pain spreads to his left arm. He asks you to drive him home to rest. What is the best action?",options:[{text:"Call 122, help him sit and rest, and stay with him",correct:!0},{text:"Drive him home so that he can lie down in his own bed",correct:!1},{text:"Give him one of your own heart tablets to ease the pain",correct:!1},{text:"Ask him to walk around slowly to see if the pain passes",correct:!1}],explanation:"Chest pain spreading to the arm with sweating can be a heart attack. Call 122 at once, stop all activity, let him rest in the position most comfortable for him, do not let him drive and do not give him your own medicines."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_medical_emergencies.webp",title:"Stroke signs: FAST",description:"Face, arms, speech, time: recognize the signs and call 122"}],steps:[{title:"Make sure the place is safe",detail:"Before you go close, look for danger such as traffic, electricity, fire or gas. Do not become a second casualty.",critical:!0},{title:"Recognize the warning signs",detail:"Use FAST for a stroke, and look for chest pain, a seizure, sudden confusion, swelling of the face or throat, difficulty breathing or pale, cold skin. Tap the person's shoulders and ask loudly whether they are all right."},{title:"Call 122 at once",detail:"Say where you are first, then what you see, how many people are ill and any danger, and give your phone number. Do not hang up until you are told to, and follow the call-taker's instructions.",critical:!0},{title:"Give only safe, simple help",detail:"Unconscious and breathing normally, with no suspected neck, back, hip or pelvis injury: recovery position. Not breathing normally: CPR and an AED. Give nothing by mouth to a person who is not fully awake, and give no medicines; only help the person use their own prescribed adrenaline auto-injector or inhaler.",critical:!0},{title:"Stay with the person and hand over",detail:"Keep watching their breathing, note the time the signs began and give the ambulance team the person's medicines, medical papers and what you saw."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"At a wedding, a woman of about 40 who is allergic to nuts suddenly has a swollen face and lips and is wheezing after eating a dessert. Her husband says her own adrenaline auto-injector is in her bag. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I call 122 at once and help her use her own auto-injector as its instructions show, while someone else brings the bag.",correct:!0,feedback:"Swelling and wheezing after a food are signs of a severe allergic reaction. Calling 122 and helping her use her own prescribed device is the right first aid."},{text:"I give her an allergy tablet from my own bag and wait to see whether the swelling goes down.",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not give your own medicines to others. Waiting in a severe reaction loses time because the throat can close: call 122 and help her use her own device."},{text:"I give her a glass of water and ask her to walk outside for some fresh air.",correct:!1,feedback:"Walking can make a severe allergic reaction worse, and drinking is not safe if her throat is swelling. Let her sit or lie down and call 122."}]},{prompt:"The ambulance is on the way. She has used her auto-injector and now says she feels better and wants to stand up and go home. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I keep her sitting (or lying down if breathing is easy), stay with her, wait for the ambulance team and tell them when the device was used.",correct:!0,feedback:"She may get worse again even though she feels better now, and standing up can make her collapse. Do not let her stand or walk; the ambulance team should check her."},{text:"I let her go home, because the device has worked and the danger is over.",correct:!1,feedback:"A severe reaction can come back after a short improvement. She must not stand or walk, and she still needs the ambulance team to check her."},{text:"I cancel the ambulance and drive her to a pharmacy to get more medicine.",correct:!1,feedback:"A pharmacy is not the right place, and cancelling the ambulance takes away expert help. Keep her still and wait for 122."}]}],realCase:"At a family lunch, a grandfather suddenly could not hold his cup and one side of his mouth drooped. His granddaughter, who had learned FAST at school, asked him to smile and raise both arms, and saw that one arm drifted down. She called 122, gave the address and a landmark first and told the call-taker the time the signs began. The family wanted to give him tea and a tablet, but she reminded them to give nothing by mouth. She stayed with him and had his list of medicines ready for the ambulance team. The lesson: recognize the signs, call 122 at once, note the time and give no food, drink or medicines."},{id:24,icon:"Handshake",accent:"#0369a1",title:"Evacuating people who need help",subtitle:"Children, older people, people with disabilities, pregnant women and people who are ill",summary:"In a fire, an earthquake or a flood, some people cannot get out quickly or on their own: young children, older people, people with disabilities, pregnant women and people who are ill. Their safety depends on a plan made before the emergency. This lesson explains how to make a personal evacuation plan, how to communicate with people who are deaf or blind, why ordinary elevators are not used in a fire, and how to help without putting yourself or the person in danger.",blocks:[{heading:"Who may need help?",body:"Think about everyone in your home, your building and your street who may not be able to get out quickly or alone:",points:["Babies and young children, who must be woken and taken out by an adult.","Older people who walk slowly, use a walker or cannot hear the alarm well.","People who use a wheelchair, crutches or a cane, and people with a visual, hearing, intellectual or mental health disability.","Pregnant women, people who are ill or recovering from an operation, and anyone who depends on medicines or medical equipment."]},{heading:"A personal evacuation plan",body:"Make the plan before an emergency, together with the person:",points:["The person knows their own abilities and needs best: ask them, and plan with their family and the building manager.","Choose a helper (a buddy) and a backup helper for each person, and make sure both agree to this role.","Write down the person's needs: medicines, medical devices, mobility aids, how they communicate and whom to call.","At night, keep glasses, hearing aids, the walking aid, a phone and a flashlight next to the bed.","If possible, the person sleeps on the ground floor, close to an exit.","Practice the plan together regularly."]},{heading:"Communicating with people who are deaf or blind",points:["Deaf or hard of hearing: the person may not hear the alarm or a shout. Go right up to them and get their attention with a wave or a flashing light, then face them and speak clearly without shouting.","Explain with simple gestures, point to the exit or write a short note. In the dark, shine a light on your face and hands so that they can see you.","Blind or low vision: say who you are and what is happening, and ask how you can help. Do not grab the person or push them.","Offer your arm and let them hold it just above the elbow. Pause before steps, curbs and doorways and say what is ahead.","At the assembly point, tell them where they are and who is with them."]},{heading:"Elevators and refuge areas",points:["Never use an ordinary elevator in a fire. When the fire alarm sounds, elevators usually go to one floor and can then be operated only by firefighters.","A person who cannot use the stairs waits with their helper at the safe place named in the building's emergency plan, such as a refuge area, away from the fire and smoke.","If you cannot reach a person who needs help, get out and call 115. Tell the firefighters at once where the person is waiting: the floor, the room and how many people.","Agree on these arrangements in advance with the building manager, and follow the instructions of Civil Defense."]},{heading:"Helping without danger",points:["Ask before you help, and follow the person's own instructions on how to help them.","Do not lift or carry a person if you are not trained, unless there is immediate danger to life: you can injure them and yourself.","Do not move a wheelchair, walker or cane away from the person; take the mobility aid along if you can.","Take the person's medicines and devices only if they are at hand; never go back inside for them.","At the assembly point, stay with the person and tell whoever is counting people that they are safe."]}],tips:["Make a list of the neighbors in your building who may need help, and agree with them who will help whom.","Keep a copy of each person's needs list in your emergency kit.","Smoke alarms with a flashing light or a vibrating pad help people who are deaf or hard of hearing.","If someone uses a walker or a wheelchair, check that they can get through every exit door of your home.","Include the person who needs help in your drills, so that the plan really fits them."],warnings:["Never use an ordinary elevator during a fire.","Never go back into a burning building to rescue someone: tell the firefighters exactly where the person is."],activity:{prompt:"The fire alarm sounds in your apartment building. Your neighbor on the fourth floor is an older woman who uses a walker and cannot manage the stairs quickly. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I tell her what is happening, help her at her own pace down the stairs or to the agreed safe waiting place, and tell the firefighters exactly where she is.",correct:!0,feedback:"Helping her follow the plan and telling the firefighters exactly where she is gets trained rescuers to her quickly, without putting either of you at extra risk."},{text:"I take her down in the elevator, because it is faster than the stairs.",correct:!1,feedback:"Ordinary elevators must not be used in a fire: they go to one floor and are then controlled by firefighters. Use the stairs or the agreed waiting place."},{text:"I leave without her, because rescuing people is only the firefighters' job.",correct:!1,feedback:"Leaving her without telling anyone delays her rescue. Warn her, help as your plan says, and tell the firefighters where she is."},{text:"I lift her onto my back by myself and hurry down the stairs.",correct:!1,feedback:"Lifting her without training can injure you both, and a fall on the stairs blocks the way for others. Carry a person only if you are trained or there is immediate danger to life."}]},quiz:[{question:"Who should make a person's personal evacuation plan?",options:[{text:"The person, with their family, helpers and the building manager",correct:!0},{text:"Only the building manager, without asking the person what they need",correct:!1},{text:"Only the firefighters, on the day that an emergency actually happens",correct:!1},{text:"Nobody: it is better to decide what to do when the alarm goes off",correct:!1}],explanation:"The person knows their own needs best, so the plan is made with them, their family, their helpers and the building manager, and it is practiced before an emergency."},{question:"The fire alarm sounds. How do you get the attention of a neighbor who is deaf?",options:[{text:"Go up to them, wave or flash a light and face them",correct:!0},{text:"Shout loudly from the end of the corridor until they hear you",correct:!1},{text:"Grab their arm from behind and pull them quickly toward the exit",correct:!1},{text:"Send a message to their phone and carry on toward the exit alone",correct:!1}],explanation:"A deaf person may not hear the alarm or a shout. Go right up to them, get their attention, face them and explain with gestures or a written note."},{question:"How do you guide a person who is blind out of a building?",options:[{text:"Say who you are, offer your arm and describe steps and obstacles",correct:!0},{text:"Hold both of their shoulders and steer them from behind toward the exit",correct:!1},{text:"Take their hand and pull them along quickly without saying anything",correct:!1},{text:"Point toward the exit and tell them to find the way on their own",correct:!1}],explanation:"Say who you are and ask how to help. Let the person hold your arm just above the elbow, and say what is ahead, such as steps, curbs and doorways."},{question:"Why must ordinary elevators not be used in a fire?",options:[{text:"In a fire they go to one floor and only firefighters control them",correct:!0},{text:"They are fine to use as long as the fire is on another floor",correct:!1},{text:"They are safe for wheelchair users but not for anyone else in a fire",correct:!1},{text:"They may be used if someone holds the doors open with a chair",correct:!1}],explanation:"Ordinary elevators are not used in an emergency unless they are specially designed for it. When the fire alarm sounds they usually go to a set floor and only firefighters can operate them. A person who cannot use the stairs waits at the agreed place, and the firefighters are told where they are."},{question:"You are not trained to carry people, and there is no immediate danger. A man in a wheelchair cannot use the stairs. What is best?",options:[{text:"Help him to the agreed place and tell firefighters where he is",correct:!0},{text:"Lift him out of the chair and carry him down the stairs on your own",correct:!1},{text:"Leave him in the corridor and go outside without telling anybody",correct:!1},{text:"Take him down in the elevator, because the stairs are too narrow",correct:!1}],explanation:"Lifting without training can injure both of you. Unless there is immediate danger, help him to the agreed safe place and make sure the firefighters know exactly where he is."},{question:"During a fire evacuation, a neighbor who uses crutches asks you to go back to her apartment for her medicines. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stay out and tell the teams which medicines she needs",correct:!0},{text:"Run back in quickly, because her medicines are very important",correct:!1},{text:"Ask a child to run in and bring the medicines for her",correct:!1},{text:"Go back in only for the medicines near the door",correct:!1}],explanation:"Take a person's medicines and devices only if they are at hand when you leave; never go back inside for them. The teams can help her with what she needs."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_assisted_evacuation.webp",title:"Helping a neighbor evacuate",description:"A helper guides a blind man by the arm, while a wheelchair user waits with a buddy at the refuge area"}],steps:[{title:"Plan in advance with the person",detail:"Ask the person what help they need, choose a helper and a backup helper, and write down their medicines, devices and how they communicate."},{title:"Warn them in a way they understand",detail:"Go to the person and get their attention in a way that suits them: face a deaf person, and tell a blind person who you are. Explain simply what is happening.",critical:!0},{title:"Help them out by the stairs at their pace",detail:"Follow their instructions, take their mobility aid along if you can and never use an ordinary elevator in a fire.",critical:!0},{title:"If they cannot use the stairs",detail:"Go with them to the safe waiting place in the building's emergency plan, away from fire and smoke. Do not lift or carry them unless you are trained or there is immediate danger to life.",critical:!0},{title:"Tell the firefighters and stay with them",detail:"Tell the firefighters exactly where anyone is waiting: the floor, the room and how many people. At the assembly point, stay with the person and report that they are safe. Never go back inside.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"The fire alarm sounds at night in your apartment building. There is a smell of smoke, but the stairs are still clear. Your neighbor across the hall is a deaf man who lives alone, and his door is closed. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I knock hard on his door and, when he opens, face him, point to the exit and we leave together by the stairs.",correct:!0,feedback:"He may not hear the alarm. Getting his attention face to face and explaining with gestures gets him out with you while the stairs are still clear."},{text:"I shout his name loudly from the stairs and leave, because sooner or later he will hear the alarm.",correct:!1,feedback:"A deaf person may not hear the alarm or a shout. Go to him and get his attention face to face."},{text:"I phone him and wait at his door until he answers.",correct:!1,feedback:"A phone call may not reach a deaf person, and waiting in a smoky corridor puts you at risk. Knock on the door; if he does not answer, get out and tell the firefighters where he is."}]},{prompt:"You both reach the street. Your neighbor writes to tell you that the older woman on the top floor, who uses a wheelchair, may still be inside. Smoke is now coming out of the stairwell. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stay out, go to the firefighters at once and tell them the floor, the apartment and that she uses a wheelchair.",correct:!0,feedback:"With smoke in the stairwell, going back inside could trap you. Exact information gets trained firefighters to her quickly."},{text:"I run back up the stairs with a wet cloth over my face to bring her down.",correct:!1,feedback:"Never go back into a burning building. Smoke in the stairwell can overcome you quickly; tell the firefighters exactly where she is."},{text:"I wait at the assembly point to see whether she comes out on her own.",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting without telling anyone delays her rescue. Tell the firefighters at once where she is and that she cannot use the stairs."}]}],realCase:"In an apartment building, a family had agreed with their older neighbor, who uses a walker, that the son would be her helper and his sister the backup. A short list of her medicines and needs was in the family's emergency kit. One evening the fire alarm sounded because of smoke from a kitchen on the ground floor. The son went to her door, told her calmly what was happening and walked down the stairs with her at her pace, taking her walker with them. His sister told the firefighters that everyone from their floor was out. Planning beforehand meant that nobody rushed, used the elevator or went back inside."},{id:25,icon:"TrafficCone",accent:"#ea580c",title:"At a crash or vehicle fire",subtitle:"Protect yourself first, then call and help safely",summary:"Most crashes do not end in fire, but a crash scene is still dangerous: moving traffic, leaking fuel and smoke can hurt the people who stop to help. This lesson shows how to protect yourself first, how to call 115 and 122 with a location the teams can find, how to help injured people without making their injuries worse and what to do if a vehicle catches fire.",blocks:[{heading:"Protect yourself first",points:["Stop only where it is safe, off the road if you can, and do not block the way for ambulances and fire engines.","Switch on your hazard lights, switch off your engine and ask other drivers to switch off theirs.","Nobody smokes or lights a flame near the vehicles: fuel and most other vehicle fluids catch fire easily.","Put on a high-visibility vest if you have one, and never stand between the vehicles and oncoming traffic.","If you carry a warning triangle, place it only where you can do so without walking into traffic. The local rules on its use and distance are set by the traffic authorities.","Move uninjured people, especially children, away from the vehicles and the road to a safer place."]},{heading:"Call 115 and 122",points:["Call at once: after a crash, minutes can make the difference between life and death. Call 115 for fire, trapped people or leaking fuel, 122 for injured people, and both when both are needed.","Where: the name of the road or the two towns it connects, your direction of travel and a landmark nearby, such as a bridge, a gas station, a junction or a road sign.","If you are not sure where you are, read the nearest road signs or use the location shown on your phone's map.","What: how many vehicles, how many people are hurt or trapped, and any danger such as fire, smoke, leaking fuel or a truck with hazard plates.","Give your name and phone number and do not hang up until you are told to. If no one is hurt and there is no fire, leak or other danger, report the crash to the traffic police on 104."]},{heading:"Help the injured without making things worse",points:["Do not move an injured person unless they are in immediate danger, such as fire, heavy smoke or traffic that cannot be stopped: after a crash, the neck or back may be injured.","If the person is awake, talk to them calmly, ask them to keep their head and neck still and tell them that help is coming.","Do not remove a motorcyclist's helmet. It comes off only if the person is not breathing or their airway is blocked, and then only by trained people, ideally two, who keep the head and neck still.","Press firmly on heavy bleeding with a clean cloth and follow the instructions of the 122 call-taker.","Stay with the injured until the teams arrive, and then follow their instructions."]},{heading:"If a vehicle catches fire",points:["Warning signs: smoke or flames, or a smell of burning rubber or plastic. Act at once.","If you are driving, pull over as soon as it is safe and switch off the engine.","Get everyone out and move at least 30 meters (100 feet) from the burning vehicle, well away from traffic and out of the smoke, upwind if you can.","Never go back to a burning vehicle, not for a phone, a bag or documents.","Do not open the hood or the trunk if you think the fire is inside: fresh air can make the fire grow.","Do not try to fight the fire yourself. Call 115 from a safe distance."]},{heading:"Leaking fuel and dangerous loads",points:["Fuel vapor can catch fire from a spark, a cigarette or a hot engine: no smoking, no flames and engines off.","Keep people away from leaking fuel and do not walk through it.","A truck or tanker with orange plates or hazard signs: stay well away, do not try to rescue people from it yourself, tell 115 what the signs show and do not use your phone close to it.","Do not crowd around the scene or film the injured, and if you are only passing, keep your eyes on the road."]}],tips:["Keep a high-visibility vest, a flashlight and a first aid kit in your car.","Always notice the name of the road, your direction and the landmarks you pass.","Have your vehicle serviced regularly by a trained mechanic and get any fluid leak checked.","Never carry fuel cans or gas cylinders inside the passenger compartment.","Take a first aid course so that you know what to do while you wait for help."],warnings:["A burning vehicle can trap people quickly: get out at once and never go back.","Do not move injured people unless they are in immediate danger, and do not remove a motorcyclist's helmet.","Smoking, flames and running engines near leaking fuel can start a fire."],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_crash_scene_safety.webp",title:"Safe help at a crash scene",description:"A driver has stopped off the road with hazard lights on and calls for help, while everyone waits well away from a smoking car."}],activity:{prompt:"At night on a road between two towns, the car in front of you hits the side barrier. Smoke starts coming from under its hood. The driver is awake and is trying to open the door. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop safely behind it, off the road, with hazard lights on and my engine off, help the driver get out and away because of the smoke, call 115 and 122, and we wait at least 30 meters from the car.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: smoke means immediate danger, so helping the awake driver out is justified. You protect yourself first, call both numbers and keep everyone at least 30 meters away."},{text:"I open the hood to see where the smoke is coming from, so that I can tell 115 exactly what is burning.",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening the hood lets fresh air in, and the fire can grow into your face. Keep it closed, get everyone away and call 115."},{text:"I stop right next to the car in the same lane and leave my engine running, so that we can drive away quickly.",correct:!1,feedback:"Stopping in the lane puts you in the path of other traffic, and a running engine near a smoking car is one more source of ignition. Stop off the road with your engine off."},{text:"I wait a few minutes to see whether the smoke stops before I call, so that I do not make a false call.",correct:!1,feedback:"A real crash with smoke is never a false call. Minutes matter after a crash, so call 115 and 122 at once."}]},quiz:[{question:"A car is on fire. How far away should everyone move?",options:[{text:"At least 30 meters (100 feet), away from traffic",correct:!0},{text:"About 5 meters, close enough to watch the fire",correct:!1},{text:"Right beside the car, to take the bags out first",correct:!1},{text:"In the middle of the road, where drivers can see you",correct:!1}],explanation:"Fire safety guidance (USFA and NFPA) advises moving everyone at least 30 meters (100 feet) from a burning vehicle and well away from traffic. Never go back for belongings."},{question:"Smoke is coming from under a car's hood. What should you do with the hood?",options:[{text:"Keep it closed: air would make the fire grow",correct:!0},{text:"Open it wide so that the smoke can get out faster",correct:!1},{text:"Lift it a little and pour water onto the engine",correct:!1},{text:"Open it to check the engine before calling anyone",correct:!1}],explanation:"USFA advises: if you think there is a fire under the hood or in the trunk, do not open it, because fresh air could make the fire grow. Get away and call 115."},{question:"When may you move an injured person out of a crashed car?",options:[{text:"Only if they are in immediate danger, such as fire or traffic",correct:!0},{text:"Always at once, so that they can lie flat on the ground beside the car",correct:!1},{text:"Whenever there are two strong people to lift them",correct:!1},{text:"When the ambulance has not arrived after ten minutes",correct:!1}],explanation:"After a crash the neck or back may be injured. First aid guidelines say not to move an injured person unless the place is unsafe, for example because of fire, smoke or traffic."},{question:"A motorcyclist lies on the road after a crash. He is awake and breathing. What do you do about his helmet?",options:[{text:"Leave it on and ask him to keep his head still",correct:!0},{text:"Take it off at once so that he can breathe better",correct:!1},{text:"Loosen it and turn his head to check his neck",correct:!1},{text:"Pull it off quickly before the ambulance arrives",correct:!1}],explanation:"The helmet stays on while the rider is breathing. It is removed only if he is not breathing or his airway is blocked, and then by trained people, ideally two, who keep the head and neck still."},{question:"What does the 115 or 122 team need to hear from you first?",options:[{text:"The road, your direction and a nearby landmark",correct:!0},{text:"The make, model and color of every car involved",correct:!1},{text:"Your opinion about who caused the crash and why",correct:!1},{text:"The hospital you would like the injured taken to",correct:!1}],explanation:"Teams can only help if they find you. Give the location first, then what happened, how many are hurt, any dangers and your phone number, and do not hang up until told."},{question:"At a crash, an injured man is lying in the road and the traffic cannot be stopped. He is awake but cannot walk. What is right?",options:[{text:"Move him off the road, keeping his head and neck as still as possible",correct:!0},{text:"Leave him in the road, because injured people must never be moved",correct:!1},{text:"Lift him by his arms and legs and carry him quickly to your car",correct:!1},{text:"Ask him to stand up and walk to the side of the road on his own",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not move an injured person unless there is immediate danger. Traffic that cannot be stopped is immediate danger, so move him to safety while keeping his head and neck as still as you can."}],steps:[{title:"Stop safely and protect the scene",detail:"Stop off the road without blocking the way for emergency vehicles, switch on your hazard lights, switch off your engine and make sure nobody smokes. Never stand between the crashed vehicles and oncoming traffic.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 115 and 122",detail:"Give the road, your direction and a nearby landmark first, then how many vehicles, how many people are hurt or trapped and any fire, smoke or leaking fuel. Do not hang up until you are told to.",critical:!0},{title:"Move uninjured people away",detail:"Move uninjured people, especially children, away from the vehicles and the road. If a vehicle is on fire, everyone moves at least 30 meters (100 feet) away and never goes back."},{title:"Do not move the injured unless there is immediate danger",detail:"Talk to them, ask them to keep the head and neck still and leave a motorcyclist's helmet on. Move a person only if fire, heavy smoke or traffic that cannot be stopped puts their life in immediate danger.",critical:!0},{title:"Give simple first aid and wait",detail:"Press firmly on heavy bleeding with a clean cloth, follow the 122 call-taker's instructions and stay with the injured until the teams arrive."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"On a road outside town, a car has hit a tree, and you are the first to stop. A woman in the passenger seat is awake but says her neck hurts. There is no smoke, fire or leaking fuel, and the car is off the road. Her husband wants to pull her out and lay her on the ground. What do you advise?",options:[{text:"She stays in the seat and keeps her head still, while I call 122 and talk to her calmly.",correct:!0,feedback:"Neck pain after a crash can mean a neck injury. With no immediate danger, moving her can make it worse; keeping her still and calling 122 is right."},{text:"I help him pull her out gently so that she can lie flat on the ground.",correct:!1,feedback:"Even gentle pulling can worsen a neck injury. Move her only if fire, heavy smoke or traffic puts her in immediate danger."},{text:"I give her a pain tablet from my car so that she is calmer until the ambulance arrives.",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not give medicines on your own. Keep her still, talk to her calmly and follow the 122 call-taker's instructions."}]},{prompt:"While you wait, you smell fuel and then see smoke coming from under the hood. The woman is still in her seat and cannot get out alone. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"With her husband, I help her out while keeping her head and neck as still as possible, move everyone at least 30 meters away and tell 115 about the smoke.",correct:!0,feedback:"Smoke and leaking fuel are immediate danger, so moving her is now justified. Keep the head and neck as still as you can and tell 115 that the car is smoking."},{text:"I open the hood to see whether the smoke is only steam from the radiator.",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening the hood lets in fresh air, and the fire can grow into your face. Keep it closed and get everyone away."},{text:"I leave her in the car, because a person with neck pain must never be moved.",correct:!1,feedback:"The rule has an exception: when fire or smoke puts her life in immediate danger, moving her is safer than leaving her. Help her out and get away."},{text:"I try to put the fire out with water from my bottles before I help her out.",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not try to fight a vehicle fire yourself, and every second near the car counts. Get her out, move away and call 115."}]}],realCase:"Late one evening, a minibus slid off a wet road into a ditch. A passing driver stopped well off the road, switched on her hazard lights and switched off her engine. She called 115 and 122, gave the road name, the direction of travel and a nearby bridge, and said there was no fire but several people were hurt. She led the passengers who could walk away from the road and asked the injured driver to keep his head still instead of trying to climb out. Her calm call with a clear location helped the teams find the scene quickly."},{id:26,icon:"Waves",accent:"#0e7490",title:"Floods for families",subtitle:"Warnings, flash floods, leaving in time and returning home safely",summary:"Floods and flash floods can come quickly, sometimes with little or no warning, and most people who die in floods drown. This lesson helps your family get ready, act on warnings, stay out of floodwater, leave in time and return home safely.",blocks:[{heading:"Warnings and getting ready",points:["Follow official announcements from Civil Defense and the authorities on radio, television and their official pages, and do not act on rumors.","Ask Civil Defense and your local authorities how flood warnings and evacuation orders are announced where you live.","Make a family plan: where you will go on higher ground, which way you will go and how you will contact each other.","Prepare an emergency kit: drinking water, food that does not spoil, medicines, a flashlight, a radio, batteries, a first aid kit and copies of important documents in a waterproof bag.","Plan for children, older people, people with disabilities and anyone who needs help to move."]},{heading:"Flash floods in valleys and dry riverbeds",points:["A flash flood usually comes within 6 hours of heavy rain, sometimes within minutes, and the rain may have fallen far away from you.","A dry riverbed or valley can fill with fast water in minutes, even when it is not raining where you are.","Do not camp, picnic or park in low ground next to a stream or in a dry riverbed when rain is forecast or storms are near.","If the water starts to rise, climb to higher ground at once: walk up, do not try to drive out.","At night a flood is hard to see coming, so take warnings seriously before dark."]},{heading:"Turn around, don't drown",points:["Never walk, swim or drive into floodwater: 15 cm of moving water can knock an adult down and 30 cm can carry most cars away.","Turn around and find another way: the road under the water may have collapsed. Never drive around barriers that close a flooded road.","Stay off bridges over fast-moving water.","Floodwater can hide holes, debris and electrical dangers, and it is often contaminated.","If someone is in the water, do not go in after them: call 115 and 122, then reach out with a pole or rope or throw something that floats from a safe place."]},{heading:"When and how to leave",points:["Leave at once if the authorities tell you to, and leave early enough not to be trapped by the water.","Take your emergency kit, follow the recommended routes and do not take shortcuts: they may be blocked or flooded.","Switch off the electricity before leaving only if you are told to and can do it safely. Never touch switches or electrical equipment while you are wet or standing in water.","Gas: if you smell gas, do not touch any switch; go out, warn others and call 115 from outside. Close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it without going into the area where the gas has collected.","If water traps you inside a building, go to the highest safe level and call 115 with your exact location. Do not go into a closed room under the roof where the water could trap you; go onto the roof only if you must."]},{heading:"Returning home safely",points:["Return only when the authorities say it is safe. If the house is damaged, do not go in until a building expert has checked it.","Keep the electricity off until an electrician has checked the house. If you would have to step into water to reach the main switch, call an electrician instead.","Do not touch electrical equipment if it is wet or if you are standing in water.","Follow local advice on whether the tap water is safe to drink, and throw away any food that may have touched floodwater: when in doubt, throw it out.","Air out and dry the house. If it stayed closed and wet for several days, expect mold, and wear rubber boots and gloves when you clean.","Run a generator only outdoors, at least 6 meters (20 feet) from doors, windows and vents, and never inside the house, even with the doors open."]},{heading:"Why floods happen",body:"Floods come in different ways, and some of them come without any rain where you are:",points:["River floods rise slowly after days of rain or melting snow, and the river spills over its banks.","Flash floods come within minutes or hours after very heavy rain, often in valleys and dry riverbeds.","City floods happen when drains cannot carry all the rain away and water collects in streets and basements.","People make floods worse: building in valleys and riverbeds, blocked drains, cutting down trees and covering the ground with concrete leave the water nowhere to go.","Water released from a dam, or a damaged dam, can suddenly flood the valley below it."]},{heading:"When a flood is forecast",points:["Move documents, valuables and important things to an upper floor or a high shelf.","Keep the drains and channels near your home clear of rubbish so that water can flow away.","If there is time, place sandbags in front of doors and low openings, but leave at once if you are told to evacuate.","Move people who need help, such as older people and people with disabilities, to higher ground early, and keep the emergency kit and first aid supplies ready."]},{heading:"After the flood: hygiene and records",points:["Take photos of the damage before you clean, to use for reports and requests for help.","Do not go to flooded areas to look around: you can block rescue teams and put yourself in danger.","Floodwater can spread disease: wash your hands with soap and clean water after touching anything wet with floodwater."]}],tips:["Find out whether your home, work or school is in a low area or near a valley that can flood.","Agree on a meeting place on higher ground and a relative outside the area whom everyone can contact.","When heavy rain is forecast, keep your phone charged and a battery radio ready.","Check on older neighbors and people with disabilities before and after a flood.","Practice your family plan together."],warnings:["Never walk, swim or drive into floodwater: turn around and find another way.","Electricity and water together can kill: never touch switches or electrical equipment while you are wet or in water.","Children must never play in floodwater, streams or drainage channels after rain."],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_flood_family_higher_ground.webp",title:"Families move to higher ground",description:"A family with an emergency bag walks up a hill, away from a valley stream that is rising after rain."}],activity:{prompt:"Heavy rain fell in the mountains all night. Your family is having a picnic in a valley next to a dry riverbed, and it is not raining where you are. Suddenly the riverbed starts to fill with muddy water. What do you do?",options:[{text:"We leave our things and climb at once on foot to higher ground away from the riverbed, then call 115 if anyone is in danger.",correct:!0,feedback:"A flash flood can rise within minutes. Leaving everything and walking up to higher ground at once is the safest choice; belongings can be replaced, lives cannot."},{text:"We quickly pack everything into the car and drive out along the road at the bottom of the valley.",correct:!1,feedback:"Packing wastes minutes, and the valley road may flood first: 30 cm of moving water can carry a car away. Walk up, do not drive out."},{text:"We wait and watch, because it is not raining here, so the water will not rise much.",correct:!1,feedback:"Flash floods often come from rain that fell far away. A dry riverbed can become a torrent within minutes, so go to higher ground now."},{text:"We cross the riverbed quickly to the other side before the water gets deep.",correct:!1,feedback:"Never walk into moving water: just 15 cm can knock an adult down. Climb to higher ground on your own side."}]},quiz:[{question:"How deep does moving floodwater need to be to knock an adult down?",options:[{text:"About 15 cm",correct:!0},{text:"About 1 meter",correct:!1},{text:"About 50 cm",correct:!1},{text:"Only above the waist",correct:!1}],explanation:"The US National Weather Service (NWS) warns that about 15 cm (6 inches) of fast-moving water can knock over an adult and about 30 cm (12 inches) can carry away most cars."},{question:"The road ahead is covered with moving water. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Turn around and take another route",correct:!0},{text:"Drive through slowly in a low gear",correct:!1},{text:"Walk across first to test the depth",correct:!1},{text:"Follow the car in front through it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Turn around, don't drown: you cannot see how deep or fast the water is, or whether the road under it has collapsed. Never walk or drive into floodwater."},{question:"Before you leave home in a flood, when should you switch off the electricity?",options:[{text:"Only if you are told to and can stay dry while doing it",correct:!0},{text:"Always, even if you must stand in water to reach it",correct:!1},{text:"Never: leave every appliance on so the house stays lit",correct:!1},{text:"Only once water has already covered the floor of the house",correct:!1}],explanation:"Switch off the power before leaving only if you are told to and can do it safely. Never touch switches or electrical equipment while you are wet or standing in water."},{question:"After a flood, when can your family go back home?",options:[{text:"When the authorities say it is safe",correct:!0},{text:"As soon as the rain has stopped",correct:!1},{text:"When the water outside is below your knees",correct:!1},{text:"The next morning, whatever the damage",correct:!1}],explanation:"Return only when the authorities say it is safe. A damaged house must be checked by a building expert, and the electricity by an electrician, before you use them."},{question:"Where may a generator run after a flood?",options:[{text:"Outdoors, at least 6 meters from doors and windows",correct:!0},{text:"In the garage, if the garage door is left open",correct:!1},{text:"Inside the house, right next to an open window",correct:!1},{text:"In the hallway, as long as all the doors are left open",correct:!1}],explanation:"Generators give off carbon monoxide (CO), a poisonous gas with no color or smell. Run them only outdoors, at least 6 meters (20 feet) from doors, windows and vents, and never inside, even with the doors open."},{question:"After heavy rain, your neighbor's son has been swept into a drainage channel and is holding on to a branch. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Call 115 and 122 and reach him with a pole or rope from the bank",correct:!0},{text:"Jump into the channel at once and swim to him before he lets go",correct:!1},{text:"Wade in holding hands with two other adults to reach him",correct:!1},{text:"Run to find his parents first and decide together what to do",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not go into floodwater after someone: the current can sweep you away too. Call 115 and 122, then reach out with a pole or rope or throw something that floats from a safe place."},{question:"Heavy rain is forecast for tonight and your house is on a low street that floods. Your documents are in a box in the basement. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I move the documents to an upper floor now and follow the official warnings",correct:!0},{text:"I leave them in the basement because its walls are strong and rain cannot reach it",correct:!1},{text:"I wait until water reaches the street, then go down to bring them up",correct:!1},{text:"I wrap them in paper and leave them on the basement floor",correct:!1}],explanation:"The basement is the first place to flood. Move important things up before the flood; going down to the basement once water is coming risks being trapped and electric shock."}],steps:[{title:"Follow official warnings",detail:"Listen to Civil Defense and the authorities on radio, television and their official pages, and do not act on rumors."},{title:"Get ready to leave early",detail:"Keep your emergency kit ready, charge your phone and agree on a meeting place on higher ground. Decide who will help children, older people and people with disabilities."},{title:"Go to higher ground at once",detail:"When you are told to leave, or when water starts to rise, leave by the recommended route. In a valley or a dry riverbed, climb on foot; do not try to drive out.",critical:!0},{title:"Never walk or drive into floodwater",detail:"Turn around and find another way. 15 cm of moving water can knock an adult down and 30 cm can carry most cars away. Stay off bridges over fast-moving water.",critical:!0},{title:"If someone is in the water, do not go in",detail:"Call 115 and 122, then reach out with a pole or rope or throw something that floats from a safe place.",critical:!0},{title:"Return only when it is safe",detail:"Go back only when the authorities say it is safe. Keep the electricity off until an electrician has checked the house, and never touch switches while you are wet."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"It has rained heavily all afternoon. You are driving home with your two children when you reach a dip in the road covered by muddy, moving water. The car in front of you has just driven through. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I turn around and take another route, even if it is longer.",correct:!0,feedback:"You cannot see how deep or fast the water is, or whether the road beneath has collapsed. 30 cm of moving water can carry most cars away."},{text:"I follow the car in front, because it got across safely.",correct:!1,feedback:"The water may be deeper by then, or the road may give way under you. Never drive into floodwater: turn around."},{text:"I stop and send my older child across on foot to check the depth.",correct:!1,feedback:"15 cm of moving water can knock an adult down, and a child even more easily. Nobody walks into floodwater."}]},{prompt:"You reach home by another route. An hour later, Civil Defense announces that families in your low-lying street must leave. Water is starting to cover the street. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I take the emergency kit and leave at once with the children by the recommended route to higher ground.",correct:!0,feedback:"Leaving early, before the water rises further, keeps you from being trapped, and the recommended route avoids flooded or blocked roads."},{text:"I stay at home to protect the furniture and leave only if water comes inside.",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting can trap your family. Leave when the authorities tell you to; belongings can be replaced, lives cannot."},{text:"I wade through the street to my car, which is parked at the lowest corner, and we drive out.",correct:!1,feedback:"Never walk or drive into floodwater: it can hide holes, debris and electrical dangers. Take the recommended route that avoids the water."}]}],realCase:"After a night of heavy rain in the mountains, a family was picnicking beside a dry riverbed in a valley. It was not raining where they were. The father heard a roar upstream and saw the riverbed starting to fill with muddy water. Remembering the lesson, he told everyone to leave the blankets and food and climb the slope at once. Within minutes the water covered the place where they had been sitting and the low valley road. Another family tried to drive out along that road and had to leave their car and wait for help. Climbing early on foot was the safest choice."},{id:27,icon:"CloudLightning",accent:"#7c3aed",title:"Severe weather for the public",subtitle:"Heat, lightning, dust storms, strong wind, snow and ice",summary:"Severe weather can hurt people in a short time. In our region we face heat waves, thunderstorms with lightning, dust storms, strong winds, snow and ice. This lesson gives the main safe actions for each one. Always follow the weather warnings of the authorities and the instructions of Civil Defense.",blocks:[{heading:"Heat waves",points:["Older people, young children and people with long-term illnesses are at greater risk. Check on them often during a heat wave.","Drink water regularly through the day, even before you feel thirsty, and avoid hard work and sport in the hottest hours.","Heat exhaustion: heavy sweating, weakness, dizziness, headache, nausea and thirst. Move the person to a cool place, cool them with water and give sips of water if they are fully alert. Get medical help if they vomit, if the symptoms get worse or if they last more than an hour.","Heat stroke: confusion, strange behavior, slurred speech, a seizure or collapse in the heat; the skin may be hot and dry or still sweating. Call 122 and cool the person at once: the best way is cold water up to the neck for 15 minutes or until they are thinking clearly again, whichever comes first; otherwise use a cold shower, cold wet towels, ice packs and fanning. Give nothing to drink to a person who is confused.","Never leave a child, or anyone, in a parked car, not even for a minute: the inside can quickly become dangerously hot."]},{heading:"Lightning",points:["When thunder roars, go indoors: if you can hear thunder, lightning can reach you. There is no safe place outside during a thunderstorm.","Shelter in a solid building or in a car with a metal roof and the windows closed, and stay inside until 30 minutes after the last thunder.","If you cannot reach shelter, keep away from hilltops, open fields, lone tall trees, water and metal fences. A tent gives no protection.","Indoors, keep away from windows and doors, do not use corded phones and avoid showers and washing dishes until the storm has passed."]},{heading:"Dust storms",points:["At home, stay indoors and close windows and doors.","Infants, older people and people with asthma or other breathing problems need extra care: they stay indoors, and people with asthma follow their own asthma action plan and keep their own prescribed inhaler with them.","Driving: do not drive into a dust storm. If visibility drops, pull off the road as far as you can, stop, switch off all your lights, set the parking brake and take your foot off the brake pedal. Never stop on the road itself.","Your lights must be off while you are parked: drivers behind you may follow your lights and crash into you."]},{heading:"Strong winds",points:["Before the wind, tie down or bring in loose things on roofs, balconies and yards so that they do not blow into people or windows.","During the wind, go inside a sturdy building and move to an inner room away from windows.","Fallen power lines may be live: do not go near them or anything touching them, keep others away and call 115.","If you are driving, hold the wheel with both hands, slow down and keep your distance from trucks and buses."]},{heading:"Snow, ice and heating",points:["Heaters: keep fresh air coming into the room, install a CO alarm, never heat the house with a gas oven and never use a generator or a charcoal fire indoors.","Headache, dizziness, nausea or confusion near a heater can mean carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning: get everyone into fresh air at once and call 115 and 122 from outside.","On ice, wear shoes with a good grip, take short steps and walk slowly.","In snow and ice, avoid driving if you can. If you must drive, slow down, even if the road only looks wet, and keep blankets, water, a flashlight and a charged phone in the car."]}],tips:["Follow the weather forecast and the warnings of the authorities and Civil Defense.","In very hot or very cold weather, check on older neighbors and relatives often.","Secure loose objects on roofs and balconies as soon as strong wind is forecast.","Keep a flashlight, a battery radio and a charged phone ready for storms.","Have heaters and stoves checked and keep anything that can burn away from them."],warnings:["Never leave a child in a parked car, not even for a minute.","When thunder roars, go indoors: no place outside is safe.","Never run a generator or a charcoal fire indoors, and never heat a home with a gas oven."],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_severe_weather_actions.webp",title:"Safe actions in severe weather",description:"Four panels: cooling a person in the shade during a heat wave, a family going indoors as a thunderstorm comes, a car parked far off the road with its lights off in a dust storm and a person keeping away from a fallen power line."}],activity:{prompt:"On a very hot afternoon, your older neighbor has been working in his garden. Now he is confused, speaks strangely and his skin is very hot. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I call 122, move him into the shade or a cool room and start cooling him at once with cold water, wet towels and fanning, and I give him nothing to drink.",correct:!0,feedback:"Confusion in the heat means heat stroke, an emergency. Calling 122 and cooling him at once can save his life, and a confused person must not be given drinks."},{text:"I give him a big glass of cold water to drink and let him rest on a chair in the garden.",correct:!1,feedback:"A confused person must not be given drinks, and the garden keeps him in the heat. Call 122, move him to a cool place and cool him at once."},{text:"I help him to bed indoors so that he can sleep it off, and I check on him again in the evening.",correct:!1,feedback:"Heat stroke can kill if cooling is delayed. Do not leave him to sleep: call 122 and start cooling now."},{text:"I drive him to the hospital myself straight away, without stopping to cool him first.",correct:!1,feedback:"With heat stroke, cooling comes first and transport second. Call 122 and start cooling at once; the call-taker will guide you."}]},quiz:[{question:"During a thunderstorm, when should you go indoors?",options:[{text:"As soon as you hear thunder",correct:!0},{text:"Only when the rain gets heavy",correct:!1},{text:"Only when lightning is right overhead",correct:!1},{text:"Once the wind starts to blow hard",correct:!1}],explanation:"If you can hear thunder, you are close enough to be struck by lightning. When thunder roars, go indoors: there is no safe place outside during a thunderstorm."},{question:"After the last thunder, how long should you wait before going back outside?",options:[{text:"30 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"5 minutes",correct:!1},{text:"Until the rain stops",correct:!1},{text:"There is no need to wait",correct:!1}],explanation:"Lightning can still strike after the rain eases. The US National Weather Service (NWS) advises waiting 30 minutes after the last lightning or thunder before going back outside."},{question:"While you are driving, a dust storm suddenly cuts visibility. What is the safest action?",options:[{text:"Pull far off the road, stop and switch off all lights",correct:!0},{text:"Stop in your lane and switch on the hazard lights to be seen",correct:!1},{text:"Speed up to get out of the dust as fast as you can",correct:!1},{text:"Follow the taillights of the car in front of you",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never stop on the road itself. Pull off as far as you can, switch off all lights, set the parking brake and keep your foot off the brake, so that drivers behind do not follow your lights into you (NWS, Pull aside, stay alive)."},{question:"Which of these points to heat stroke rather than heat exhaustion?",options:[{text:"Confusion or strange behavior in the heat",correct:!0},{text:"Thirst after a long day of working in the sun",correct:!1},{text:"Heavy sweating and feeling tired after work",correct:!1},{text:"A mild headache while sitting in a hot room",correct:!1}],explanation:"Confusion, strange behavior, a seizure or collapse in the heat mean heat stroke, a life-threatening emergency: call 122 and cool the person at once. Thirst, heavy sweating and a mild headache fit heat exhaustion."},{question:"After a storm you see a power line lying on the ground. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stay away, keep others away and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Move it aside with a dry wooden stick",correct:!1},{text:"Step over it carefully if it is not sparking",correct:!1},{text:"Drive slowly over it to get past",correct:!1}],explanation:"A fallen power line, and anything or any water touching it, may be live even if it is not sparking. Never touch it, move it or drive over it: keep everyone away and call 115."},{question:"During a strong wind storm, your family is at home. Which place is the safest?",options:[{text:"An inner room away from windows",correct:!0},{text:"On the balcony, to watch the storm",correct:!1},{text:"Next to a large window, to see outside",correct:!1},{text:"On the roof, tying down the water tank",correct:!1}],explanation:"During strong wind, go inside a sturdy building and move to an inner room away from windows. Secure loose things before the wind, not during it."}],steps:[{title:"Follow the forecast and warnings",detail:"Follow the weather warnings of the authorities and the instructions of Civil Defense, and plan your day around them."},{title:"Prepare your home",detail:"Tie down or bring in loose things on roofs and balconies, keep a flashlight, a battery radio and a charged phone ready, and check on older neighbors."},{title:"Heat: drink, rest, check and cool",detail:"Drink water through the day, avoid hard work in the hottest hours and never leave anyone in a parked car. Confusion or collapse in the heat: call 122 and cool the person at once.",critical:!0},{title:"Thunder: go indoors",detail:"When thunder roars, go into a solid building or a car with a metal roof, and stay there until 30 minutes after the last thunder.",critical:!0},{title:"Dust and wind: off the road, away from lines",detail:"In a dust storm, pull as far off the road as you can, stop and switch off all lights. Keep away from fallen power lines and call 115.",critical:!0},{title:"Cold: heat your home safely",detail:"Keep fresh air coming in, install a CO alarm and never use a generator, a charcoal fire or a gas oven to heat the house.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"A heat wave is forecast for the week. Your older neighbor lives alone on the top floor, and his air cooler is broken. On the second hot afternoon you visit him. He is sweating heavily, feels weak and dizzy and has a headache, but he is alert and talking normally. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I move him somewhere cool, cool him with water and give him sips of water while I watch him closely.",correct:!0,feedback:"These are signs of heat exhaustion in an alert person. Cooling and sips of water help; get medical help if he vomits, gets worse or is not better within an hour."},{text:"I tell him to rest in his hot room and come back to check on him tomorrow.",correct:!1,feedback:"Heat exhaustion can turn into heat stroke if he stays in the heat. Move him somewhere cool now and keep watching him."},{text:"I give him a cup of hot tea and a tablet for the headache.",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not give medicines on your own, and a hot drink does not cool him. Move him somewhere cool and give him sips of water."}]},{prompt:"Half an hour later he becomes confused, does not know where he is and his speech is slurred. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I call 122 and cool him at once with cold water, wet towels, ice packs and fanning, and give him nothing to drink.",correct:!0,feedback:"Confusion and slurred speech in the heat mean heat stroke, a life-threatening emergency. Fast cooling and calling 122 can save his life; a confused person must not be given drinks."},{text:"I keep giving him water to drink, because he needs more fluids.",correct:!1,feedback:"A confused person may choke: give him nothing to drink. Call 122 and cool him at once."},{text:"I put him to bed to sleep and phone his family to come tomorrow.",correct:!1,feedback:"Heat stroke can kill if cooling is delayed. Do not let him sleep: call 122 and start cooling now."},{text:"I drive him to hospital myself without cooling him first.",correct:!1,feedback:"With heat stroke, cooling comes first and transport second. Call 122 and start cooling; the call-taker will guide you."}]}],realCase:"One winter evening, during a power cut, a family closed every window and lit a charcoal brazier in the living room to keep warm. After an hour the mother had a headache and felt dizzy, and the youngest child became very sleepy. The CO alarm that the eldest son had fitted that autumn began to sound. The father got everyone outside into fresh air at once and called 115 and 122 from the street. The ambulance team checked them all. They learned never to burn charcoal or run a generator indoors, and to keep the CO alarm working."},{id:28,icon:"Truck",accent:"#4d7c0f",title:"Agriculture and rural safety",subtitle:"Pesticides, tractors and machinery, grain and hay, and dangers hidden in the soil",summary:"Farm work has dangers of its own: poisons used to protect crops, heavy machines that can overturn or catch clothing, stores of grain and hay that can bury a person or catch fire, and old explosive ordnance in the ground. Children who play or ride near the work are in special danger. This lesson explains how farming families and farm workers can protect themselves and what to do when something goes wrong.",blocks:[{heading:"Pesticides: store them safely",body:"Many poisonings happen because a pesticide was within reach or looked like a drink:",points:["Keep pesticides locked away, out of children's reach, and never with or near food, animal feed or medicines.","Keep every pesticide in its original container with its label. Never pour it into a drink bottle, a cup or a food container: someone may drink it.","Do not keep highly toxic pesticides or concentrates in the house; keep them in a separate locked store.","Never use empty pesticide containers for water, food or animal feed. Get rid of them as the label and the local authorities say.","Treated seed and rodent bait can look like ordinary grain: keep them locked away from children and animals."]},{heading:"Using pesticides safely",points:["Read the whole label before you open the container, and follow it exactly: the amount, the mixing, the protective equipment and how long people must stay out of the treated area.","Wear the protective clothing that the label asks for, such as waterproof gloves, long pants and long sleeves.","Keep children, other people and animals away while you mix and spray, and until the time on the label has passed.","After spraying, change your clothes and wash your hands at once. Do not leave work clothes or spraying equipment where children play."]},{heading:"If someone is poisoned by a pesticide",body:`Dizziness, tiredness, blurred vision, nausea, a dry throat, difficulty breathing, stinging eyes, itchy skin or weak muscles during or after work with pesticides can be signs of poisoning.

1. Protect yourself first: wear gloves and do not touch the pesticide with bare hands. Take the person away from it, into fresh air.
2. Call 122 at once, even if the person seems well, and keep the container or its label to show the ambulance team and the doctors.
3. If it was swallowed: do not make the person vomit, and give nothing to eat or drink unless a doctor tells you to.
4. On the skin or in the eyes: take off soiled clothing and rinse the skin, or the open eye, with plenty of running water for at least 15 minutes.
5. If the person is unconscious but breathing normally and has no injury to the neck, back, hip or pelvis, lay them on their side and keep checking their breathing. If they stop breathing normally, start CPR (see the lesson "Poisoning at home").`},{heading:"Tractors and machinery",body:"Overturns, people being run over and clothing caught in a turning power take-off (PTO) shaft cause most serious tractor injuries.",points:["Use a tractor with a rollover protective structure (a roll bar or safety cab, ROPS) and always fasten the seat belt: together they give the driver the best chance of surviving an overturn. A qualified dealer can fit a frame to many older tractors.","Keep away from ditches, banks and holes, stay off slopes that are too steep, and slow down when you turn and on rough, slippery or muddy ground.","Keep every guard on the PTO shaft and the machines in place. Tie back long hair and do not wear loose clothing near moving parts.","Before you clean, adjust or unblock a machine, stop the engine and wait until the shaft and every moving part have stopped.","Nobody rides on a tractor unless it has a seat and a seat belt for that person. Children never ride on tractors or machines and never play near them.","Before you start the engine or the PTO, make sure that everyone, especially children, is well away from the machine."]},{heading:"Grain, hay and straw",points:["Never go into a grain bin, silo or trailer while grain is moving or being emptied: flowing grain acts like quicksand and can bury a person in seconds. Never walk on grain to make it flow.","Grain that has formed a crust or a steep pile can collapse suddenly on anyone standing on it or next to it.","Children never play in grain stores, grain trailers or haystacks.","Bale and store hay only when it is fully dry: damp or green hay can heat up inside the stack and catch fire by itself, from two weeks to three months or more after baling.","Warning signs: steam rising from the stack, mold, or a burning, musty or sweet caramel smell. If you see or smell smoke, or part of the stack is very hot, do not pull it apart: keep people away and call 115.","Keep hay and straw stacks away from houses, machinery, fuel, chemicals and power lines."]},{heading:"Explosive ordnance and field fires",points:['If plowing or digging uncovers a strange object, stop work at once. Do not touch or move it, keep everyone away, move well away before you use a phone, and report it to 115 (see the lesson "Mines and unexploded ordnance: stay safe").',"Do not farm or graze animals on land that is marked as dangerous or that other people avoid. A place without warning signs is not necessarily safe: an unused, overgrown field next to farmed fields may hide mines.","If you think you are in a mined area: stop, stay still, call for help and wait for a trained team.",'Do not park tractors or cars on dry grass or stubble: a hot exhaust can set it on fire. Harvest fires are covered in the lesson "Crop, stubble and vegetation fires".']}],tips:["Lock pesticides away in their original, labeled containers.","Read the label before every use and wear the protective clothing it asks for.","Fasten the seat belt on a tractor with a roll bar, and keep every guard in place.","Stop the engine and wait until everything has stopped before you clean or unblock a machine.","Agree as a family where children may play: far from machines, grain stores, haystacks and sprayed fields."],warnings:["Never put pesticides or fuel in drink bottles or food containers.","Never let a child ride on a tractor or play near working machines.","Never go into a grain bin or silo while grain is moving, and never walk on grain to make it flow."],activity:{prompt:"Your neighbor is about to plow a field on a slope. His 8-year-old son asks to ride on the tractor's fender, and the father says it is only for a few minutes. What do you advise?",options:[{text:"The boy stays off the tractor and away from the field; the father fastens his seat belt and keeps off any slope that is too steep.",correct:!0,feedback:"A child can fall under the wheels or the plow, and a tractor can overturn on a slope in seconds. Children keep well away from working machines, and the driver wears the seat belt."},{text:"The boy can ride if he sits on the fender and holds on tightly with both hands.",correct:!1,feedback:"Children fall from fenders and are run over by the wheels or the plow. Nobody rides without their own seat and seat belt, and children never ride."},{text:"The boy can ride as long as his father drives very slowly on the slope.",correct:!1,feedback:"An overturn can happen in seconds, even at low speed, and a slope adds to the danger. The boy stays off the tractor."},{text:"The boy can walk behind the plow to watch how it turns the soil.",correct:!1,feedback:"A child walking near a moving plow can be run over or caught by it. Children stay well away from working machines."}]},quiz:[{question:"Where should pesticides be kept?",options:[{text:"Locked away in the original labeled container, far from food",correct:!0},{text:"In a clean drink bottle, with the name written on it in pen",correct:!1},{text:"On a high kitchen shelf, where the family can always find them",correct:!1},{text:"In an open box in the yard, where fresh air carries the fumes away",correct:!1}],explanation:"In a drink bottle or a kitchen a pesticide can be mistaken for a drink or reached by children. Keep it locked away in its original container with its label, away from food and animal feed."},{question:"A farm worker feels dizzy, cannot see clearly and feels sick after spraying. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Move him away into fresh air and call 122, keeping the label",correct:!0},{text:"Give him a glass of milk to wash the poison out of his stomach",correct:!1},{text:"Make him vomit at once, then let him rest in the shade",correct:!1},{text:"Let him finish the spraying, then take a shower at home",correct:!1}],explanation:"These can be signs of pesticide poisoning. Take the person away from the pesticide, call 122 and keep the container or label for the doctors; never make the person vomit and do not give milk or home remedies."},{question:"Why must the driver of a tractor with a roll bar (ROPS) wear the seat belt?",options:[{text:"To stay inside the protected zone if the tractor overturns",correct:!0},{text:"To avoid a fine when the tractor is driven on a main road",correct:!1},{text:"Because the seat belt makes the engine use less fuel on long trips",correct:!1},{text:"It is not needed, because the roll bar alone is enough",correct:!1}],explanation:"The roll bar protects only the space around the seat. Without the seat belt, the driver can be thrown out and crushed by the tractor."},{question:"What must you do before you unblock a machine driven by the PTO shaft?",options:[{text:"Stop the engine and wait until every part has stopped",correct:!0},{text:"Slow the engine down and keep your hands near the guard",correct:!1},{text:"Take off the guard so that you can reach the blockage",correct:!1},{text:"Ask a child to watch the shaft and shout if it speeds up",correct:!1}],explanation:"A turning shaft can catch clothing or hair in a moment. Stop the engine, wait until everything has stopped and keep every guard in place."},{question:"While plowing, you uncover a rusty metal object that looks like an old shell. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stop work, keep everyone away and report it to 115",correct:!0},{text:"Carry it to the edge of the field so that work can go on",correct:!1},{text:"Plow around it, because an old rusty shell cannot explode",correct:!1},{text:"Tap it with a spade to check whether it is empty inside",correct:!1}],explanation:"Old and rusty ordnance can still explode if it is moved or struck. Do not touch it: stop work, keep everyone away, move well away before you phone and report it."},{question:"Steam is rising from a stack of hay baled two weeks ago, and part of it feels very hot. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Keep people away and call 115 without pulling the stack apart",correct:!0},{text:"Pull the hot bales apart quickly so that the heat can escape",correct:!1},{text:"Climb onto the stack and pour water into the hottest part",correct:!1},{text:"Cover the stack with plastic sheets so that air cannot get in",correct:!1}],explanation:"Steam, a strange smell or very hot hay are warning signs that the stack may catch fire by itself. Do not pull it apart or climb onto it: keep everyone away and call 115."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_farm_safety.webp",title:"Safe work on the farm",description:"A farmer wearing a seat belt drives a tractor with a roll bar while his children watch from behind the yard fence; pesticides stand in their labeled containers in a locked store."}],steps:[{title:"Store pesticides safely",detail:"Lock them away in their original labeled containers, far from food, feed, medicines and children; never pour them into drink bottles.",critical:!0},{title:"Read the label and protect yourself",detail:"Follow the label exactly: the amount, the mixing, the protective clothing and how long people must stay out of the treated area. Keep children and animals away."},{title:"Make machines safe before you start",detail:"Use a tractor with a roll bar (ROPS) and fasten the seat belt, keep every guard in place, and make sure everyone, especially children, is well away before you start the engine or the PTO.",critical:!0},{title:"Stop before you touch moving parts",detail:"Stop the engine and wait until every part has stopped before you clean, adjust or unblock a machine. Never go into a grain bin while grain is moving.",critical:!0},{title:"Act fast in an emergency",detail:"Poisoning: protect yourself, move the person into fresh air, call 122 and keep the label. Smoke from a haystack: keep people away and call 115. A strange object in the soil: stop work, keep away and call 115.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"During spraying season, your neighbor's 3-year-old daughter is found in the yard with an open soft-drink bottle that her uncle had filled with a pesticide. Her lips are wet, there is a chemical smell and she is crying. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I take the bottle away, call 122 at once and keep the bottle and the pesticide label for the doctors.",correct:!0,feedback:"She may have swallowed poison. Calling 122 at once and keeping the product details helps the doctors choose the right treatment."},{text:"I make her vomit by putting a finger down her throat to get the poison out.",correct:!1,feedback:"Never make a poisoned person vomit: the poison can harm the throat and lungs on the way up. Call 122 and keep the container."},{text:"I give her a glass of milk to weaken the poison and watch her for an hour.",correct:!1,feedback:"Milk and home remedies do not treat poisoning, and waiting loses time. Give nothing to eat or drink unless a doctor says so, and call 122."}]},{prompt:"While you wait for the ambulance, the girl becomes very sleepy and then does not respond, but she is breathing normally. She has not fallen or hurt her head. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I lay her on her side in the recovery position, keep checking her breathing and am ready to start CPR if it stops.",correct:!0,feedback:"An unconscious child who breathes normally and has no suspected neck or back injury is placed on her side to keep the airway open. If breathing stops, start CPR."},{text:"I sit her up and try to give her water so that she wakes up.",correct:!1,feedback:"Give nothing by mouth to a child who is not awake: she could choke. Put her on her side and watch her breathing."},{text:"I put her in the car on my own and rush her to hospital instead of waiting.",correct:!1,feedback:"An unconscious child needs someone watching her breathing all the time, and the ambulance team is already on the way. Keep her on her side and follow the call-taker's instructions."}]}],realCase:"During the harvest, a hay baler on a family farm became blocked. The young farmer was about to pull the hay out by hand while the tractor was still running. His father stopped him, switched off the engine and waited until the PTO shaft and every moving part had stopped. Only then did they clear the blockage, wearing gloves and close-fitting clothes. Later that week the family noticed a sweet, caramel-like smell from a stack of hay that had been baled damp. They did not pull it apart; they kept the children away and called 115. Two simple rules prevented two serious accidents."},{id:29,icon:"Mountain",accent:"#15803d",title:"Outdoor and mountain recreation",subtitle:"Picnics, barbecues, hiking and camping without fires, falls or getting lost",summary:"Many families enjoy picnics, hiking and camping in the mountains and valleys in spring and summer. Most accidents outdoors can be prevented with a little planning: a fire lit in the wrong place, a fall from rocks, a person who gets lost, a bite or sting, a sudden flood or a storm. This lesson explains how to plan a safe trip and what to do if something goes wrong far from help.",blocks:[{heading:"Before you go",points:["Tell someone who is not coming with you where you are going, which way you will go and when you will be back. Ask them to call for help if you are not back on time.","Check the weather forecast for the place you are going, not only for your town, and change your plans if storms or heavy rain are expected.","Take enough drinking water, food, a charged phone, a flashlight, a first aid kit, warm clothes and a whistle for every person, especially children.",'Stay on well-used paths and roads. In areas where there was fighting, unused paths and overgrown ground may hide mines or explosive ordnance: respect every warning sign (see the lesson "Mines and unexploded ordnance: stay safe").',"Always stay together as a group, and keep children where you can see them."]},{heading:"Picnic fires and barbecues",points:["Light a fire or a barbecue only where it is allowed. Ask about the local rules before you go.","Never light a fire on dry grass or on a dry, windy day. Clear away dry grass and leaves for at least 3 meters (10 feet) around it, and keep it at least 8 meters (25 feet) from trees, tents, cars and anything else that can burn.","Keep the fire small and never leave it alone, even for a few minutes. Keep children at least 1 meter (3 feet) from it, and never use gasoline or other fuels to light it or bring it back to life.","Keep water, or a shovel and soil, nearby. Before you leave, pour water on all the coals, stir and pour again, until there is no heat, glow, flame or smoke.","Never take a barbecue, a charcoal fire or hot coals into a tent, a car or a house, even to keep warm: they can cause a fire or carbon monoxide poisoning, which kills."]},{heading:"If you get lost",body:`Rescue can take hours, so make yourself easy to find:

1. Stop and stay calm. Stay where you are: moving makes it harder for rescuers to find you. Move only if you are in immediate danger, for example from rising water or a fire.
2. Stay together; nobody goes off alone to look for the way.
3. If you have a signal, call 115 and say where you started, where you last knew where you were, what you can see around you, how many you are and whether anyone is hurt or ill. Share your location from your phone if you can.
4. Blow your whistle three times, rest and repeat: a whistle carries further than your voice and saves your energy. Wear or spread out bright clothing so that you can be seen.
5. Find shelter from the sun, rain or cold and wait for help. Do not light a fire if there is any risk of a wildfire.

Teach children: if you get lost, stay where you are, "hug a tree" (or a big rock) and blow your whistle.`},{heading:"Falls and rocks",points:["Stay on the path and watch where you walk, especially near cliffs and on slippery ground.","Do not climb on rocks near waterfalls or streams: spray and algae make them slippery, and people have fallen to their deaths there.","Keep children close and watch them all the time near edges and water.","If someone falls and may have hurt the head, neck or back, do not move them unless the place is unsafe. Call 122, keep them still and warm, and wait for help."]},{heading:"Snakes and scorpions",points:["Wear closed shoes or boots and long pants. Do not put your hands under stones or into piles of wood or holes, and keep away from tall grass and piles of leaves where you can.","Shake out shoes, clothes and bedding before you use them.","Never touch, catch or try to kill a snake.","Snake bite: keep the person calm and still, take off rings, watches and anything tight before swelling starts, and get them to the hospital fast: call 122, and do not let them drive. Do not cut the bite, suck it, tie a tight band, put ice on it or use traditional remedies.","Scorpion sting: keep the person calm. You may put a cold pack wrapped in a cloth on the sting, but do not put the limb in ice water, and give no medicines. Get medical help for every sting, and call 122 at once if the person has trouble breathing, muscle twitching, drooling, slurred speech or strange restlessness."]},{heading:"Floods, lightning and heat",points:["Never camp, picnic or park in a dry riverbed or on low ground next to a stream: a flash flood can come in minutes, even if the rain falls far away. If the water starts to rise, climb to higher ground at once.",'Never walk or drive into flowing water (see the lesson "Floods for families").',"When thunder roars, go indoors: there is no safe place outside. Shelter in a solid building or a car with a metal roof; a tent or a picnic shelter gives no protection. Wait 30 minutes after the last thunder.","If you cannot reach shelter, keep away from hilltops, ridges, open fields, lone tall trees, water and metal fences, and spread out if you are in a group.",'Take plenty of water, rest in the shade and avoid hard walking in the hottest hours. Confusion or collapse in the heat can be heat stroke: call 122 and cool the person at once (see the lesson "Summer safety").']}],tips:["Tell someone where you are going and when you will be back.","Give every child a whistle and teach them to stay where they are if they get lost.","Light fires only where it is allowed, and put them out completely with water before you leave.","Shake out shoes and clothes before you put them on.","Check the forecast for the place you are going, and turn back when a storm comes."],warnings:["Never leave a fire or a barbecue burning, and never light one on dry grass or on a windy day.","Never take a barbecue or hot coals into a tent, a car or a house.","Never camp or park in a dry riverbed or next to a stream when rain is possible."],activity:{prompt:"You are hiking in the mountains with two friends and realize that you have lost the path. The sun will set in two hours and your phone has a weak signal. What do you do?",options:[{text:"We stop, stay together where we are, call 115 with our last known location and what we can see, and blow a whistle from time to time.",correct:!0,feedback:"Staying in one place makes it easier for rescuers to find you, and a call with clear location details and a whistle that carries far help them reach you."},{text:"We split up to look for the path in different directions and meet again later.",correct:!1,feedback:"Splitting up can turn one lost group into several lost people. Stay together and stay where you are."},{text:"We walk downhill as fast as we can, even after dark, until we find a road.",correct:!1,feedback:"Walking fast on unknown ground in the dark can lead to falls, and moving makes you harder to find. Stop, call for help and wait."},{text:"We light a big fire in the dry grass so that people can see the smoke.",correct:!1,feedback:"A fire in dry grass can spread and trap you. Signal with a whistle, a light or bright clothing, and call 115."}]},quiz:[{question:"When can you leave a picnic fire?",options:[{text:"When it has been soaked and stirred and no heat or smoke remains",correct:!0},{text:"When the flames have died down and only glowing red coals are left",correct:!1},{text:"When the burning wood is covered with a layer of dry leaves",correct:!1},{text:"When the wind has turned and blows the smoke away from you",correct:!1}],explanation:"Coals can stay hot for hours, and the wind can carry them into dry grass. Pour water on all the coals, stir, pour again and check that no heat, glow, flame or smoke remains."},{question:"Your family is lost on a mountain. What is the best thing to do?",options:[{text:"Stay together where you are, call 115 and blow a whistle",correct:!0},{text:"Walk quickly in one direction until you reach a road or a village",correct:!1},{text:"Send the older children ahead alone to look for the path",correct:!1},{text:"Keep climbing to the highest point to wave, even after dark",correct:!1}],explanation:"Moving makes it harder for rescuers to find you and can lead to falls. Stay together where you are, call 115 with your last known location and signal with a whistle."},{question:"Someone is bitten on the leg by a snake. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Keep them still, remove tight items and get them to the hospital fast",correct:!0},{text:"Cut the bite and suck out the venom before you set off for the hospital",correct:!1},{text:"Tie a tight band above the bite and put ice on the wound",correct:!1},{text:"Let them walk home quickly and treat the bite with a home remedy",correct:!1}],explanation:"Keeping still slows the spread of the venom, and hospital treatment is what helps. Cutting, sucking, tight bands, ice and traditional remedies only cause more harm."},{question:"Rain is forecast in the mountains. Which is the most dangerous place for your tent?",options:[{text:"In a dry riverbed or on low ground beside a stream",correct:!0},{text:"On a flat, raised area well away from the water",correct:!1},{text:"Next to your car in a gravel parking area on high ground",correct:!1},{text:"In a campsite on a hillside, well above the valley floor",correct:!1}],explanation:"A flash flood can fill a dry riverbed in minutes, even when the rain falls far away. Camp and picnic on higher ground, and climb up at once if water starts to rise."},{question:"Thunder starts while you are camping. Where is the safest place?",options:[{text:"In a solid building or a car with a metal roof",correct:!0},{text:"Inside your tent, with the door zipped shut",correct:!1},{text:"Under the tallest tree standing alone in the field",correct:!1},{text:"On the hilltop, where you can watch the storm",correct:!1}],explanation:"There is no safe place outside in a thunderstorm, and a tent gives no protection. Go to a solid building or a hard-topped car and stay there until 30 minutes after the last thunder."},{question:"Your family wants to use a barbecue in a forest picnic area on a hot, windy afternoon. The grass is dry. What is the best decision?",options:[{text:"Do not light it today; eat food that needs no fire",correct:!0},{text:"Light it but keep it small and stay close beside it",correct:!1},{text:"Light it on the dry grass and keep a bottle of water near",correct:!1},{text:"Light it in the tent so that the wind cannot reach the coals",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never light a fire on dry grass or on a dry, windy day: sparks can start a wildfire. And never take a barbecue or hot coals into a tent."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_outdoor_safety.webp",title:"A safe family picnic in the mountains",description:"A family picnics on high ground, well away from a dry riverbed; the father pours water on the barbecue coals while a child with a whistle around his neck stays close by."}],steps:[{title:"Plan and tell someone",detail:"Check the weather for the place you are going, tell someone who is not coming where you are going and when you will be back, and take water, food, a charged phone, a first aid kit and a whistle for each person."},{title:"Stay on the path and together",detail:"Use well-used paths, respect every warning sign about mines and keep children where you can see them.",critical:!0},{title:"Keep fire under control",detail:"Light a fire only where it is allowed, never on dry grass or on a windy day; never leave it alone, and soak it, stir it and soak it again before you leave. Never take a barbecue or hot coals into a tent or a car.",critical:!0},{title:"Move away from water and storms in time",detail:"Do not camp in a dry riverbed or beside a stream when rain is possible, and climb to higher ground if the water rises. When thunder roars, go into a solid building or a car with a metal roof.",critical:!0},{title:"If you are lost: stop, stay, call and signal",detail:"Stay together where you are, call 115 and describe what you can see, blow your whistle three times and wait for help.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"Your family is camping in a valley in late spring. In the evening, dark clouds gather over the mountains and you hear distant thunder. The tent is pitched on flat sand next to a dry riverbed. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I move everyone and the car now to higher ground away from the riverbed, and we shelter in the car with the windows closed.",correct:!0,feedback:"Thunder means lightning can reach you, and rain upstream can fill the riverbed in minutes. Higher ground and a car with a metal roof protect you from both."},{text:"We stay in the tent and zip it closed until the storm passes.",correct:!1,feedback:"A tent gives no protection from lightning, and the riverbed can flood within minutes from rain far away. Move to higher ground and shelter in a building or a car with a metal roof."},{text:"We shelter under the tallest tree nearby until the thunder stops.",correct:!1,feedback:"A lone tall tree attracts lightning. Shelter in a building or a car with a metal roof, away from the riverbed."}]},{prompt:"The storm passes. Next morning, your 9-year-old son is missing after going to collect firewood, and you hear nothing when you call him. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I keep the rest of the family together, call 115 with the place he was last seen, and blow my whistle and listen for his.",correct:!0,feedback:"Rescue teams need the place he was last seen. Staying together stops more people getting lost, and a whistle carries further than a voice."},{text:"I send each family member off alone in a different direction to look for him.",correct:!1,feedback:"Splitting up can turn one lost person into several. Stay together, call 115 and use the whistle."},{text:"I wait until evening, because he will probably find his own way back.",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting loses daylight and time. Call 115 at once and say where he was last seen."}]}],realCase:"A group of friends went hiking in the mountains in late spring. Before leaving, one of them told her brother their route and that they would be back by sunset. On the way down they took a wrong turn, and as the sun began to set they realized they were lost. They stopped, stayed together and found shelter beside a large rock. With a weak signal they called 115, described the last place they were sure of and what they could see, and then blew their whistles at intervals. Her brother had already raised the alarm when they were late. Rescuers reached them that night."},{id:30,icon:"Construction",accent:"#a16207",title:"After a building collapse: what citizens can do",subtitle:"Helping safely from outside, reporting trapped people and protecting yourself if you are trapped",summary:"A building can collapse after an earthquake or an explosion, in a fire, or because it is old or badly built. The first people to help are usually those nearby, before rescue teams arrive. But untrained people who rush into damaged buildings or onto rubble are often seriously hurt, and some have died. This lesson explains what you can safely do from outside, what to tell 115 and how to protect yourself if you are trapped.",blocks:[{heading:"Your safety comes first",points:["Never go into a damaged building and never climb onto rubble, even to save someone: rubble can shift and collapse, and an aftershock can bring down more.","If you are in a damaged building after the shaking stops, go out and move quickly away from it, to an open place away from buildings, trees, streetlights and power lines.","Signs that a building may fall: it leans or has moved off its base, part of it has already collapsed, there are large cracks in the foundation, or you hear creaking or groaning. Keep everyone away from it.","Expect aftershocks, sometimes for weeks. If you feel one: Drop, Cover, Hold On if you are inside, or stay in the open if you are outside.","If you smell gas, do not use switches, lighters or phones near the building: get away, warn others and call 115 from a safe place. Never touch fallen power lines or anything touching them."]},{heading:"Helping from outside",points:["Help people who can walk to get away from the building to a safe, open place, and keep onlookers back.","Give first aid to injured people you can reach without going into the building or onto the rubble, and call 122. Do not move a person who may have a neck or back injury unless the place is unsafe.","Do not pull a trapped person out by force and do not lift heavy pieces of rubble: other pieces can fall on them and on you. Leave this to the trained rescue team.","Do not dig into the rubble and do not crawl into gaps: they can close at any moment.",'From a safe place, call out and listen: "Can anyone hear me? Tap or call out." Note exactly where any voice or tapping comes from.',"When the rescue team arrives, tell them what you know and follow their instructions. Help only if they ask you to."]},{heading:"Calling 115: what to say",body:`Call 115 (and 122 for the injured) from a safe place and say:

1. Where: the area, the street, the building and a landmark; which side of the building and which floor.
2. What happened: a collapse, and any fire, smell of gas or fallen power lines.
3. People: how many may be trapped, where you heard voices or tapping, and how many are injured.
4. Your name and phone number. Do not hang up until you are told to.

When the rescue team arrives, tell them who lived in the building, who has been seen safe and who is still missing.`},{heading:"If you are trapped",body:`1. Move as little as possible, so that you do not kick up dust or bring down more debris.
2. Cover your nose and mouth with a cloth or your clothing.
3. Do not light a match or a lighter: there may be leaking gas.
4. Tap on a pipe or a wall, or blow a whistle, so that rescuers can find you. If your phone works, call 115 or send a message with your location.
5. Shout only as a last resort: shouting makes you breathe in dangerous amounts of dust.

Keep a whistle and a flashlight next to your bed and in your emergency kit.`},{heading:"In the hours and days after",points:["Use text messages rather than calls when you can, to keep the phone network free for emergencies.","Keep the streets clear for ambulances and rescue vehicles: do not drive into the area to look.","Children do not take part in clearing rubble or cleaning up.","Do not go back into a damaged building until engineers or the authorities say it is safe.","Follow official announcements from Civil Defense and the authorities, and do not spread rumors."]}],tips:["Keep a whistle and a flashlight next to your bed and in your emergency kit.","Agree with your family on a meeting place outside, away from buildings.","Save 115 and 122 in your phone, and know your street name and nearby landmarks.","After a collapse, call out and listen from a safe place, and tell the rescue team exactly where you heard someone.","Use text messages after a disaster to keep the phone lines free."],warnings:["Never go into a damaged building or climb onto rubble, even to rescue someone.","If you smell gas, do not use switches, lighters or phones there: get away and call 115 from a safe place.","Never touch fallen power lines or anything touching them."],activity:{prompt:"After an earthquake, part of a neighbor's house has collapsed. You hear a weak voice under the rubble, and several young men are starting to climb onto it to dig. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I call 115 with the exact place and where the voice is, ask the young men to come down and keep people back, and keep talking to the person from a safe place.",correct:!0,feedback:"Rubble can shift and aftershocks can bring down more. Exact information helps the rescue team, and talking to the trapped person from a safe place keeps them calm without putting more people in danger."},{text:"I climb up with them, because every minute counts and more hands dig faster.",correct:!1,feedback:"More weight on the rubble can make it collapse onto the trapped person and onto the people digging. Stay off the rubble and call 115."},{text:"I pull out the large concrete pieces at the edge to open a way in.",correct:!1,feedback:"Pulling out pieces can bring down the debris that holds up the space where the person is breathing. Leave heavy debris to the trained team."},{text:"I wait until the morning to report it, because the rescue teams are busy now.",correct:!1,feedback:"Trapped people need help as early as possible. Call 115 at once and give the exact location."}]},quiz:[{question:"You are trapped under debris after an earthquake. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Cover your mouth, and tap on a pipe or wall or use a whistle",correct:!0},{text:"Light a match so that you can see the space around you",correct:!1},{text:"Shout as loudly as you can, without stopping, until help comes",correct:!1},{text:"Push hard on the debris above you to make a way out quickly",correct:!1}],explanation:"A flame can ignite leaking gas, constant shouting makes you breathe in dust, and pushing can bring down more debris. Tapping and a whistle help rescuers find you; shout only as a last resort."},{question:"What is the safest way for a citizen to help after a building collapse?",options:[{text:"Help people outside the building and report trapped people to 115",correct:!0},{text:"Go into the damaged building and check every room for survivors",correct:!1},{text:"Climb onto the rubble and lift the heavy pieces one by one",correct:!1},{text:"Drive your car into the street so that you can see what is happening",correct:!1}],explanation:"Untrained people who go into damaged buildings or onto rubble are often hurt or killed. Help from outside, keep the streets clear for rescue vehicles and report trapped people to 115."},{question:"What should you tell 115 first when you report people trapped in a collapse?",options:[{text:"The exact location: the area, the street, the building and a landmark",correct:!0},{text:"Your opinion about who built the building and why it fell down so suddenly",correct:!1},{text:"Only that something has happened somewhere in your neighborhood",correct:!1},{text:"The full names of everyone who has ever lived in the building",correct:!1}],explanation:"Rescue teams must find the place quickly. Give the location first, then what happened, how many may be trapped, where you heard them and any dangers, and do not hang up until you are told to."},{question:"Why should a trapped person shout only as a last resort?",options:[{text:"Shouting makes you breathe in dangerous amounts of dust",correct:!0},{text:"Rescue teams are trained to listen only for tapping sounds",correct:!1},{text:"The noise of shouting can make the rubble above you collapse",correct:!1},{text:"Shouting quickly uses up the battery of your mobile phone",correct:!1}],explanation:"Dust in a collapsed building can harm your lungs, and shouting makes you breathe in more of it. Tapping on a pipe or wall and a whistle carry well and protect your breathing."},{question:"You smell gas near a collapsed building. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Use no switches or flames there, get away and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Go in quickly and close the gas valve in the kitchen",correct:!1},{text:"Switch on the lights in the hallway to see the leak",correct:!1},{text:"Hold a lighter near the pipes to find where the leak is",correct:!1}],explanation:"A spark or a flame can ignite the gas and cause an explosion. Never go into the building to reach a valve: get away, warn others and call 115 from a safe place."},{question:"After an earthquake, your house is cracked and leaning, and your important documents are inside. When may you go back in?",options:[{text:"Only when engineers or the authorities say it is safe",correct:!0},{text:"Once the first aftershock has passed and all is calm",correct:!1},{text:"Quickly, if you stay inside for only a minute or two",correct:!1},{text:"As soon as the walls stop making creaking sounds",correct:!1}],explanation:"A cracked, leaning building may collapse, and aftershocks can come for weeks. Do not go back into a damaged building until engineers or the authorities say it is safe."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_collapse_citizen_help.webp",title:"Helping safely after a collapse",description:"Neighbors stay back from a partly collapsed house; one woman calls 115 and points to where a voice was heard, while others help an injured man to an open area away from buildings and power lines."}],steps:[{title:"Get out and stay away",detail:"Leave the damaged building and go to an open place away from buildings, trees and power lines. Never go back in or climb onto rubble.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 115 and 122 from a safe place",detail:"Give the exact location, what happened, any fire, gas or fallen power lines, how many may be trapped and where you heard voices.",critical:!0},{title:"Help from outside only",detail:"Help people who can walk to reach a safe place, give first aid to those you can reach without going inside and keep onlookers back.",critical:!0},{title:"Listen and note where people are",detail:'From a safe place, call out "Can anyone hear me?", listen for voices or tapping and note the exact place to tell the rescue team.'},{title:"Hand over to the rescue team",detail:"Tell them who lived in the building, who is safe and who is missing; help only if they ask you to and follow their instructions."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"Shortly after an earthquake, you are standing in the street outside your home. Across the road, part of a two-story house has collapsed and there is a smell of gas. A neighbor is shouting that her mother is inside. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I keep people back from the building, use no lighter or phone near it, move to a safe place and call 115 and 122.",correct:!0,feedback:"Gas and a damaged building are both deadly dangers. Calling from a safe place brings trained rescuers without adding more victims."},{text:"I run inside with the neighbor to look for her mother before the rescue team arrives.",correct:!1,feedback:"Untrained people who go into damaged buildings are often seriously hurt or killed, and an aftershock can bring down more. Stay out and call 115."},{text:"I light a lighter to look into a dark gap in the rubble for her mother.",correct:!1,feedback:"A flame can ignite leaking gas. Use no flames or switches near the building; call 115 from a safe place."}]},{prompt:"While you wait for the team, you hear tapping from under the rubble at the back corner of the house. The neighbor wants to start moving heavy pieces of concrete. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I ask her not to move the rubble, call out toward the tapping from a safe place and tell 115 exactly where it is coming from.",correct:!0,feedback:"Moving heavy pieces can bring more debris down on the trapped person. Exact information about where the tapping is helps the rescue team find her quickly."},{text:"I help her lift the concrete slowly, piece by piece, so that it does not fall.",correct:!1,feedback:"Even careful lifting can shift the rubble onto the trapped person and onto you. Leave heavy debris to the trained rescue team."},{text:"I crawl into the gap under the concrete to reach the mother.",correct:!1,feedback:"Gaps in rubble can close at any moment. Never crawl into them: stay outside and guide the rescue team to the tapping."}]}],realCase:"After a strong earthquake, the back of an old apartment building collapsed. Neighbors gathered, and some young men began climbing onto the rubble. A woman who had attended a Civil Defense awareness session asked them to come down and keep people back. She called 115 from across the street, gave the street, a nearby school as a landmark and the side of the building, and said she could hear tapping near the stairwell. When the rescue team arrived, she told them which families had come out and which were missing. The team found the trapped man at the place she had described."},{id:31,icon:"Users",accent:"#0f766e",title:"Crowd safety at gatherings",subtitle:"Weddings, festivals, religious occasions, stadiums and markets",summary:"Weddings, festivals, religious occasions, matches and busy markets bring many people together. When too many people crowd into a small space, or everyone rushes toward one point, people can be crushed or trampled. In crowd disasters, most people die because they are squeezed so hard that they cannot breathe. This lesson explains how to recognize a dangerous crowd early, what to do if you are caught in one, how to protect children and what organizers must do.",blocks:[{heading:"When you arrive",points:["Look for all the exits as soon as you arrive, and find at least two ways out: in an emergency the door you came in by may be blocked.","Check that the exits are not locked, chained or blocked. If there are not at least two ways out, tell the organizers, and leave if it is not fixed at once.","Note where the first aid point is, and the information or lost-children point if there is one.","Agree with your family or friends on a meeting place in case you are separated.","Stay near the edges of the crowd or in less crowded areas, on level, dry ground.","If the place feels overcrowded or unsafe, leave early."]},{heading:"Signs of a dangerous crowd",body:"Leave early, while you can still move freely, if you notice:",points:["There is no space left between you and the people around you, and more people keep pushing in.","The crowd starts to sway or surge in waves, and you are moved where you do not want to go.","People are pushing or rushing, for example toward an entrance, a stage or a free distribution.","People are being pressed against walls, barriers or fences.","It becomes hard to breathe."]},{heading:"If you are caught in a crowd surge",points:["Keep your arms up in front of your chest, like a boxer, to keep space to breathe.","Stay on your feet: plant them firmly and try not to fall. Do not bend down to pick anything up.","Do not push against the crowd. If the people in front of you stop, do not push: hold on and ask those behind you to hold back too.","When the pressure eases for a moment, move diagonally across the crowd toward its edge.","Keep away from walls, barriers and fences, where people can be crushed.","If you fall, curl into a ball, protect your head and get up as soon as you can. If someone near you falls, ask the people around you to hold back and help them up."]},{heading:"Children, older people and people with disabilities",points:["Keep small children close, hold their hands and make sure they have enough space.","Put a card in your child's pocket with your name and phone number.","Show children the meeting place, and teach them to go there if they lose you.","Stay with older people and people with disabilities near the edges of the crowd and close to an exit."]},{heading:"What organizers must do",points:["Know the safe capacity of the place, including the areas where people queue, gather and leave, and never let in more people than it can hold safely.","Keep every exit unlocked and every route to it clear for the whole event; exit doors must open from the inside without a key.","Have enough trained stewards to guide people, watch for pushing and crowd build-up, and stop entry when an area is full.","Plan first aid and medical help for the expected crowd, and agree in advance with Civil Defense and the ambulance service how they will reach the place.","Have a public address system and clear emergency instructions, and report signs of a dangerous build-up at once.","Plan entry and exit so that people do not rush toward one point, such as a single gate or a free distribution.","Follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the local authorities for permits and safety arrangements."]},{heading:"If something goes wrong",points:["If an alarm sounds, or you see smoke, fire or a sudden disturbance, leave calmly at once by the nearest exit.","Move at the pace of the crowd; do not run and do not push.","Once you are out, stay out. Go to your meeting place and tell the rescue teams if someone is missing.","Do not spread rumors; follow the instructions of the stewards and Civil Defense."]}],tips:["Find two ways out as soon as you arrive.","Agree on a meeting place with your family before you go in.","Stay near the edges of the crowd, especially with children.","Leave early if the crowd keeps growing and you can no longer move freely.","Put a card with your phone number in your child's pocket."],warnings:["Never push in a crowd; if the people in front stop, hold back and ask those behind you to stop.","Organizers: never lock or block an exit while people are inside.","Never let in more people than the place can hold safely."],activity:{prompt:"At a crowded festival, the crowd suddenly surges toward the stage. You are pushed forward and can barely move your arms. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I keep my arms up in front of my chest, stay on my feet, do not push back, and when the pressure eases I move diagonally toward the edge.",correct:!0,feedback:"Arms in front of the chest keep space to breathe, staying on your feet keeps you from being trampled, and moving diagonally during a lull takes you out of the strongest pressure."},{text:"I push back hard against the people behind me to make more space.",correct:!1,feedback:"Pushing adds to the pressure on everyone and can make people fall. Do not resist the force of the crowd; wait for a lull."},{text:"I bend down to pick up my phone, which has fallen to the ground.",correct:!1,feedback:"Bending down in a surge can make you fall and be trampled. Stay on your feet and leave the phone."},{text:"I move toward the barrier at the front, where I can hold on to something.",correct:!1,feedback:"People are crushed against barriers and walls. Move away from them, diagonally toward the edge of the crowd."}]},quiz:[{question:"Why should you find two exits when you arrive at a wedding hall or a stadium?",options:[{text:"The door you came in by may be blocked in an emergency",correct:!0},{text:"The organizers may ask you to sit near one of the exits",correct:!1},{text:"It is polite for guests to know where the doors of a hall are",correct:!1},{text:"The second exit is always closer to the parking area outside",correct:!1}],explanation:"In emergencies most people head for the door they came in by, and it can quickly become blocked. A second way out lets you leave by the nearest safe exit."},{question:"What should you do with your arms if you are caught in a crowd surge?",options:[{text:"Keep them up in front of your chest, like a boxer",correct:!0},{text:"Hold them straight down by your sides to take less room",correct:!1},{text:"Raise them high above your head so that people can see you",correct:!1},{text:"Use them to push the people in front of you out of the way",correct:!1}],explanation:"Arms in front of the chest keep a little space for your lungs. In crowd crushes, most people die because they are squeezed so hard that they cannot breathe."},{question:"During a crowd surge, the pressure eases for a moment. Which way should you move?",options:[{text:"Diagonally across the crowd toward its edge",correct:!0},{text:"Straight back, against the flow of the crowd",correct:!1},{text:"Forward, toward the barrier at the front",correct:!1},{text:"Down to the ground, to rest until it is over",correct:!1}],explanation:"Moving diagonally toward the edge takes you out of the strongest pressure without fighting the crowd. Barriers and the ground are where people are crushed or trampled."},{question:"Which of these is an organizer's duty at a large wedding or festival?",options:[{text:"Keep every exit unlocked and clear while guests are inside",correct:!0},{text:"Lock the side doors so that nobody enters without an invitation",correct:!1},{text:"Let in everyone who comes, even after the hall is full",correct:!1},{text:"Hand out free gifts at one narrow gate to please the guests",correct:!1}],explanation:"Exits must open from the inside without a key, and the routes to them must stay clear. Organizers must also keep to the safe capacity and avoid anything that makes people rush to one point."},{question:"What helps most if your child gets separated from you in a crowd?",options:[{text:"A meeting place agreed in advance and a card with your phone number",correct:!0},{text:"A promise that you will search every corner of the festival ground",correct:!1},{text:"A rule that the child always walks alone at the front of the family",correct:!1},{text:"A plan for the child to go home alone by bus if they cannot find you",correct:!1}],explanation:"A meeting place the child knows and a card with your phone number help stewards and other adults bring you together quickly. A child should never leave alone."},{question:"You are near the front of a crowd at a concert, and the people in front of you suddenly stop. The crowd behind you is still pressing forward. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Hold back and ask the people behind you to stop pushing",correct:!0},{text:"Push harder so that the people in front start moving again",correct:!1},{text:"Bend down to make yourself smaller and wait for it to pass",correct:!1},{text:"Move toward the barrier at the front so that you can hold on",correct:!1}],explanation:"If the people in front stop, pushing can crush them. Hold back and ask those behind you to stop too; keep away from barriers, where people get crushed, and do not bend down."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_crowd_safety.webp",title:"Staying safe in a crowd",description:"Families at a festival stand near the edge of the crowd beside a clearly marked, open exit; a steward in a bright vest guides people, and a child has a card with a parent's phone number in a pocket."}],steps:[{title:"Find two ways out when you arrive",detail:"Look for all the exits, check that they are not locked or blocked and note where the first aid point is.",critical:!0},{title:"Agree on a meeting place",detail:"Agree with your family and friends where to meet if you are separated, and put a card with your phone number in your child's pocket."},{title:"Watch the crowd and leave early",detail:"Stay near the edges. If there is no space left around you, the crowd starts to sway or people are pressed against barriers, leave while you can still move.",critical:!0},{title:"In a surge: arms up, stay on your feet",detail:"Keep your arms in front of your chest, do not push and, when the pressure eases, move diagonally toward the edge. Keep away from walls and barriers.",critical:!0},{title:"If someone falls, help them up",detail:"Ask the people around you to hold back and help them up. If you fall, curl into a ball, protect your head and get up as soon as you can.",critical:!0},{title:"In an emergency, leave calmly",detail:"Use the nearest exit at the pace of the crowd, stay out and go to your meeting place."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"You arrive with your family at a wedding hall that is already very full. You see that one of the two emergency exits is locked with a chain, and guests are standing in front of the other. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I tell the organizers at once that the exit must be unlocked and cleared, and leave with my family if it is not fixed.",correct:!0,feedback:"Exit doors must open from the inside without a key. With only one blocked way out, a fire or a panic could trap many people."},{text:"I stay, because nothing usually happens at weddings.",correct:!1,feedback:"Crowd disasters often start without warning. A locked exit is a serious danger: report it and leave if it is not fixed."},{text:"We sit at a table in the middle of the hall, where the children will be safe.",correct:!1,feedback:"The middle of a crowd is the hardest place to get out from. Stay near the edges and a working exit, and report the locked one."}]},{prompt:"The exit has been opened. Later, a rumor of a fire spreads and the crowd rushes toward the main door. Your 7-year-old daughter is beside you. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I hold her close, move calmly with the crowd toward the nearest other exit and do not push.",correct:!0,feedback:"Keeping her close prevents separation, and moving calmly to another exit avoids the crush at the main door."},{text:"I push hard through the crowd to reach the main door before anyone else.",correct:!1,feedback:"Pushing increases the pressure on everyone and can make people fall. Move calmly toward the nearest exit."},{text:"I let go of her hand so that we can both move faster through the crowd.",correct:!1,feedback:"She could be separated from you or knocked down. Keep her close and go together to the nearest exit and then to your meeting place."}]}],realCase:"At a busy festival, a family agreed on a meeting place beside the first aid tent as soon as they arrived, and the mother put a card with her phone number in each child's pocket. In the evening, a free distribution started near the stage and people began rushing toward it. The father noticed that there was no space left between people and that the crowd was starting to sway, so the family moved to the edge and left early. In the crowd, the youngest son lost hold of his sister's hand, but he remembered the meeting place and waited there until his parents arrived."},{id:32,icon:"PlugZap",accent:"#a16207",title:"Home electrical and battery safety",subtitle:"Outlets and extension cords, chargers, e-bikes and e-scooters, lithium-ion batteries and first aid for electric shock",summary:"Every home is full of electrical equipment and rechargeable batteries: outlets and extension cords, phone chargers, power banks, e-bikes and e-scooters. An overloaded outlet, a damaged cable or a failing battery can start a fire, and an electric shock can kill. This lesson explains how to use them safely, how to recognize the warning signs, what to do if a battery overheats or catches fire, and how to help a person who has had an electric shock.",blocks:[{heading:"Outlets, extension cords and cables",points:["Do not overload outlets, extension cords or power strips: too many appliances on one outlet can overheat it and start a fire.","Never plug an extension cord or a power strip into another one (daisy-chaining).","Extension cords are for temporary use only. If you need one all the time, have a qualified electrician check whether more outlets are needed.","Plug heaters, refrigerators, washing machines and other large appliances directly into a wall outlet, never into an extension cord or a power strip.","Do not run cables under carpets or rugs, across doorways or where furniture can crush them.","Replace cracked or damaged cables and extension cords; do not try to repair them yourself.","Do not touch outlets, switches or appliances with wet hands."]},{heading:"Warning signs in outlets and wiring",body:"If you notice any of these signs, stop using the outlet or appliance, switch it off and unplug it if that is safe, and have a qualified electrician find and fix the cause. If there is smoke or fire, get everyone out, close the door and call 115:",points:["Outlets or switches that are warm or discolored, or plugs that get hot.","A smell of burning or hot plastic near an outlet or an appliance.","Sparks, buzzing or crackling sounds, or scorch marks around an outlet or a plug.","Lights that flicker, or fuses and circuit breakers that keep blowing or tripping.","A tingling feeling when you touch an appliance or a metal sink."]},{heading:"Chargers, e-bikes and e-scooters",points:["Phones, laptops, power banks, e-bikes and e-scooters have lithium-ion (Li-ion) batteries. They store a lot of energy, and a damaged battery or the wrong charger can make them overheat, catch fire or explode.","Use the charger that came with the device, or one that the manufacturer recommends. Buy chargers and batteries from reliable sellers: cheap copies may not meet safety standards.","Charge on a hard, flat surface such as a table or the floor, never on a bed, a sofa or under a pillow, where the heat cannot escape.","Do not leave devices charging while you sleep or when nobody is at home, and unplug the charger when the battery is full.","Charge e-bikes and e-scooters outside the home if you can. Never charge or keep them in a hallway, by the front door or on the stairs, where a fire would block the way out.","Fit a smoke alarm where you charge e-bikes and e-scooters, and let the battery cool down after a ride before you charge it.","Do not use a battery that is damaged, or one that has been modified or repaired by someone who is not qualified."]},{heading:"A battery that overheats, smokes or burns",body:"Warning signs: the battery gets very hot, swells or changes shape or color, leaks, smells strange, hisses, crackles or pops, or gives off smoke. Lithium-ion battery fires grow fast, are hard to put out and give off toxic smoke and gases.",points:["If there is no smoke or fire, stop using the battery at once. If it is safe, switch the device off and unplug the charger, then ask the manufacturer or the seller what to do.","If the battery smokes or burns, get everyone out at once, close the door behind you and call 115 from outside. Stay outside.","Do not try to put out or cool a burning battery yourself unless you are trained: leave it to the firefighters.","Do not throw old or damaged batteries in the household trash; ask where they can be collected safely."]},{heading:"After floods and earthquakes, and neighborhood generator cables",points:["Electricity and water together can kill: never touch switches, outlets or appliances while you are wet or standing in water.","After a flood, keep the electricity off until a qualified electrician has checked the house. If you would have to step into water to reach the main switch, call an electrician instead.","Do not use appliances, chargers, e-bikes or e-scooters that have been in water until a qualified person has checked them.","After an earthquake, watch out for fallen power lines and damaged wiring and stay well away from them. Treat a fallen line, and anything touching it, as live, and call 115.","If you smell gas, do not switch any light or appliance on or off: get everyone out, warn the neighbors and call 115 from outside.","Neighborhood generator cables: do not touch them or connect anything to them yourself. Report loose or damaged cables, sparks or a burning smell to the generator operator, and call 115 in an emergency. Only a qualified electrician should connect your home to the neighborhood line."]},{heading:"Electric shock: first aid",body:`1. Do not touch the person while they may still be in contact with electricity: you could get a shock too.
2. Switch off the power first: pull out the plug or switch off the main switch at the fuse box, if you can do it safely.
3. If you cannot switch off the power, or a high-voltage line or a fallen power line is involved, keep well away, keep others away and call 115 and 122.
4. When the power is off, call 122. If the person is not breathing normally, start CPR (compressions only if you are not trained) and follow the dispatcher's instructions; use an AED if one is available.
5. Even if the person seems well, keep watching them and get medical advice from 122: an electric shock can cause deep burns and internal injuries that cannot be seen.`}],tips:["Know where the main switch (the fuse box) of your home is, so that you can switch off the power quickly and safely.","Plug heaters and other large appliances directly into a wall outlet.","Unplug chargers and small appliances when you are not using them.","Fit a smoke alarm where you charge e-bikes or e-scooters, and test the smoke alarms in your home every month.","Buy chargers, batteries and extension cords from reliable sellers, and use only the charger that the manufacturer recommends."],warnings:["Never touch a person who may still be in contact with electricity: switch off the power first.","Never charge an e-bike or e-scooter in the hallway or by the front door, and never leave it charging while everyone is asleep.","If a battery smokes or catches fire, get out, close the door and call 115; do not try to put it out yourself."],activity:{prompt:"It is late at night. Your son's e-scooter is charging in the hallway next to the front door. You hear the battery hissing and notice a strong chemical smell. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I wake everyone and get them out at once, we close the doors behind us and I call 115 from outside; nobody stays behind to deal with the scooter.",correct:!0,feedback:"Hissing and a chemical smell are signs that the battery is failing and may catch fire very quickly. In the hallway a fire would block the way out, so getting everyone out first and calling 115 is right."},{text:"I pour a bucket of water over the battery to cool it down and stay next to it to watch.",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not try to cool or put out a failing battery yourself unless you are trained: it can suddenly burst into flames and give off toxic smoke. Get everyone out and call 115."},{text:"I carry the scooter out through the front door to the street so that it cannot set the house on fire.",correct:!1,feedback:"A hissing battery can catch fire at any moment: carrying it puts you in danger, and a fire in the hallway would block the way out. Get everyone out first and call 115."},{text:"I unplug the charger, open a window and go back to bed, and I check the battery in the morning.",correct:!1,feedback:"These are the warning signs of a battery that may catch fire. Leaving it in the hallway while everyone sleeps puts the whole family at risk: get out and call 115."}]},quiz:[{question:"What is the safe way to use extension cords and power strips at home?",options:[{text:"For a short time only, and never plugged into another cord or strip",correct:!0},{text:"Plug a second power strip into the first when you need more outlets",correct:!1},{text:"Run the cord under the carpet so that nobody trips over the cable",correct:!1},{text:"Use one for the heater and the refrigerator together to save outlets",correct:!1}],explanation:"Extension cords are for temporary use. Plugging one into another can overload the outlet and start a fire, cables under carpets can be crushed and damaged, and heaters and large appliances go directly into a wall outlet."},{question:"Where is the safest place to charge a phone or a power bank?",options:[{text:"On a hard, flat surface such as a table, unplugged once it is full",correct:!0},{text:"On the bed under a pillow, so that it stays out of the way at night",correct:!1},{text:"On the sofa all night, so that the battery is full in the morning",correct:!1},{text:"Next to a heater, where the warm air helps the battery charge faster",correct:!1}],explanation:"Soft surfaces such as beds, pillows and sofas trap heat, and a battery that cannot cool down can overheat and catch fire. Charge on a hard, flat surface, not while you sleep, and unplug the charger when the battery is full."},{question:"Which of these is a warning sign that a lithium-ion battery is failing?",options:[{text:"It swells, gets very hot or makes a hissing sound",correct:!0},{text:"It takes a little longer to charge than when it was new",correct:!1},{text:"Its charging cable is a different color from the device",correct:!1},{text:"The phone screen gets dimmer by itself in the evening",correct:!1}],explanation:"Strong heat, swelling or a change of shape or color, leaks, a strange smell, hissing or popping sounds and smoke are warning signs. Stop using the battery at once; if it smokes or burns, get out and call 115."},{question:"A power bank on the table starts to smoke. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Get everyone out, close the door and call 115 from outside",correct:!0},{text:"Pour a glass of water on it and stay until you see it go out",correct:!1},{text:"Put it in the refrigerator so that it cools down more quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Pick it up and carry it quickly to the kitchen sink",correct:!1}],explanation:"Lithium-ion battery fires grow fast and give off toxic smoke and gases. Do not try to put out, cool or carry a burning battery unless you are trained: get out, stay out and call 115 from outside."},{question:"After an electric shock, a man lies on the floor still holding the cable of an appliance. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Switch off the power at the plug or the main switch, without touching him",correct:!0},{text:"Grab his arm and pull him away from the cable as quickly as you possibly can",correct:!1},{text:"Pour water on his hands to cool the burns before doing anything else",correct:!1},{text:"Start CPR at once, even while he is still holding the cable",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never touch a person who may still be in contact with electricity: you could get a shock too. Switch off the power first, then call 122, and start CPR if he is not breathing normally."},{question:"Your neighbor runs three heaters from one power strip, which is plugged into an extension cord. The plug feels hot. What is the best advice?",options:[{text:"Stop using it, unplug it safely and have an electrician check it",correct:!0},{text:"Wrap the hot plug in tape so that the heat stays inside it",correct:!1},{text:"Keep using it, but switch one heater off when it gets very hot",correct:!1},{text:"Add a second power strip so that the load is shared between the two",correct:!1}],explanation:"A hot plug is a warning sign of overloading. Heaters are plugged directly into a wall outlet, never into a power strip or an extension cord, and strips are never plugged into each other. Have a qualified electrician find and fix the cause."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_home_electrical_battery.webp",title:"Safe charging and outlets at home",description:"A phone charging on a table, an e-scooter charging outside the home away from the door, a wall outlet with only one appliance plugged in and a smoke alarm on the ceiling."}],steps:[{title:"Use outlets and cords safely",detail:"Do not overload outlets, never plug one extension cord or power strip into another and plug large appliances directly into a wall outlet.",critical:!0},{title:"Charge batteries safely",detail:"Use the right charger and charge on a hard surface, not while you sleep or when nobody is home. Never charge e-bikes or e-scooters in a hallway or by the front door.",critical:!0},{title:"Act on warning signs",detail:"If an outlet gets warm, smells of burning or sparks, or a battery swells, hisses or gets very hot, stop using it, switch it off and unplug it if that is safe, and call a qualified electrician or the seller."},{title:"If a battery smokes or burns, get out",detail:"Get everyone out, close the door behind you and call 115 from outside. Do not try to put it out yourself.",critical:!0},{title:"Electric shock: power off before you touch",detail:"Switch off at the plug or the main switch if you can do it safely; then call 122 and start CPR if the person is not breathing normally.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"In the kitchen, you find your 16-year-old son lying on the floor next to the washing machine, his hand still on its metal door. There is water on the floor, and he does not respond. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Without touching him or stepping in the water, I switch off the electricity at the main switch, then call 122.",correct:!0,feedback:"He may still be in contact with electricity, and the water can carry it to you. Switching off the power first protects you; then you can help him."},{text:"I pull his hand off the machine at once so that the current stops.",correct:!1,feedback:"Touching him while the power may still be on can give you a shock too. Switch off the power first."},{text:"I pour a glass of water on his face to wake him up.",correct:!1,feedback:"Water and electricity together can kill, and waking him is not the priority. Switch off the power, then call 122."}]},{prompt:"The power is now off and you have called 122. Your son does not respond and is not breathing normally. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I start CPR at once (compressions only if I am not trained), follow the call-taker's instructions and send someone for an AED if there is one nearby.",correct:!0,feedback:"After an electric shock the heart can stop. CPR and an AED as early as possible give him the best chance."},{text:"I put him in the recovery position and wait for the ambulance.",correct:!1,feedback:"The recovery position is only for a person who is breathing normally. He is not, so start CPR."},{text:"I wait a few minutes to see whether he starts breathing again on his own.",correct:!1,feedback:"Every minute without CPR lowers his chance of survival. Start compressions at once."}]}],realCase:"One night, a family's power bank was charging under a pillow on a teenager's bed. She woke to a hissing sound and a strong chemical smell and saw smoke coming from under the pillow. Instead of trying to carry it out, she shouted to wake everyone, and the family left the apartment, closing the bedroom door and the front door behind them. They called 115 from the street and waited outside. The firefighters dealt with the burning battery, and the closed doors kept the smoke from spreading. Afterwards, the family agreed to charge devices only on a hard surface and never overnight."},{id:33,icon:"HeartHandshake",accent:"#7c3aed",title:"Psychological first aid for families",subtitle:"Look, listen and link: supporting family members and neighbors after a frightening event, children's reactions and when to get professional help",summary:"After a fire, flood, earthquake or accident, many people feel afraid, shocked, sad or angry. These are common reactions to a very hard event, and most people feel better with time and with support from the people around them. Psychological first aid is simple, caring help that anyone can give: make sure people are safe, listen without pressure and connect them with the help they need. This lesson explains how to support adults and children in your family and neighborhood, and when to ask for professional help.",blocks:[{heading:"What psychological first aid is and is not",points:["Psychological first aid is humane, supportive help for people who are suffering after a serious event: comfort, practical help and connection to services.","Anyone can give it: a parent, a neighbor, a teacher or a volunteer. You do not need to be a professional.","It is not counseling or treatment: do not try to make a diagnosis and do not give or suggest medicines.","Do not press people to tell what happened or how they feel. Some want to talk and others do not, and both are normal.","Safety comes first: help only where it is safe, and call 115 or 122 when there is danger or injury."]},{heading:"Look, listen, link",body:`1. Look: check that the place is safe, see who needs urgent medical help and who is very upset or needs extra attention: children on their own, older people and people with disabilities or illness.
2. Listen: come close calmly, introduce yourself, ask what the person needs and what worries them, listen without judging and help them feel calm.
3. Link: help them meet basic needs (water, food, shelter, warmth and information), reach their family and the services they need, and know how to get help again.`},{heading:"How children may react",points:["Children may cling to their parents, fear being left alone or have trouble sleeping and nightmares.","Younger children may go back to earlier behavior such as bedwetting or thumb-sucking, and some play out the event again and again.","Older children and teenagers may become withdrawn, angry or irritable, or take risks.","Keep children with their family as much as possible, keep daily routines (meals, sleep, school and play) and give them simple, honest information for their age.","Limit how much frightening pictures and news they see and hear, and let them ask questions, draw or play."]},{heading:"When to get professional help",points:["Most reactions become weaker with time. Ask a doctor, a health center or a mental health professional for help if they do not get better or they become worse.","Get help when a person cannot look after themselves or their children, cannot sleep or eat for a long time, or cannot go back to daily life.","Get help at once if a person talks about harming themselves or others, or is so confused that they do not know where they are: do not leave them alone, and call 122 if there is immediate danger.","Children also need help when fear, sleep problems or changes in behavior do not get better.","Take care of yourself too: rest, eat, stay in touch with others and ask for support when you need it."]}],steps:[{title:"Make sure the place is safe",detail:"Before helping, check that there is no fire, gas, falling objects or traffic. If there is danger or injury, call 115 or 122 first.",critical:!0},{title:"Look for who needs help most",detail:"See who is injured, who is very upset and who needs extra attention: children alone, older people and people with disabilities.",critical:!0},{title:"Come close and listen",detail:"Introduce yourself, sit or stand at their level, ask what they need and listen without pressure. Silence is fine.",critical:!1},{title:"Help with basic needs",detail:"Offer water, food, a warm place, information about what is happening and a way to contact family.",critical:!1},{title:"Link with help and follow up",detail:"Help the person reach family, Civil Defense, health services or other support, and check on them again later. If they talk about harming themselves, stay with them and call 122.",critical:!0}],tips:["Speak calmly and simply, and use the person's name.","Offer practical help first: water, a blanket or a phone to reach family.","Give only information that you know is true; if you do not know, say so and help find out.","Keep family members together, especially children with their parents."],warnings:["Never force anyone to talk about what happened: it can make the distress worse.","Do not make a diagnosis, give medicines or promise things you cannot do.","Never leave a person who talks about harming themselves alone: stay with them and get help at once."],activity:{prompt:"Last night a fire was put out in your neighbor's apartment. This morning the neighbor sits on the stairs, shaking and silent, and her six-year-old son is holding her hand. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I sit with them calmly, offer water and a blanket, ask what they need and help them call their relatives.",correct:!0,feedback:"This is psychological first aid: calm presence, practical help and linking them with their family, without pressing them to talk."},{text:"I ask her to tell me in detail what happened in the fire, so that she gets it off her chest.",correct:!1,feedback:"Pressing someone to tell the details can make the distress worse. Let her decide whether she wants to talk."},{text:"I tell her that she is in shock and give her one of my own calming tablets.",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not make a diagnosis and never give medicines: that is for a doctor. Offer calm support and practical help."},{text:"I take the boy to my home so that he does not see his mother upset, and leave her to rest.",correct:!1,feedback:"Children should stay with their parents. Separating them adds fear for both, so support them together."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"After a flood, a family is staying with relatives. The father tells you that he cannot stop thinking about the water and has hardly slept. The children keep asking whether the water will come back. What is the best first response?",options:[{text:"I listen calmly, tell him that such reactions are common after this kind of event and help him plan simple things for the family today.",correct:!0,feedback:"Listening, explaining that common reactions are normal and helping with practical needs are the core of psychological first aid."},{text:"I tell him to stop thinking about it and to be strong for the children.",correct:!1,feedback:"Telling someone how to feel does not help and can make them feel judged. Listen and offer practical support."},{text:"I tell him that he has a mental illness and must go to hospital today.",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not make diagnoses. Many reactions are normal at first; watch and link him with help if they do not get better."},{text:"I show the children videos of the flood so that they understand what happened.",correct:!1,feedback:"Frightening pictures can increase children's fear. Give simple, honest information for their age instead."}]},{prompt:"Weeks later the father is worse: he does not eat, does not leave the room and says that the family would be better off without him. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I stay with him, tell the family and get help at once from a doctor or health service; if there is immediate danger I call 122.",correct:!0,feedback:"Talk of the family being better off without him is a warning sign that needs professional help at once. Never leave the person alone."},{text:"I keep it a secret because he told me in confidence.",correct:!1,feedback:"Safety comes before secrecy. Someone who may harm himself needs help at once, and the family should know."},{text:"I give him more time, because these feelings always pass on their own.",correct:!1,feedback:"Reactions that become worse, and any talk of harming oneself, need professional help now, not more waiting."},{text:"I buy him some medicine from the pharmacy to help him sleep.",correct:!1,feedback:"Never give or buy medicines for someone. A doctor decides on treatment; your role is to stay with him and get help."}]}],realCase:"After an earthquake, a neighborhood spent the night outdoors. A mother with three children sat apart and did not speak. A neighbor who had attended an awareness session brought water and blankets, sat with her and asked what she needed. She wanted to know where her husband was. The neighbor helped her call relatives until they found him in a shelter. Over the following days the youngest child had nightmares. The parents kept him close, kept meals and bedtime regular and answered his questions simply. When his fear did not ease, they asked a health center for advice.",quiz:[{question:"Which three actions are the basis of psychological first aid?",options:[{text:"Look, listen and link",correct:!0},{text:"Diagnose, treat and follow up",correct:!1},{text:"Question, record and report",correct:!1},{text:"Calm, sedate and send home",correct:!1}],explanation:"Psychological first aid is built on look (safety and needs), listen (calm, caring contact) and link (basic needs, information, family and services). It is not a diagnosis or a treatment."},{question:"A neighbor who survived a fire does not want to talk about it. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Respect that, stay close and offer water or a phone",correct:!0},{text:"Keep asking until she tells the whole story, so that she feels better",correct:!1},{text:"Tell her that keeping silent will make her ill later",correct:!1},{text:"Leave at once, because she clearly needs no support at all",correct:!1}],explanation:"Nobody should be pressed to talk. Calm presence and practical help support the person; pressing for details can increase distress."},{question:"After an earthquake your five-year-old wets the bed again and will not sleep alone. What helps most?",options:[{text:"Keep him close, keep daily routines and reassure him calmly",correct:!0},{text:"Punish him so that he stops the old behavior quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Show him news pictures so that he understands the danger",correct:!1},{text:"Send him to stay with distant relatives for a few weeks",correct:!1}],explanation:"Going back to earlier behavior is a common reaction in young children. Closeness, routines and calm reassurance help; punishment, frightening pictures and separation make fear worse."},{question:"Which situation needs professional help at once?",options:[{text:"A person says that he wants to end his life",correct:!0},{text:"A person feels sad and tired the day after a fire",correct:!1},{text:"A child asks many questions about the flood",correct:!1},{text:"A neighbor cries when she sees her damaged house",correct:!1}],explanation:"Talk of ending one's life is a warning sign: stay with the person, do not leave them alone and get help at once (122 if there is immediate danger). The other reactions are common and usually ease with support."},{question:"Which of these is wrong in psychological first aid?",options:[{text:"Giving a very upset person one of your own calming tablets",correct:!0},{text:"Introducing yourself and asking what the person needs",correct:!1},{text:"Helping a family reach their relatives by phone",correct:!1},{text:"Telling the truth and saying when you do not know",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never give medicines: treatment is decided by a doctor. The other actions are part of good psychological first aid."},{question:"A distressed person is standing next to a burning car and will not move. What comes first?",options:[{text:"Safety: help him move away and call 115 and 122",correct:!0},{text:"Sit with him beside the car and listen to his story",correct:!1},{text:"Ask him about his feelings before doing anything",correct:!1},{text:"Leave him, because psychological help can only start later",correct:!1}],explanation:"Look starts with safety. Move the person away from danger and call for help; listening comes once everyone is safe."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w10_psychological_first_aid.webp",title:"Supporting a family after an emergency",description:"A neighbor sits calmly with a mother and her child outside their home, offering water and a blanket while the child holds the mother's hand."}]},{id:34,icon:"Sparkles",accent:"#b45309",title:"Celebrations safely: fireworks and celebratory gunfire",subtitle:"Firework injuries and burns, why shooting into the air kills and how to keep weddings, holidays and victories safe",summary:"Weddings, holidays, sports wins and graduations bring families into the streets, and fireworks and shooting into the air often come with them. Fireworks cause burns, eye injuries and lost fingers, and they start fires in homes, fields and parked cars. A bullet fired into the air does not disappear: it falls back and can kill or injure someone far away. This lesson explains the dangers, safe ways to celebrate, first aid for burns and injuries, and what to do when someone is hurt.",blocks:[{heading:"Fireworks: what can go wrong",points:["Fireworks can burn skin and clothing, damage the eyes and blow off fingers or hands.","Even small fireworks and sparklers burn very hot and injure many children.","A firework that did not go off (a dud) can explode when someone goes back to it.","Fireworks can set fire to dry grass, rooftops, balconies, curtains and parked cars.","Homemade fireworks and fireworks from unknown sellers are especially dangerous."]},{heading:"Safer ways to celebrate",points:["The safest choice is to watch a public display run by professionals from a safe distance and to follow the organizers' instructions.","Children never light, hold or play with fireworks, including sparklers, and an adult watches them all the time.","If an adult uses fireworks where this is allowed: one at a time, on a clear, open area away from people, houses, cars and dry grass, never in the hand and never pointed at anyone.","Never lean over a firework when lighting it, and move away at once.","Never relight or pick up a firework that did not go off: wait, then soak it in water.","Keep a bucket of water or a hose nearby, and soak used fireworks before putting them in the bin."]},{heading:"Celebratory gunfire: a falling bullet kills",points:["A bullet fired into the air comes back down. It can kill or seriously injure a person on a roof, in a courtyard or in the street, even far away.","The shooter usually cannot see where the bullet falls and often never knows whom it hit.","Victims are often people who were not part of the celebration, including children at home or on a roof.","Shooting near crowds, power lines or fuel can also start fires and cause panic.","Celebrate without weapons: music, applause, lights and gatherings are safe. Follow the law and the instructions of the competent authorities."]},{heading:"First aid for firework and gunshot injuries",body:`1. Burns: cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes, remove rings and tight items near the burn, do not burst blisters and do not put ice, butter or toothpaste on it; cover it loosely with a clean cloth. Call 122 for large, deep or facial burns and for burns in children.
2. Eye injury: do not rub or press the eye and do not try to remove anything stuck in it; get medical help at once (122).
3. Severe bleeding (a hand injury or a gunshot wound): call 122, press firmly and directly on the wound with a clean cloth and keep pressing; for life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg use a tourniquet if you are trained.
4. Clothing on fire: stop, drop and roll, then cool the burn.
5. A fire in a house, field or car: get people away and call 115.`}],steps:[{title:"Stop the celebration around the injured person",detail:"Stop fireworks and any shooting, and move people away so that nobody else is hurt.",critical:!0},{title:"Call for help",detail:"Call 122 for injuries and 115 for a fire. Give the exact location first, then what happened and how many people are hurt.",critical:!0},{title:"Treat the injury",detail:"Cool burns with cool running water for 20 minutes, press firmly on heavy bleeding and do not rub or press an injured eye.",critical:!0},{title:"Deal with a fire safely",detail:"If a fire has started, get everyone away from it and wait for 115 at a safe place; do not go back for belongings.",critical:!0},{title:"Prevent the next injury",detail:"Afterwards, soak duds and used fireworks in water, and agree in the family that the next celebration will be without shooting.",critical:!1}],tips:["Agree in the family before the celebration: no shooting and no fireworks in the hands of children.","Tell guests clearly, before the event, that the celebration has no shooting.","When there is shooting nearby, stay indoors under a solid roof and keep children away from roofs and open courtyards.","Keep a first aid kit and a bucket of water ready at the place of the celebration."],warnings:["Never fire a weapon into the air: the bullet falls back and can kill someone you cannot see.","Never let children light or hold fireworks, including sparklers.","Never go back to or relight a firework that did not go off."],activity:{prompt:"At a wedding in your neighborhood, a relative takes out a rifle to fire into the air as the bride arrives. Children are playing on the nearby roofs. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I calmly ask him not to shoot, remind him that bullets fall back on people and ask the family elders to support me.",correct:!0,feedback:"Stopping the shooting before it starts protects everyone, including the children on the roofs. Asking respected family members helps."},{text:"I let him shoot, but tell him to aim high so that the bullets go far away.",correct:!1,feedback:"Aiming higher does not help: the bullets still fall back and can kill someone far away who cannot be seen."},{text:"I tell the children to stay on the roofs, where they can see the celebration well.",correct:!1,feedback:"Roofs and open courtyards are exactly where falling bullets hit people. Children should be indoors under a solid roof."},{text:"I try to take the rifle out of his hands by force.",correct:!1,feedback:"Grabbing a loaded weapon can make it go off. Speak calmly, ask elders to help and, if there is danger, call 104."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"On a holiday evening, teenagers set off fireworks in a narrow street. One rocket falls into dry grass next to a house and starts to burn, and a boy is holding his burned hand. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I stop the fireworks, call 115 for the fire and 122 for the boy and start cooling his burn with running water.",correct:!0,feedback:"Stop the danger, call for help for both the fire and the injury, and start burn first aid at once."},{text:"I first try to put out the grass fire with my feet and leave the boy for later.",correct:!1,feedback:"The boy needs burn care now and the fire needs 115. Stamping on a growing fire can burn you too."},{text:"I put ice and toothpaste on the boy's hand to stop the pain.",correct:!1,feedback:"Ice and toothpaste damage the skin. Cool running water for 20 minutes is the right first aid."},{text:"I tell the teenagers to keep going but to move a little away from the house.",correct:!1,feedback:"Fireworks must stop at once: another rocket could start a new fire or hurt someone else."}]},{prompt:"The fire is out. The boy's burn has been cooled, but there is a large blister and one of his fingers looks badly hurt. One firework did not go off and is lying in the street. What do you do next?",options:[{text:"I cover the burn loosely, get him medical care through 122 and keep everyone away from the dud until it can be soaked in water.",correct:!0,feedback:"A burn with a large blister and a hand injury in a child need medical care, and a dud can still explode."},{text:"I burst the blister so that the skin heals faster.",correct:!1,feedback:"Never burst blisters: they protect the wound from infection."},{text:"I pick up the dud and try to light it again to use it up.",correct:!1,feedback:"Never go back to or relight a dud: it can explode in your hand or face."},{text:"I let the boy go home, because the burn has already been cooled.",correct:!1,feedback:"Cooling is only first aid. A child's burn with a blister and a hurt finger needs a medical assessment."}]}],realCase:"During a wedding in a residential area, several guests fired rifles into the air when the couple arrived. A few streets away, a woman was hanging laundry on her roof. She suddenly fell, and at first her neighbors did not understand why. They called 122 and pressed a clean cloth firmly on a bleeding wound on her shoulder until the ambulance arrived. At the hospital, the doctors found a bullet. The shooters never knew where their bullets had landed. Since then, the families in that neighborhood have agreed that their celebrations have music, lights and dancing, but no shooting.",quiz:[{question:"Why is shooting into the air at a celebration dangerous?",options:[{text:"The bullet falls back and can kill someone far away",correct:!0},{text:"Only the loud noise is dangerous, so cover your ears",correct:!1},{text:"It is dangerous only if the shooter aims at people",correct:!1},{text:"Bullets fired upward burn up and never come back down",correct:!1}],explanation:"What goes up comes down: a bullet fired into the air falls back with enough force to kill or injure, often far from the shooter, who cannot see where it lands."},{question:"A firework did not go off. What is the safe action?",options:[{text:"Do not go near it, wait, then soak it in water",correct:!0},{text:"Light it again from close up to make it work",correct:!1},{text:"Pick it up and look inside to find the fault",correct:!1},{text:"Put it in your pocket and use it next time",correct:!1}],explanation:"A dud can still explode. Never relight it or pick it up: wait at a distance, then soak it in water before throwing it away."},{question:"A sparkler has burned a child's hand. What first aid comes first?",options:[{text:"Cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"Put ice directly on it for a few minutes",correct:!1},{text:"Cover it with butter or toothpaste at once",correct:!1},{text:"Burst the blisters so that the heat can get out",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cool running water for 20 minutes is the first aid for burns. Ice, butter and toothpaste harm the skin, and blisters must not be burst. Call 122 for burns in children."},{question:"Who should light fireworks at a family celebration?",options:[{text:"Never a child; the safest choice is a professional display",correct:!0},{text:"The oldest child, if an adult watches from far away",correct:!1},{text:"Any guest who has done it before, close to the crowd",correct:!1},{text:"Young children, but only with sparklers, as they are safe",correct:!1}],explanation:"Children never light or hold fireworks, sparklers included, because sparklers burn very hot. Watching a professional display is the safest choice; any use by adults follows the law and the safety rules."},{question:"Shooting starts at a wedding next door. Where are your children safest?",options:[{text:"Inside the house under a solid roof",correct:!0},{text:"On the roof, where they can watch from above",correct:!1},{text:"In the open courtyard, away from the walls",correct:!1},{text:"In the street, among the wedding guests",correct:!1}],explanation:"Falling bullets hit people on roofs, in courtyards and in the street. A solid roof gives protection, so keep children indoors until the shooting stops."},{question:"Which of these is wrong when an adult uses fireworks where they are allowed?",options:[{text:"Holding a firework in the hand while it goes off",correct:!0},{text:"Lighting one at a time on a clear, open area",correct:!1},{text:"Keeping a bucket of water ready nearby",correct:!1},{text:"Moving away at once after lighting it",correct:!1}],explanation:"A firework must never be held in the hand: it can burn the hand or blow off fingers. The other actions are part of safe use."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w10_celebration_safety.webp",title:"A safe celebration without shooting",description:"A family watches a professional fireworks display from a safe distance, the children close to their parents, with a bucket of water nearby and nobody holding a weapon."}]},{id:35,icon:"Dog",accent:"#0f766e",title:"Pets and animals in emergencies",subtitle:"Planning for pets and livestock, never going back for an animal, frightened animals, dog bites and rabies",summary:"Many families keep cats, dogs, birds, sheep or other animals, and in a fire, flood or earthquake people often risk their lives to save them. Animals matter to the family, but no animal is worth a human life. Planning ahead gives your animals the best chance and keeps you safe. This lesson explains how to include animals in your family emergency plan, what to do during an evacuation, how to stay safe around frightened or stray animals and what to do after a dog or other animal bite.",blocks:[{heading:"Never go back for an animal",points:["In a fire, get out and stay out: never go back into a burning or smoke-filled building for a pet. Tell the 115 team which animal is inside and where.","In a flood, do not walk or drive into the water to save an animal or livestock.","After an earthquake, do not go back into a damaged building to look for an animal.","If you can take your pet quickly and safely as you leave, take it; if not, leave without it.","Look for a lost animal only after the danger is over and the authorities say that it is safe."]},{heading:"Plan ahead for your animals",points:["Include your animals in the family emergency plan: who takes which animal and where you will go.","Keep a carrier, a leash or a halter near the exit so that you can take small animals quickly.","Add food, water, any medicines that the veterinarian prescribed for the animal and a recent photo of it to your emergency kit.","Put a collar with your phone number on dogs and cats, so that they can be returned to you.","Ask in advance where animals can stay if you must leave home: relatives, friends or places that the authorities name.","For livestock, plan early where they can be moved, and move them before danger arrives, not during it."]},{heading:"Frightened and stray animals",points:["A frightened or injured animal, even your own, may bite or kick. Approach slowly and speak calmly.","Keep children away from stray dogs and from animals that they do not know, and teach them not to touch or feed them.","Do not try to catch a stray, injured or aggressive animal yourself: keep away and tell the competent authority.","Keep away from animals that behave strangely, for example a usually shy animal that suddenly becomes aggressive.","After a disaster, keep your animals on a leash or indoors: familiar smells and landmarks may be gone, and they can get lost or hurt."]},{heading:"After a bite or a scratch",body:`1. Wash the wound at once and thoroughly with soap and plenty of running water.
2. If it bleeds heavily, press on it with a clean cloth and call 122.
3. Go to a doctor or a health center at once, even if the wound is small: only medical staff can decide whether the person needs rabies vaccine or other treatment.
4. Do not wait for signs of illness: once the signs of rabies start, it is almost always fatal.
5. Remember what the animal looked like and where it is, without trying to catch it, and tell the health center and the competent authority.`}],steps:[{title:"Get people out first",detail:"Warn everyone and get them out. No animal is a reason to delay leaving.",critical:!0},{title:"Take the animal only if it is quick and safe",detail:"Take a pet in its carrier or on a leash if it is close and you can do it at once; otherwise leave without it.",critical:!0},{title:"Tell 115 about animals left inside",detail:"Tell the responders which animal is inside and where; never go back in yourself.",critical:!0},{title:"Keep animals under control",detail:"Outside, keep pets in the carrier or on a leash and away from other people and animals.",critical:!1},{title:"Look for lost animals later",detail:"Look for them only when the authorities say that it is safe, and ask your neighbors.",critical:!1}],tips:["Practice putting your pet into its carrier, so that it does not hide during an emergency.","Keep a photo of yourself with your animal on your phone to show that it is yours.","Keep your pets' vaccinations up to date, as the veterinarian advises.","Tell your neighbors about your animals, so that they can inform responders if you are not at home."],warnings:["Never go back into a burning, flooded or damaged building for an animal.","Never treat an animal bite only at home: get medical care at once, even for a small wound.","Do not let children approach stray, injured or frightened animals."],activity:{prompt:"Your apartment fills with smoke at night. You and your children are at the front door, but your cat is hiding under the bed in the far room. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I leave with the children, close the door, call 115 from outside and tell them that the cat is in the far room.",correct:!0,feedback:"People first: getting out, closing the door and telling 115 where the cat is gives everyone, including the cat, the best chance."},{text:"I send the children out and go back to pull the cat out from under the bed.",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke can make you unconscious very quickly, and the children would be outside alone. Never go back in for an animal."},{text:"I wait at the door and call the cat until it comes out to me.",correct:!1,feedback:"Every moment in the smoke is dangerous for you and your children. Leave now and tell 115."},{text:"I open the windows so that the smoke goes out, and then I go and look for the cat.",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening windows can feed the fire, and searching in smoke risks your life. Get out and stay out."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Heavy rain is falling and the authorities announce that families near the river must leave. You have a dog in the house and five sheep in the yard. What do you do?",options:[{text:"We leave at once, taking the dog on a leash; the sheep stay behind if moving them would delay us.",correct:!0,feedback:"Leaving when told is what keeps the family alive. A dog on a leash is quick to take; moving livestock should have been planned before the warning."},{text:"We stay to move all the sheep to a relative's farm before we leave.",correct:!1,feedback:"Moving livestock during the warning uses up time that you need to get out safely; it must be planned before danger."},{text:"We leave the dog tied up in the yard so that it does not run away.",correct:!1,feedback:"A tied animal cannot escape rising water. Take the dog with you on a leash if you can."},{text:"We wait until the water reaches the yard and then decide.",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting makes leaving more dangerous and may trap the whole family. Leave as soon as you are told."}]},{prompt:"Two days later the water has gone down. Near the house, your son finds a dog that he does not know; it is wet and growling, and it bites his hand. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I wash the wound well with soap and running water, take him to a doctor at once and tell the authority about the dog.",correct:!0,feedback:"Washing at once and getting medical care the same day are what prevent rabies and infection; reporting the dog protects others."},{text:"I wash the wound and wait to see whether the dog becomes ill before going to a doctor.",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not wait: medical staff decide at once whether rabies vaccine is needed, and once signs of rabies start it is almost always fatal."},{text:"I put a traditional remedy on the bite and cover it tightly.",correct:!1,feedback:"Home remedies do not prevent rabies or infection. Wash the wound and get medical care at once."},{text:"I try to catch the dog so that the doctor can examine it.",correct:!1,feedback:"Catching a growling, frightened dog risks more bites. Remember what it looked like and tell the competent authority."}]}],realCase:"One evening a fire started in the kitchen of a ground-floor home. The father got his wife and two children out, and they met at the street corner. Their daughter cried that the cat was still inside and tried to run back. Her father held her and told the Civil Defense team, who arrived soon after, that the cat was hiding in a bedroom. The team found the cat once the fire was under control. Later the family added a carrier by the door to their plan, practiced who carries what and made a rule: people first, and nobody goes back in.",quiz:[{question:"Your house is on fire and your dog is still inside. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stay outside and tell 115 where the dog is",correct:!0},{text:"Go back in quickly with a wet cloth over your face",correct:!1},{text:"Send your teenage son in, because he runs the fastest",correct:!1},{text:"Stand in the doorway and keep calling the dog by name",correct:!1}],explanation:"Get out and stay out. Nobody goes back into a burning building for an animal; responders can search safely if they know where it is."},{question:"A stray dog bites your hand and the wound is small. What is right?",options:[{text:"Wash it well with soap and water and see a doctor at once",correct:!0},{text:"Wash it and see a doctor only if you feel ill later",correct:!1},{text:"Cover it with a tight bandage and forget about it",correct:!1},{text:"Put salt or a home remedy on it to stop infection",correct:!1}],explanation:"Even a small bite can pass on rabies. Wash it at once with soap and running water and get medical care the same day: only medical staff can decide whether vaccine is needed."},{question:"Which items belong in the emergency kit for a family cat?",options:[{text:"Food, water, a carrier and a recent photo of the cat",correct:!0},{text:"Only a toy, because the cat will find its own food",correct:!1},{text:"Sleeping pills from the pharmacy to keep it calm",correct:!1},{text:"Nothing, since animals look after themselves in disasters",correct:!1}],explanation:"A pet needs food, water and a carrier to travel safely, and a recent photo helps you find it if it gets lost. Give an animal only medicines that a veterinarian has prescribed for it."},{question:"Why should children stay away from frightened or stray animals?",options:[{text:"Frightened animals may bite, even if they look friendly",correct:!0},{text:"Stray animals are always sick and must be chased away",correct:!1},{text:"Only large dogs bite, so small ones are always safe",correct:!1},{text:"Touching animals is safe if the child moves very fast",correct:!1}],explanation:"Fear or pain can make any animal bite, whatever its size. Children should not touch or feed animals that they do not know, and they tell an adult."},{question:"Flood water is rising and your sheep are in the field. What is the safe decision?",options:[{text:"Leave without them if moving them would delay you",correct:!0},{text:"Walk into the water to bring them to higher ground",correct:!1},{text:"Drive the car through the water to reach the field",correct:!1},{text:"Stay in the field with them until the water stops rising",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never walk or drive into flood water. Livestock should be moved early, before the danger, as part of the plan; once the water is rising, people come first."},{question:"Which of these is wrong after an animal bite?",options:[{text:"Waiting for the animal to show signs of rabies",correct:!0},{text:"Washing the wound at once with soap and water",correct:!1},{text:"Pressing on heavy bleeding with a clean cloth",correct:!1},{text:"Telling the health center what the animal looked like",correct:!1}],explanation:"Waiting is dangerous: medical care must start at once, because once signs of rabies appear it is almost always fatal. The other actions are correct."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w10_pets_emergencies.webp",title:"A family leaves with its pets",description:"A family walks calmly out of its home: a parent carries a cat in a carrier, a child walks a dog on a leash and the father carries an emergency bag."}]}],t={lessons:e};export{t as default,e as lessons};
